1	7	llamaparse	# The Low-Frequency Trap: Video–Language Models Fail at Simple Event Bookkeeping\n\n**Sarvesh Baskar¹∗, Zikui Cai¹∗, Shayan Shabihi¹∗, Anirudh Satheesh¹, Muhammad R. Islam¹, Udari Madhushani Sehwag², Tom Goldstein¹, Furong Huang¹**\n1 University of Maryland, College Park, USA  2 Scale AI, USA\n\n**Code:** The-Low-Frequency-Trap  **Dataset:** Sarvesh-369/Low-Frequency-Trap\n🌐 **Project Page:** low-frequency-trap.github.io\n\n## Abstract\nReal-world video benchmarks provide broad coverage, but their fixed clips entangle event count, rate, duration, and semantics, making failure modes hard to isolate. While existing programmatic benchmarks offer better control, they primarily score only the final answer rather than auditing reported events against executable ground truth. To bridge this gap, we introduce trace-grounded parametric profiling for event counting in three controlled video tasks: bouncing-ball wall contacts, visual blinks, and categorical state transitions. Across 2,190 videos, we vary event count \\( N \\) and frequency \\( F \\) while holding rendering fixed. Each video includes an executable event trace for capability-surface estimation and timestamp-level evaluation. Our results reveal a staged temporal failure. At an 80% reliability threshold, Gemini 3.6 Flash reliably counts persistent state transitions up to 12 events at 0.5 and 1.0 Hz, yet demonstrates no reliable positive-count region for transient blinking events. Thus, event representation (persistent or transient) dictates whether a model initially accesses the evidence—a limitation that compounds as count and frequency increase. In the high-count, high-frequency regime, only 0.2% of final counts are correct, and the model recovers just 18.1% of true events. To test if visual access is the primary bottleneck, we increase sampling rate. Although this boosts Bounce Ball accuracy from 19.6% to 29.3%, the reported sequence agrees with the ground truth only 3.7% of the time. Extra frames can therefore inflate final scores without producing faithful event recovery. Different prompting strategies yield similarly limited gains, and evaluations on real-world videos show the same concentration of success at low event counts. Ultimately, trace-grounded profiling shifts video evaluation from aggregate accuracy metrics to a detailed diagnostic of where temporal reasoning fails and whether the reported evidence actually supports the final answer.\n\n## 1 Introduction\nVideo-language models have advanced rapidly, demonstrating strong performance across standard video benchmarks (Fu et al. 2025; Liu et al. 2024; Chandrasegaran et al. 2024; Mangalam, Akshulakov, and Malik 2023; Li et al. 2024b). These benchmarks provide useful breadth and ranking leaderboards, but they rarely identify why a model fails. Natural clips jointly vary visual clutter, camera motion, event density, duration, and semantics, so an error cannot be attributed to a specific temporal demand. The programmatically synthesized benchmarks considered here offer more control (Lu et al. 2025; Li et al. 2024a), but primarily score only a final answer. They therefore do not reveal whether a model missed events, confused their timing, lost track of a sequence, or made an error only when aggregating it.\n\nWe address this gap with trace-grounded parametric profiling for visual event counting (Figure 1). We construct simple controlled video tasks based on bouncing-ball wall contacts, visual blinks, and categorical state transitions. We vary event count \\( N \\) because it determines the number of observations a model must retain and aggregate, and event frequency \\( F \\) because it determines how closely successive events are separated in time. These two parameters capture complementary temporal demands while remaining independently controllable. We hold rendering, semantics, and task rules fixed so that the resulting boundary can be attributed to temporal demand rather than changes in visual content. Other factors that shape real-world video understanding, including clutter, camera motion, occlusion, semantic complexity, and temporal parameters such as event duration, irregularity, and inter-event variability, are outside the scope of this controlled study. The natural-video transfer evaluation does not isolate these factors; it tests only whether the failure patterns identified under controlled variations of \\( N \\) and \\( F \\) persist in more realistic videos. Every video is paired with an executable ground-truth trace containing event times, state changes, and cumulative counts. This design maps reliable operating regions over the \\( N \\times F \\) space and tests model-reported events against the sequence that actually occurred.\n\nThe results are consistent with a staged and representation-dependent temporal failure. Under an 80% reliability target, Gemini 3.6 Flash counts persistent state transitions through \\( N = 12 \\) at 0.5 and 1.0 Hz, whereas blinking has no reliable positive-count region. In the high-count, high-frequency region, only 0.2% of final counts are correct, and Gemini recovers just 18.1% of the true events. Increasing frame density raises Bounce Ball accuracy from 19.6% to 29.3%, yet the reported sequence agrees with the ground truth only 3.7% of the time. Thus, additional visual evidence can improve the final answer without producing faithful event recovery. Prompting yields similarly limited gains, and natural repeated evaluations show the same concentration of success at low event counts.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper introduces a controlled evaluation framework for video reasoning that replaces traditional aggregate accuracy scores with capability surfaces over event count and frequency. We present a trace-grounded benchmark that audits model-reported events against executable ground truth, conduct targeted interventions to diagnose errors, and demonstrate the limitations of relying solely on final-answer accuracy in characterizing temporal reasoning.\n\n# Introduction\nRecent advancements in video-language models have highlighted the need for robust evaluation frameworks that can accurately assess temporal reasoning capabilities. Traditional benchmarks often rely on a single aggregate accuracy score, which may obscure underlying weaknesses in model performance. Our research aims to address these limitations by introducing a controlled evaluation framework that emphasizes the importance of event count and frequency in video reasoning tasks.\n\n# Method\nWe propose a trace-grounded parametric profiling approach that generates controlled videos with executable event traces. This method allows for a more nuanced evaluation of model performance by focusing on specific event access and retention capabilities. Our framework includes:\n\n1. **Controlled Evaluation Framework**: This framework replaces aggregate scores with capability surfaces that reflect model performance across varying event counts and frequencies.\n2. **Trace-Grounded Benchmark**: We provide a benchmark that audits model-reported events against executable ground truth, ensuring a rigorous assessment of temporal reasoning.\n3. **Targeted Interventions**: By employing denser sampling, event-centered keyframes, and prompt variants, we can diagnose whether errors stem from limited visual evidence, event localization issues, or reasoning over the recovered sequence.\n\n# Results\nOur findings indicate that final-answer accuracy alone can mischaracterize temporal reasoning capabilities. We provide evidence that the controlled failure pattern extends to natural repeated-event videos, suggesting that traditional benchmarks may not fully capture the complexities of video reasoning.\n\n## Figure 1\n*Overview of trace-grounded parametric profiling. Unlike standard benchmark evaluation based on a single aggregate accuracy score, our framework generates controlled videos with executable event traces.*\n\n# Related Work\n## Video-Language Evaluation\nRecent models in video-language evaluation, such as Qwen, InternVL, and Molmo, combine strong visual encoders with long-context reasoning. Existing benchmarks assess broad temporal understanding on fixed, heterogeneous clips, but often fail to provide the granularity needed for effective evaluation.\n\n## Grounded Video Evaluation\nMORSE-500 exemplifies the value of fully scripted, controllable videos for stress-testing multimodal reasoning. While other studies, such as TransRAC, focus on repetitive-action counting from natural video, they do not evaluate model-reported temporal reasoning in a controlled manner.\n\n# Conclusion\nOur research contributes to the understanding of video reasoning by providing a framework that emphasizes the importance of event count and frequency. By moving beyond traditional aggregate accuracy scores, we can better diagnose model performance and identify areas for improvement in temporal reasoning tasks. Future work will explore the transferability of our findings to natural video settings and further refine our evaluation methodologies.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a novel approach to evaluating video-language models (VLMs) through controlled profiling and trace evaluation. We introduce a method that utilizes programmatically generated videos with known event structures to isolate model performance metrics. Our evaluation focuses on the accuracy of event counting and recovery in VLMs, specifically examining the impact of event frequency and count on model performance.\n\n# Introduction\nThe evaluation of video-language models often suffers from confounding factors such as event count, event rate, duration, visual complexity, and semantic content. This paper addresses these challenges by employing a controlled environment where the event structure is precisely defined. We aim to provide a clearer understanding of model capabilities and limitations in processing temporal events in video data.\n\n# Method\n## Controlled Profiling and Trace Evaluation\nTo mitigate the complexities of real-world video benchmarks, we generate videos with known event structures. Each video is paired with an executable trace that reflects the same event schedule used for rendering. This allows for a direct comparison between model predictions and the expected outcomes.\n\n### Model Evaluation Setup\nWe utilize Gemini 3.6 Flash as our primary video-language model due to its advanced capabilities in handling native video input. Additionally, we employ Qwen3-VL-235B as a supplementary model for cross-system validation. Each model operates under specific configurations, with Gemini processing at 1 FPS and Qwen at 2 FPS. The models are evaluated using a zero-shot template across various domains without any per-cell tuning.\n\n### Controlled Temporal Task Space\nEach video is characterized by its domain \\(d\\), event count \\(N\\), event frequency \\(F\\), and a random seed \\(s\\). The event definitions vary across different tasks, such as wall contacts in Bounce_ball or on-pulses in Blinking. The seed influences visual attributes while maintaining the event schedule. We evaluate a range of event counts and frequencies, generating a total of \\(S = 10\\) videos for each combination.\n\n## Executable Traces and Metrics\nFor a video containing \\(N\\) events, the renderer produces a trace defined as:\n\n\\[\nT^* = (t_i, e_i, s^-, s^+, c_i)^N, \\quad c_i = c_{i-1} + 1, \\quad c_0 = 0\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(t_i\\) is the event timestamp, \\(e_i\\) is the event type, \\(s^-\\) and \\(s^+\\) are the states before and after the event, and \\(c_i\\) is the cumulative count. The model's output includes \\(M\\) timestamped events and a final count \\(\\hat{y}\\), which we evaluate against the true event count \\(N\\).\n\n### Evaluation Metrics\nThe primary evaluation metrics include accuracy, precision, recall, and the F1 score, defined as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Acc} = \\frac{\\hat{y}}{N}, \\quad P = \\frac{K}{M}, \\quad R = \\frac{K}{N}, \\quad F_1 = \\frac{2PR}{P + R}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(K\\) represents the number of aligned event pairs. The Visual Observation Ratio (VOR) is also calculated to assess count bias.\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate that the models exhibit varying performance based on event frequency and count. The Accidental Correctness Rate (ACR) and Reasoning Failure Rate (RFR) are computed to further analyze the models' capabilities in accurately reporting event counts. The findings suggest that while some models perform well under certain conditions, they struggle with others, highlighting the need for further refinement in model training and evaluation.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study demonstrates the effectiveness of controlled profiling and trace evaluation in assessing video-language models. By isolating event structures and employing precise metrics, we provide a clearer picture of model performance. Future work will focus on expanding the range of evaluated models and refining the evaluation framework to enhance understanding of VLM capabilities in diverse contexts.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the operational boundaries and reliability of models in processing controlled video inputs. We define rigorous criteria for evaluating model performance based on event counts and frequencies, establishing a framework for assessing the reliability of model outputs.\n\n# Introduction\nThe ability of models to accurately interpret video data is critical in various applications, from autonomous systems to interactive media. This study aims to delineate the reliable operating regions of models by analyzing their performance across different event counts and frequencies.\n\n# Method\n## Experimental Protocol and Operating Boundaries\nWe conducted experiments using controlled videos and executable traces, as illustrated in Figure 2. For each pair of event count \\(N\\) and frequency \\(F\\), the renderer records event times, state changes, and cumulative counts \\(c_i\\) for alignment with model-reported timestamps.\n\nUnparseable final answers are deemed incorrect, and malformed traces receive no matches. Interventions utilize the same videos and seeds, with sampling and prompting altering one requested component. Event-centered keyframes serve as an upper-bound visual-access condition, as they may reveal \\(N\\).\n\nWe set a stringent operational reliability target of \\(\\tau = 0.80\\). A cell must be correct on at least 8 of 10 randomized renderings. This threshold summarizes the full surface rather than providing a confidence bound; the cell-wise accuracy surfaces remain available for inspection. Appendix B.5 details seed-level variation and boundary robustness.\n\nLet \\(N = N \\setminus \\{0\\}\\) denote positive counts. Here, \\(\\hat{d}\\) represents a visual domain, \\(\\theta\\) identifies the evaluated model, and \\(P_\\theta(N, F)\\) is its observed exact-match accuracy from Equation 1 at count \\(N\\) and frequency \\(F\\). For domain \\(d\\) and fixed frequency \\(F\\), the count boundary is defined as:\n\n\\[\nN^*_{d}(F) = \\max \\{ n \\in N \\mid \\min P_\\theta(n, F) \\geq \\tau \\} \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(n\\) is a candidate positive count, and \\(n'\\) ranges over each tested positive count up to \\(n\\). The outer maximum selects the largest candidate whose lowest observed accuracy across that prefix meets \\(\\tau\\). Thus, it is the largest tested count for which accuracy remains at least 80% at that count and every smaller positive count. If accuracy is below 80% at \\(N = 1\\), we report the count boundary as \\(N^*_{d}(F) &#x3C; 1\\). The \\(N = 0\\) condition is treated separately as a no-event control.\n\nFor domain \\(d\\) and fixed count \\(N\\), the complementary operational frequency boundary is defined as:\n\n\\[\nF^*_{d}(N) = \\max \\{ f \\in F \\mid \\min P_\\theta(N, f) \\geq \\tau \\} \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n## Role of Input Sampling\nThe profiles evaluate complete end-to-end systems. Gemini receives 1 FPS input, while Qwen receives 2 FPS input, introducing sampling and interface as confounding factors. Higher-density frame sequences in Section 6 probe whether sampling contributes to high-frequency failures.\n\n# Results\n## Gemini Count Boundaries\nThe Bounce Ball task reaches \\(N = 10\\) only at 0.5 Hz and fails the target at the first positive count from 1.0 to 4.0 Hz. The Blinking task shows no reliable positive-count region. The State Machine task is reliable through \\(N = 12\\) at 0.5 and 1.0 Hz, contracts to \\(N = 2\\) at 1.5 Hz, and is generally limited to \\(N = 1\\) above it.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study establishes a framework for evaluating the reliability of models in processing video data, highlighting the importance of operational boundaries defined by event counts and frequencies. Future work will explore the implications of these findings in practical applications and further refine the assessment criteria.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the performance of two models, Gemini and Qwen3-VL-235B, across various synthetic domains, focusing on their capabilities in event counting and timestamp accuracy. We analyze the operating regions of these models, particularly in relation to their ability to handle different frequencies and counts of events.\n\n# Introduction\nThe ability of machine learning models to accurately process and interpret events in synthetic domains is crucial for applications in various fields. This study compares the performance of Gemini and Qwen3-VL-235B, particularly in terms of their final-answer exact match and timestamp recovery across different event counts and frequencies.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted experiments to evaluate the models' performance across three synthetic domains: Bounce Ball, Blinking, and State Machine. The evaluation metrics included final-answer exact match, timestamp recall, and trace F₁ score. The models were tested under varying conditions of event counts (N) and frequencies (F).\n\n# Results\n## Capability Surfaces\nFigure 3 illustrates the capability surfaces of both models across the synthetic domains. Gemini demonstrates a broader region for persistent State Machine transitions, while Qwen shows a concentration of accuracy at lower counts and frequencies.\n\n### Frequency Boundaries\nGemini's performance is characterized by specific frequency boundaries. For instance, Bounce Ball is reliable only up to 0.5 Hz, while Blinking lacks a reliable positive-count frequency region. State Machine achieves 3.0 Hz at N = 1, 1.5 Hz at N = 2, and 1.0 Hz from N = 3 to N = 12. Notably, Bounce Ball has an isolated success at N = 8 at 1.0 Hz, but its accuracy at N = 12 is below the target at the lowest tested frequency.\n\n### Operating-Region Interpretation\nThe analysis reveals that there is no universal count or frequency threshold applicable to all models. Persistent State Machine transitions yield the broadest operational region, although the visual form and semantics vary across domains. The active span grows as N/F, profiling joint event load and retention span.\n\n## Trace Fidelity\nThe fidelity of trace representation is assessed, with macro-average final exact match at 21.1%, timestamp recall at 31.2%, and trace F₁ at 36.4%. State Machine outperforms the other domains with a 37.1% exact match and 53.9% trace F₁, while Blinking shows the lowest performance with 6.4% exact match and 18.6% trace F₁.\n\n### Supplementary Qwen Comparison\nFigure 3b provides a supplementary comparison of the Qwen3-VL-235B system. The results indicate that Bounce Ball and Blinking lack a broad reliable region, and State Machine only achieves N = 1 in isolated frequency cells. The performance ordering aligns with the operating regions depicted in Figure 3a, indicating that persistent event displays yield more recoverable outputs.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study highlights the differences in performance between Gemini and Qwen3-VL-235B across synthetic domains, emphasizing the importance of understanding operating regions and frequency boundaries for effective model deployment in event processing tasks. Further research is needed to explore the underlying mechanisms that contribute to these performance variations.\n\n\n\n# Evaluation of Trace Metrics in Task Domains\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper evaluates the performance of trace metrics across various task domains and capability regions, focusing on the Bounce Ball intervention. We analyze the effectiveness of different sampling rates and intervention strategies in improving timestamp recovery and overall accuracy.\n\n## Introduction\nThe evaluation of trace metrics is crucial for understanding the performance of models in various task domains. This study investigates the impact of different factors, such as event count and frequency, on the accuracy of reported timestamps and final answers. We specifically focus on the Bounce Ball task due to its complexity, which requires both spatial localization and temporal reasoning.\n\n## Method\nWe conducted a series of evaluations categorized into three parts:\n\n1. **Trace Evaluation by Task Domain**: We assessed the performance of the model across different tasks, including Bounce Ball, Blinking, and State Machine.\n2. **Trace Evaluation across Capability Regions**: We analyzed the model's performance in regions defined by event count and frequency.\n3. **Trace Diagnosis of Bounce Ball Interventions**: We examined various intervention strategies to improve timestamp recovery.\n\nThe metrics used for evaluation include Final EM, VOR Trace P, Trace R, Trace F₁, ACR, and RFR, as summarized in Table 1.\n\n## Results\n### Part A: Trace Evaluation by Task Domain\nThe results for trace evaluation by task domain are summarized in Table 1. The Bounce Ball task achieved a Final EM of 19.6%, with a VOR Trace P of 0.71 and a Trace F₁ of 36.6%. The State Machine task performed the best with a Final EM of 37.1%.\n\n### Part B: Trace Evaluation across Capability Regions\nIn this section, we observed significant variations in performance based on the capability regions. The Low Count, Low Frequency region yielded the highest Final EM of 47.7%, while the High Count, High Frequency region showed a drastic drop to 0.2%.\n\n### Part C: Trace Diagnosis of Bounce Ball Interventions\nDifferent intervention strategies were tested to improve performance in the Bounce Ball task. The Event-Centered Keyframes approach yielded the highest Final EM of 68.6%, indicating that targeted interventions can significantly enhance timestamp recovery.\n\n## Discussion\nThe analysis reveals that the error patterns change with count and frequency. In the low-count, low-frequency region, the Visual Observation Ratio is 1.19, indicating over-reporting of timestamps. Conversely, in the high-count, low-frequency region, under-reporting is prevalent, with a Visual Observation Ratio of 0.57.\n\nThe Bounce Ball task's complexity necessitates a careful balance between spatial and temporal reasoning, which is reflected in the varying performance across different interventions. The results suggest that denser sampling can improve temporal access, as evidenced by the increase in accuracy from 19.6% to 29.3% with 4 FPS sampling.\n\n## Conclusion\nThis study highlights the importance of evaluating trace metrics across different task domains and capability regions. The findings suggest that targeted interventions and denser sampling can significantly improve timestamp recovery and overall accuracy in complex tasks like Bounce Ball. Future work should focus on refining the alignment of missed and merged events to further enhance performance.\n\n## References\n- Table 1: Timestamp-recovery evaluation for Gemini 3.6 Flash. Trace metrics use δ(F ) = 1/(2F ) and positive-event trials. Final EM includes no-event controls. Part B uses N ≤ 3, N ≥ 5, F ≤ 2.0, and F ≥ 2.5 Hz. Part C evaluates Bounce Ball.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis study investigates the impact of various interventions on the accuracy of event recognition in natural videos. We analyze the effects of sampling, event-centered frames, and prompting on the recovery of ground-truth events. Our findings reveal that while keyframe evidence significantly enhances recovery, the reliability of results diminishes as the number of events increases.\n\n# Introduction\nThe ability to accurately recognize events in natural videos is crucial for various applications in computer vision and artificial intelligence. This research aims to explore how different prompting formats and visual access interventions affect the accuracy of event recognition. We focus on the challenges posed by high event counts and the limitations of existing methods.\n\n# Method\nWe utilized 151 clips from the Tran-sRAC dataset with repetition annotations (Hu et al. 2022), grouped by event count. Each clip was evaluated under three prompting formats: zero-shot, direct-answer, and chain-of-thought prompting, across 456 trials. Keyframes were provided at specific time intervals (ti - 0.1, ti, ti + 0.1 seconds) to facilitate event recognition while minimizing the search space.\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate that keyframe evidence produces the largest recovery, achieving an accuracy of 68.6% through N = 5. However, accuracy declines significantly at higher event counts, with a trace F₁ score remaining at 9.7%. The analysis shows that while structured tracing improves precision to 59.2%, it only recovers 31.5% of true events. No prompting format successfully expands the reliable count or frequency region.\n\n## Figure 4\n*Figure 4: Bounce Ball interventions. Final-answer exact match averaged over frequencies. Sampling helps low counts, event-centered frames help through N = 5, and prompting does not expand the reliable region.*\n\n## Figure 5\n*Figure 5: Natural-video count-pattern check. Exact-match accuracy on 152 TransRAC clips (Hu et al. 2022). All prompting formats concentrate accuracy in low-count bins.*\n\n# Conclusion and Discussion\nThe findings of this study highlight the limitations of current methods in recognizing events in natural videos, particularly as event counts increase. The interventions tested demonstrate that improved visual access does not eliminate the challenges associated with high-count boundaries. Our results suggest that future research should focus on developing more robust methods for event recognition that can handle varying event loads and improve overall accuracy.\n\n## Limitations\nThis study is constrained by the use of clean, regularly spaced repeated events, and the evaluation of natural videos does not control for frequency or utilize executable traces. The reported traces serve as behavioral evidence rather than direct access to latent computation.\n\n\n\n# Acknowledgments\nBaskar, Cai, Shabihi, Satheesh, Islam, and Huang are supported by DARPA HR001124S0029-AIQ-FP-019, National Science Foundation TRAILS Institute (2229885). Private support was provided by Open Philanthropy and Apple. The Authors acknowledge the National Artificial Intelligence Research Resource (NAIRR) Pilot for contributing to this research result.\n\n# References\nAgarwal, A.; Patel, H. L.; Panda, S.; Meghwani, H.; Singh, J.; Dua, K.; Li, P.; Sheng, T.; Ravi, S.; and Roth, D. 2025. RCI: A Score for Evaluating Global and Local Reasoning in Multimodal Benchmarks. arXiv:2509.23673.\n\nBai, S.; Cai, Y.; Chen, R.; Chen, K.; Chen, X.; Cheng, Z.; Deng, L.; Ding, W.; Gao, C.; Ge, C.; Ge, W.; Guo, Z.; Huang, Q.; Huang, J.; Huang, F.; Hui, B.; Jiang, S.; Li, Z.; Li, M.; Li, M.; Li, K.; Lin, Z.; Lin, J.; Liu, X.; Liu, J.; Liu, C.; Liu, Y.; Liu, D.; Liu, S.; Lu, D.; Luo, R.; Lv, C.; Men, R.; Meng, L.; Ren, X.; Ren, X.; Song, S.; Sun, Y.; Tang, J.; Tu, J.; Wan, J.; Wang, P.; Wang, P.; Wang, Q.; Wang, Y.; Xie, T.; Xu, Y.; Xu, H.; Xu, J.; Yang, Z.; Yang, M.; Yang, J.; Yang, A.; Yu, B.; Zhang, F.; Zhang, H.; Zhang, X.; Zheng, B.; Zhong, H.; Zhou, J.; Zhou, F.; Zhou, J.; Zhu, Y.; and Zhu, K. 2025. Qwen3-VL Technical Report. arXiv:2511.21631.\n\nCai, Z.; Wang, A.; Satheesh, A.; Nakhawa, A.; Jae, H.; Powell, K.; Liu, M.; Jay, N.; Oh, S.; Wang, X.; Liang, Y.; Goldstein, T.; and Huang, F. 2025. MORSE-500: A Programmatically Controllable Video Benchmark to Stress-Test Multimodal Reasoning. arXiv:2506.05523.\n\nChandrasegaran, K.; Gupta, A.; Hadzic, L. M.; Kota, T.; He, J.; Eyzaguirre, C.; Durante, Z.; Li, M.; Wu, J.; and Fei-Fei, L. 2024. HourVideo: 1-Hour Video-Language Understanding. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, volume 37, 53168–53197.\n\nChen, L.; Li, J.; Dong, X.; Zhang, P.; Zang, Y.; Chen, Z.; Duan, H.; Wang, J.; Qiao, Y.; Lin, D.; and Zhao, F. 2024. Are We on the Right Way for Evaluating Large Vision-Language Models? arXiv:2403.20330.\n\nCheng, Z.; Hu, J.; Liu, Z.; Si, C. P.; Li, W.; and Gong, S. 2026. V-STaR: Benchmarking Video-LLMs on Video Spatio-Temporal Reasoning. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR) Findings.\n\nChow, W.; Mao, J.; Li, B.; Seita, D.; Guizilini, V.; and Wang, Y. 2025. PhysBench: Benchmarking and Enhancing Vision-Language Models for Physical World Understanding. In International Conference on Learning Representations.\n\nClark, C.; Zhang, J.; Ma, Z.; Park, J. S.; Salehi, M.; Tripathi, R.; Lee, S.; Ren, Z.; Kim, C. D.; Yang, Y.; Shao, V.; Yang, Y.; Huang, W.; Gao, Z.; Anderson, T.; Zhang, J.; Jain, J.; Stoica, G.; Han, W.; Farhadi, A.; and Krishna, R. 2026. Molmo2: Open Weights and Data for Vision-Language Models with Video Understanding and Grounding. arXiv:2601.10611.\n\nCores, D.; Dorkenwald, M.; Mucientes, M.; Snoek, C. G. M.; and Asano, Y. M. 2025. Lost in Time: A New Temporal Benchmark for VideoLLMs. In 36th British Machine Vision Conference 2025, BMVC 2025. BMVA.\n\nFeng, B.; Lai, Z.; Li, S.; Wang, Z.; Wang, S.; Huang, P.; and Cao, M. 2025. Breaking Down Video LLM Benchmarks: Knowledge, Spatial Perception, or True Temporal Understanding? arXiv preprint arXiv:2505.14321.\n\nFu, C.; Dai, Y.; Luo, Y.; Li, L.; Ren, S.; Zhang, R.; Wang, Z.; Zhou, C.; Shen, Y.; Zhang, M.; Chen, P.; Li, Y.; Lin, S.; Zhao, S.; Li, K.; Xu, T.; Zheng, X.; Chen, E.; Shan, C.; He, R.; and Sun, X. 2025. Video-MME: The First-Ever Comprehensive Evaluation Benchmark of Multi-modal LLMs in Video Analysis. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 24108–24118.\n\nFu, X.; Hu, Y.; Li, B.; Feng, Y.; Wang, H.; Lin, X.; Roth, D.; Smith, N. A.; Ma, W.-C.; and Krishna, R. 2024. BLINK: Multimodal Large Language Models Can See but Not Perceive. arXiv:2404.12390.\n\nHao, Y.; Gu, J.; Wang, H. W.; Li, L.; Yang, Z.; Wang, L.; and Cheng, Y. 2025. Can MLLMs Reason in Multimodality? EMMA: An Enhanced MultiModal ReAsoning Benchmark. In Proceedings of the 42nd International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2025).\n\nHu, H.; Dong, S.; Zhao, Y.; Lian, D.; Li, Z.; and Gao, S. 2022. TransRAC: Encoding Multi-scale Temporal Correlation with Transformers for Repetitive Action Counting. arXiv preprint arXiv:2204.01018.\n\nJohnson, J.; Hariharan, B.; van der Maaten, L.; Fei-Fei, L.; Zitnick, C. L.; and Girshick, R. 2017. CLEVR: A Diagnostic Dataset for Compositional Language and Elementary Visual Reasoning. In Proceedings of the IEEE Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 2901–2910.\n\nLi, C.; Chen, Q.; Li, Z.; Tao, F.; and Zhang, Y. 2024a. VideoCogQA: A Controllable Benchmark for Evaluating Cognitive Abilities in Video-Language Models. arXiv:2411.09105.\n\nLi, K.; Wang, Y.; He, Y.; Li, Y.; Wang, Y.; Liu, Y.; Wang, Z.; Xu, J.; Chen, G.; Luo, P.; Wang, L.; and Qiao, Y. 2024b. MVBench: A Comprehensive Multi-modal Video Understanding Benchmark. arXiv:2311.17005.\n\nLi, Y.; Chen, X.; Hu, B.; Wang, L.; Shi, H.; and Zhang, M. 2024c. Videovista: A versatile benchmark for video understanding and reasoning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.11303.\n\nLiu, Y.; Li, S.; Liu, Y.; Wang, Y.; Ren, S.; Li, L.; Chen, S.; Sun, X.; and Hou, L. 2024. TempCompass: Do Video LLMs Really Understand Videos? In Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2024. Association for Computational Linguistics.\n\nLiu, Y.; Ouyang, K.; Wu, H.; Liu, Y.; Sui, L.; Li, X.; Zhong, Y.; Charles, Y.; Zhou, X.; and Sun, X. 2025. VideoReasonBench: Can MLLMs Perform Vision-Centric Complex Video Reasoning? arXiv preprint arXiv:2505.23359.\n\n\n\n# Needle In A Video Haystack: A Scalable Synthetic Evaluator for Video MLLMs\n\n## Abstract\nIn this paper, we introduce a novel framework for evaluating video multimodal large language models (MLLMs) through a synthetic evaluator. Our approach leverages a scalable methodology to generate diverse video scenarios, enabling comprehensive assessment of MLLMs' reasoning capabilities in video understanding tasks. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our evaluator through extensive experiments, highlighting its potential to enhance the robustness and reliability of video MLLMs.\n\n## Introduction\nThe rapid advancement of multimodal large language models (MLLMs) has opened new avenues for understanding and generating content across various modalities, including text and video. However, evaluating the performance of these models in video understanding remains a challenge due to the complexity and variability of video data. Traditional evaluation methods often fall short in capturing the nuanced reasoning required for effective video comprehension. This paper presents a scalable synthetic evaluator designed to address these limitations and provide a robust framework for assessing video MLLMs.\n\n## Method\nOur synthetic evaluator is built upon a generative model that creates diverse video scenarios, incorporating various elements such as actions, objects, and contextual information. The evaluation process involves the following steps:\n\n1. **Scenario Generation**: We utilize a generative model to create a wide range of video scenarios, ensuring diversity in content and complexity.\n2. **Model Evaluation**: MLLMs are evaluated based on their ability to comprehend and reason about the generated video scenarios. We employ a set of metrics to quantify their performance, including accuracy, reasoning depth, and contextual understanding.\n3. **Scalability Assessment**: We analyze the scalability of our evaluator by measuring its performance across different model sizes and configurations.\n\n## Results\nOur experiments demonstrate that the synthetic evaluator effectively captures the reasoning capabilities of video MLLMs. The results indicate a significant correlation between the complexity of the generated scenarios and the models' performance. Notably, larger models exhibit improved reasoning abilities, particularly in scenarios that require multi-step reasoning and contextual awareness.\n\n### Figure 1: Performance Comparison\n*Figure 1 illustrates the performance of various MLLMs across different scenario complexities, highlighting the advantages of our synthetic evaluator in assessing model capabilities.*\n\n## Discussion\nThe findings underscore the importance of robust evaluation frameworks in advancing the field of video understanding. Our synthetic evaluator not only provides a scalable solution for assessing MLLMs but also contributes to the ongoing discourse on the evaluation of multimodal models. Future work will focus on refining the scenario generation process and exploring additional metrics for a more comprehensive evaluation.\n\n## Conclusion\nIn conclusion, we present a scalable synthetic evaluator for video MLLMs that addresses the challenges of traditional evaluation methods. Our approach facilitates a deeper understanding of model capabilities and paves the way for future advancements in video language understanding.\n\n## References\n- Lu, H.; Huo, Y.; Du, Y.; Zhao, Z.; Guo, L.; Wang, B.; Chen, W.; Wang, X.; Wei, J.; Schuurmans, D.; Le, Q.; Chi, E.; Narang, S.; Chowdhery, A.; and Zhou, D. 2022. Self-consistency improves chain of thought reasoning in language models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2203.11171.\n- Lu, P.; Bansal, H.; Xia, T.; Liu, J.; Li, C.; Hajishirzi, H.; Cheng, H.; Chang, K.-W.; Galley, M.; and Gao, J. 2024. MathVista: Evaluating Mathematical Reasoning of Foundation Models in Visual Contexts. arXiv:2310.02255.\n- Mangalam, K.; Akshulakov, R.; and Malik, J. 2023. EgoSchema: A Diagnostic Benchmark for Very Long-form Video Language Understanding. In Oh, A.; Naumann, T.; Globerson, A.; Saenko, K.; Hardt, M.; and Levine, S., eds., Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, volume 36, 46212–46244. Curran Associates, Inc.\n- Qiu, S.; Guo, S.; Song, Z.-Y.; Sun, Y.; Cai, Z.; Wei, J.; Luo, T.; Yin, Y.; Zhang, H.; Hu, Y.; et al. 2025. Phybench: Holistic evaluation of physical perception and reasoning in large language models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2504.16074.\n- Rawal, R.; Saifullah, K.; Farr’e, M.; Basri, R.; Jacobs, D.; Somepalli, G.; and Goldstein, T. 2024. Cinepile: A long video question answering dataset and benchmark. arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.08813.\n\n\n\n# Contents\n\n## 1 Introduction\n1\n\n## 2 Related Work\n2\n\n## 3 Controlled Profiling and Trace Evaluation\n3\n### 3.1 Controlled Temporal Task Space\n3\n### 3.2 Executable Traces and Metrics\n3\n### 3.3 Experimental Protocol and Operating Boundaries\n4\n\n## 4 Mapping Reliable Operating Regions\n4\n\n## 5 Diagnosing Trace-Level Failures\n5\n\n## 6 What Moves the Boundary?\n6\n\n## 7 Natural-Video Boundary Transfer\n7\n\n## 8 Conclusion and Discussion\n7\n\n## A Benchmark Setup and Model Specifications\n10\n### A.1 Task and Renderer Specifications\n10\n### A.2 Supplied Frame Sequences\n11\n### A.3 Model Configurations\n11\n\n## B Capability Boundaries and Statistical Robustness\n12\n### B.1 Qwen3-VL Scale Comparison\n12\n### B.2 InternVL3.5 Scale and Domain Comparison\n12\n### B.3 Operational Boundary Summary\n12\n### B.4 Seed-Level Variation\n12\n### B.5 Operational Boundary Robustness\n13\n\n## C Interventions, Prompts, and Trace Evaluation\n13\n### C.1 Domain Task Questions\n13\n### C.2 Experiment 1: Full N × F Matrix Boundary Sweep\n13\n### C.3 Experiment 2: Frame Sampling Density Interventions\n13\n### C.4 Experiment 3: Oracle Keyframe Evidence Interventions\n13\n### C.5 Experiment 4: Prompting &#x26; Reasoning Mode Interventions\n14\n### C.6 Experiment 5: MORSE Trace Diagnosis &#x26; Error Taxonomy\n14\n### C.7 Real-World Transfer Evaluation Prompts\n14\n### C.8 Output Parsing and Evaluation\n15\n### C.9 Cross-Model Prompting Results\n15\n### C.10 Frame-Sampling Density Sweeps\n15\n### C.11 Event-Centered Keyframe Extraction\n15\n### C.12 Complete Gemini Intervention Analyses\n15\n### C.13 Open-Model Keyframe and Sampling Interventions\n15\n### C.14 GPT-5.6 Sol Frame-Sequence Interventions &#x26; API Limitations\n15\n### C.15 Trace Extraction and Matching Algorithm\n17\n### C.16 Aggregate Trace Error Summary\n18\n\n## D Computational Cost Analysis\n18\n\n## E Extended Related Work\n18\n### E.1 Video-Language Benchmarks and Controlled Evaluation\n18\n### E.2 Programmatic Video and Intermediate Traces\n19\n\n## F Qualitative Trace Examples and Taxonomy Profiling Case-Selection Protocol\n19\n### F.1 Faithful\n20\n### F.2 Event Recovery (Correct Matches)\n20\n### F.3 Missed Events (Under-Reporting / Perception Failure)\n20\n### F.4 Hallucinated Events (Over-Reporting / Spurious Detection)\n20\n### F.5 Wrong Accumulation (Reasoning Failure Ratio / RFR)\n20\n### F.6 Accidental Correctness (Accidental Correctness Ratio / ACR)\n20\n### F.7 Temporally Displaced Events\n20\n\n## Appendix Overview\nThis appendix provides the extended evidence, methodological detail, and reproducibility material that support the main paper’s central finding: current video–language models can fail at elementary event bookkeeping as temporal load increases. The sections below connect each supplement directly to a component of the main-paper argument.\n\n- **Section A** specifies the controlled Manim-based task renderer, supplied frame sequences, and model configurations. These details establish that the capability boundary is measured on videos with known temporal ground truth.\n- **Section B** provides the full capability heatmaps, cross-model boundary summaries, seed-level variation, and operational boundary robustness, showing how performance changes across event count, frequency, scale, and domain.\n- **Section C** details diagnostic interventions (prompting formats, sampling density sweeps, and oracle keyframes) alongside trace extraction, matching algorithms, and error auditing.\n- **Section D** provides the complete computational API and financial cost analysis across all experiments and visual domains.\n- **Section E** situates the benchmark within the broader video-language and programmatic video literature.\n- **Section F** provides representative qualitative trace examples across all 6 diagnostic failure categories.\n\n\n\n# Manim Animation Engine and Trace Generation\n\nWe construct all synthetic video benchmarks using the Manim¹ mathematical animation engine in Python. Manim is chosen because it allows programmatic, frame-accurate control over spatial trajectories, visual state transitions, and rendering framerates without visual compression artifacts or timing jitter. For each trial (d, N, F, s):\n\n1. **Event Scheduling**: Given count \\( N \\) and frequency \\( F \\), a deterministic event scheduler calculates the ground-truth event timestamps \\( t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_N \\) within the active temporal window.\n\n2. **Manim Video Rendering**: Manim scenes procedurally animate visual elements (wall collisions in `bounce_ball`, visual pulse opacities in `blinking`, and categorical node highlights in `state_machine`) matching the exact timestamps \\( t_i \\).\n\n3. **Executable Trace Generation**: Simultaneously during scene construction, the renderer logs an executable ground-truth trace\n\n\\[\nT^* = ((t_i, e_i, s^-, s^+, c_i))^N_{i=1}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( t_i \\) is the event timestamp, \\( e_i \\) is the event type, \\( s^- \\) and \\( s^+ \\) are the states before and after the event, and \\( c_i \\) is the running cumulative count. Executing the trace returns the final count \\( c_N = N \\).\n\nWe evaluate \\( N \\in \\{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12\\} \\) and \\( F \\in \\{0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 3.5, 4.0\\} \\) Hz. Every clip has a fixed duration of 24 seconds. The active event sequence occupies approximately \\( N/F \\) seconds. The remaining time is filled with static frames before or after the active sequence, with the allocation determined by the rendering seed. Thus, the design varies event load and active temporal span without changing total duration.\n\nFor each positive-count \\( (N, F) \\) cell, we render ten randomized videos. The seed changes nuisance appearance factors, including color, geometry, trajectory, initial state, orientation, and start delay, while preserving the event schedule. The \\( N = 0 \\) control is rendered ten times at nominal \\( F = 1.0 \\) Hz and is excluded from positive-event trace ratios. This yields 730 videos per domain and 2,190 videos across the three domains.\n\n## A.2 Supplied Frame Sequences\n\nThe reported sampling rates describe the frames supplied to the evaluated systems, not undocumented internal video processing. Gemini receives native video at approximately 1 FPS in our setup and Qwen receives 2 FPS. The Bounce Ball visual-access study reuses the same generated videos and seed identifiers while changing one source of evidence at a time. Frame-sampling conditions provide regularly sampled frame sequences at the selected density. The event-centered keyframe condition supplies three frames around each true event, at \\( t_i - 0.1 \\), \\( t_i \\), and \\( t_i + 0.1 \\) seconds. This condition removes the need to search the full video for event locations, but it still requires the model to retain and aggregate the supplied event evidence. The frame interventions therefore diagnose whether a change in final-answer accuracy is compatible with improved external visual access, without attributing the change to any particular internal mechanism.\n\n## A.3 Model Configurations\n\nThe main synthetic evaluation compares Gemini 3.6 Flash and Qwen3-VL-235B. Both systems receive the same domain-specific zero-shot question and use their native video interfaces. Gemini is supplied approximately 1 FPS and Qwen 2 FPS. These rates are external input settings. They should not be interpreted as measurements of either system’s internal temporal processing.\n\nTable 2 reports the configuration, domain-level exact-match accuracy, and maximum count boundary for the available cross-model profiles. The table shows that State Machine is the strongest domain for every listed model, while the reliable count prefix remains short for most open-model configurations. The 38B InternVL variant reaches the largest open-model count boundary of six, but this remains below Gemini’s boundary of twelve.\n\nTable 3 compares the main-paper Gemini macro average with pooled open-model trace metrics. Although the two render sets differ slightly, both instantiate the same three tasks and the same \\( N \\)-by-\\( F \\) design. The comparison is therefore task-matched rather than paired. As detailed in Appendix C.15, these statistics evaluate timestamps expressed in the model response and constitute behavioral evidence rather than direct measurements of internal representations.\n\nGemini attains the highest pooled trace \\( F_1 \\) (36.4%). Among the open models, Qwen3-VL-8B attains the highest reported-trace \\( F_1 \\) (15.8%), but exhibits severe over-reporting (VOR = 13.04). Qwen3-VL-32B and Qwen3-VL-235B instead under-report events (VOR = 0.43 and 0.21, respectively). InternVL yields nonzero final-answer accuracy but no extractable timestamped events. Accordingly, its zero trace values characterize the format of its visible responses, rather than establishing that the model observed no events. A controlled cross-model trace-fidelity comparison would require further investigation.\n\n1. Manim GitHub Repository\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a comparative analysis of various models in terms of their performance on tasks involving temporal profiles, specifically focusing on the Bounce Ball, Blinking, and State Machine tasks. We evaluate the models based on exact-match accuracy and other relevant metrics across different input conditions. The results highlight the operational boundaries and statistical robustness of each model, providing insights into their capabilities and limitations.\n\n# Introduction\nThe study of temporal profiles in machine learning models has gained significant attention, particularly in the context of video-language tasks. This paper aims to compare the performance of several models, including Gemini 3.6 Flash and various configurations of Qwen and InternVL3.5, across different temporal domains. We focus on the exact-match accuracy and other metrics to understand the operational boundaries of these models.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted experiments across multiple models, evaluating their performance on tasks involving temporal profiles. Each model was tested under controlled conditions, with a focus on event counts and frequencies. The metrics used for evaluation included exact-match accuracy (EM), visual object recognition precision (VOR P), recall (R), trace F₁ score, accuracy (ACR), and recall for false responses (RFR).\n\n# Results\n## Cross-model Temporal-profile Summary\nTable 2 summarizes the exact-match accuracy for each model across the Bounce Ball, Blinking, and State Machine tasks. The results indicate varying performance levels, with Gemini 3.6 Flash achieving the highest accuracy in most categories.\n\n| Model                 | Input           | Bounce EM (%) | Blink EM (%) | State EM (%) | Max. count boundary |\n|-----------------------|-----------------|----------------|---------------|---------------|---------------------|\n| Gemini 3.6 Flash      | 1 FPS           | 19.6          | 6.4           | 37.1          | 12                  |\n| Qwen3-VL-8B          | 2 FPS           | 12.4          | 10.9          | 26.6          | 2                   |\n| Qwen3-VL-32B         | 2 FPS           | 15.5          | 13.2          | 26.2          | 1                   |\n| Qwen3-VL-235B        | 2 FPS           | 19.2          | 12.0          | 26.4          | 1                   |\n| InternVL3.5-8B       | 16 frames       | 9.0           | 11.2          | 26.2          | 3                   |\n| InternVL3.5-30B-A3B  | 16 frames       | 9.2           | 10.4          | 24.5          | 3                   |\n| InternVL3.5-38B      | 16 frames       | 12.8          | 9.9           | 32.0          | 6                   |\n\n*Table 2: Cross-model temporal-profile summary. Exact-match accuracy is reported separately for Bounce Ball, Blinking, and State Machine across 730 trials per domain. The maximum count boundary is the largest reliable contiguous positive-count prefix observed at any tested frequency and domain.*\n\n## Task-matched Cross-model Trace Metrics\nTable 3 presents the task-matched trace metrics across temporal domains, providing a macro average of the performance metrics for each model.\n\n| Model                 | EM (%) | VOR P (%) | R (%) | Trace F₁ (%) | ACR (%) | RFR (%) | |\n|-----------------------|--------|-----------|-------|---------------|---------|---------|---|\n| Gemini 3.6 Flash      | 21.1   | 0.68      | 58.0  | 31.2          | 36.4    | 5.7     | 2.3     |\n| Qwen3-VL-8B          | 16.6   | 13.04     | 21.0  | 18.8          | 15.8    | 6.9     | 1.4     |\n| Qwen3-VL-32B         | 18.3   | 0.43      | 21.2  | 14.0          | 14.8    | 9.3     | 1.5     |\n| Qwen3-VL-235B        | 19.2   | 0.21      | 18.4  | 10.0          | 11.4    | 10.3    | 1.1     |\n| InternVL3.5-8B       | 15.5   | 0.00      | 0.0   | 0.0           | 0.0     | 15.5    | 0.0     |\n| InternVL3.5-30B-A3B  | 14.7   | 0.00      | 0.0   | 0.0           | 0.0     | 13.7    | 0.0     |\n| InternVL3.5-38B      | 18.2   | 0.00      | 0.0   | 0.0           | 0.0     | 13.8    | 0.0     |\n\n*Table 3: Task-matched cross-model trace metrics across temporal domains. Macro averages reported on task-matched videos, with 730 trials per domain. Metrics score only explicit, parser-recognized timestamps and do not infer events from free-form text.*\n\n# Discussion\nThe results indicate that the models exhibit a common structured-output prompt, with performance being strongest in the small-count regime. As the event count increases, performance tends to decline, even in low-frequency conditions where events are well separated. This trend is consistent across the different models evaluated.\n\n## Capability Boundaries and Statistical Robustness\nThis section provides full-resolution capability surfaces for the primary evaluation and extended model-family comparisons. Each cell reports final-answer exact match for a controlled event-count and event-frequency condition. The family plots provide a qualitative cross-architecture check of whether the low-count concentration observed in the primary profiles recurs at other model scales.\n\n### Qwen3-VL Scale Comparison\nFigure 11 compares Qwen3-VL-8B, Qwen3-VL-32B, and Qwen3-VL-235B on the State Machine task. The accuracy is concentrated at small event counts and falls sharply once the count exceeds four.\n\n### InternVL3.5 Scale and Domain Comparison\nFigure 12 extends the comparison to InternVL3.5-8B, InternVL3.5-30B-A3B, and InternVL3.5-38B across State Machine tasks. The results indicate that larger scales improve some easy cells but do not eliminate the count-dependent boundary.\n\n## Seed-Level Variation\nEach synthetic cell contains ten independently randomized renderings. The reported accuracy is calculated as k/10, where k is the number of correct answers. Seed-level uncertainty is summarized with a two-sided 95% Wilson score interval for selected headline cells and intervention comparisons.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe comparative analysis of the models reveals significant insights into their performance across various temporal tasks. The operational boundaries and statistical robustness of each model highlight the challenges faced in scaling performance with increasing event counts. Future work should focus on understanding the internal mechanisms that contribute to these observed boundaries.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a comprehensive evaluation of video event counting through a series of structured experiments. We investigate the operational boundaries of event counting models under varying conditions, including frame sampling density and the provision of oracle keyframe evidence. Our findings reveal significant insights into the performance limitations and capabilities of these models.\n\n# Introduction\nThe ability to accurately count events in video data is crucial for various applications, including surveillance, sports analytics, and human-computer interaction. This research aims to establish a baseline for operational capabilities in event counting models and to explore the effects of different sampling densities and keyframe evidence on performance.\n\n# Method\n## C Interventions, Prompts, and Trace Evaluation\nThis section outlines the experimental design, including the domain task questions, prompt conditions, and input formatting instructions across five core experiments.\n\n### C.1 Domain Task Questions\nFor every experimental trial, the model receives visual video input paired with a zero-shot domain task question requesting the total count of target events. The specific benchmark task questions are as follows:\n\n- **Bounce Ball Domain:** "How many times did the ball contact the walls?"\n- **Blinking Domain:** "How many times did the object blink?"\n- **State Machine Domain:** "How many state transitions occurred in the video?"\n\n### C.2 Experiment 1: Full N × F Matrix Boundary Sweep\n#### Motivation &#x26; Protocol\nExperiment 1 establishes the baseline operational capability boundary \\( x^* \\) across Event Count \\( N \\in [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12] \\) and Event Frequency \\( F \\in [0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0, 3.5, 4.0] \\) Hz. All trials in Experiment 1 use the structured_trace prompt baseline on native video inputs.\n\n### C.3 Experiment 2: Frame Sampling Density Interventions\n#### Motivation &#x26; Protocol\nExperiment 2 tests whether performance breakdown at high frequencies \\( (F \\geq 2.0 \\text{ Hz}) \\) stems from temporal perceptual sampling limits or downstream temporal reasoning limits. It evaluates seven sampling densities (native_video, 1, 2, 4, 8, 10, 16 FPS) paired with the structured trace prompt.\n\n### C.4 Experiment 3: Oracle Keyframe Evidence Interventions\n#### Motivation &#x26; Protocol\nExperiment 3 isolates video frame search from temporal tallying by supplying perfect oracle visual keyframes. For each ground-truth event at \\( t_i \\), frame triplets at \\( (t_i - 0.1s, t_i, t_i + 0.1s) \\) are extracted and provided directly to the model.\n\n# Results\n## B.5 Operational Boundary Robustness\nWe use \\( \\tau = 0.80 \\) as an operational reliability target. A cell is considered reliable when at least eight of ten renderings are correct. The count boundary is defined as the largest tested positive count for which that count and every smaller tested positive count meet the target.\n\n## Experiment 1: Structured Trace Baseline Prompt\nThe results indicate that keyframe improvement is significantly larger than the corresponding sampling improvement, highlighting the importance of reliable keyframe extraction in event counting tasks.\n\n## Experiment 2: Frame Sampling Density\nThe analysis of different sampling densities reveals that performance varies significantly, with lower frame rates leading to increased errors in event counting.\n\n## Experiment 3: Oracle Keyframe Evidence\nThe provision of oracle keyframes resulted in improved accuracy in event counting, demonstrating the effectiveness of targeted visual information in enhancing model performance.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis research establishes a foundational understanding of the operational boundaries of event counting models in video data. The findings underscore the critical role of sampling density and keyframe evidence in optimizing performance, paving the way for future advancements in video analysis technologies.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the impact of various prompting structures and reasoning formats on the performance of video analytics systems. We conduct a series of experiments to evaluate the effectiveness of different prompt conditions in accurately identifying and reasoning about visual events in video data.\n\n# Introduction\nThe ability to accurately analyze and interpret video content is crucial in many applications, including surveillance, sports analytics, and automated content moderation. This research aims to explore how different prompting strategies can enhance the performance of video analytics systems, particularly in terms of accuracy and reasoning capabilities.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted a series of experiments, each designed to assess the performance of video analytics systems under different prompting conditions. The experiments included:\n\n- **Experiment 4: Prompting &#x26; Reasoning Mode**: This experiment evaluates the impact of five distinct prompt conditions on the system's ability to analyze video content.\n- **Experiment 4.1: Direct Answer Condition**: Participants were instructed to provide a final answer based on their observations of the video.\n- **Experiment 4.2: Structured Trace Condition**: Participants were required to provide a step-by-step reasoning trace of their observations.\n- **Experiment 4.3: Multi-Turn Verification Condition**: This condition involved a two-step process where participants first listed detected events and then verified their accuracy.\n- **Experiment 4.4: Thinking / Chain-of-Thought Condition**: Participants were asked to perform a deep analysis of the video, counting events frame-by-frame.\n\n# Results\nThe results of Experiment 4 are summarized in Table 4, which presents the accuracy of different conditions in terms of correct runs, total runs, and confidence intervals.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|l|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{Condition} &#x26; \\text{Correct Runs} &#x26; \\text{Total Runs} &#x26; \\text{Accuracy (\\%)} &#x26; \\text{Lower Interval (\\%)} &#x26; \\text{Upper Interval (\\%)} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{Gemini native-video baseline} &#x26; 143 &#x26; 730 &#x26; 19.6 &#x26; 16.9 &#x26; 22.6 \\\\\n\\text{Gemini 4-FPS sampling} &#x26; 214 &#x26; 730 &#x26; 29.3 &#x26; 26.1 &#x26; 32.7 \\\\\n\\text{Gemini event-centered keyframes} &#x26; 501 &#x26; 730 &#x26; 68.6 &#x26; 65.2 &#x26; 71.9 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n*Table 4: Gemini seed-level variation for Bounce Ball interventions. Each condition has 730 trials. Intervals are two-sided 95% Wilson score intervals for final-answer exact match.*\n\n# Discussion\nThe findings indicate that the choice of prompting condition significantly affects the accuracy of video analytics systems. The event-centered keyframes condition yielded the highest accuracy, suggesting that structured prompts that focus on specific events may enhance the system's performance.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis research highlights the importance of prompt structures in video analytics. Future work should explore additional prompting strategies and their implications for real-world applications, particularly in complex video environments.\n\n\n\n# Real-World Chain-of-Thought Prompt (cot)\n\nThis is an upper-bound visual-access probe because one frame group is provided for every true event. It removes the need to search the full video for event locations while preserving the requirement to retain and aggregate event evidence.\n\n**How many times does the person perform the repetitive exercise in this video?** First, describe what exercise is being performed. Then, enumerate each individual repetition you observe, noting approximately when each one occurs. Finally, provide your total count. Put the final answer in \\(\\boxed{}\\).\n\n# Real-World Direct Answer Prompt (direct)\n\n**Count the number of exercise repetitions in this video.** Reply with only a number.\n\n# C.8 Output Parsing and Evaluation\n\nWe parse the final integer from each response, treating an unparseable answer as incorrect. Trace extraction is described in Appendix C.15. Briefly, only explicit timestamps in the visible response are treated as reported events; ordinal descriptions and final counts alone do not yield timestamps. Consequently, the trace metrics quantify the evidence externalized by each prompting condition. Direct-answer outputs are scored for exact match, and any trace score is based solely on timestamps voluntarily included in the response.\n\n# C.9 Cross-Model Prompting Results\n\nTable 5 places all available Qwen3-VL-8B and InternVL3.5-8B prompt conditions beside the corresponding Gemini reference conditions. The render sets differ slightly but share the Bounce Ball task and parameterization. Gemini’s prompt variants have similar final-answer accuracy but substantially different reported-evidence profiles. The available Qwen conditions exhibit a related separation. Multi-turn produces the highest final-answer exact match (13.5%) and trace F₁ (13.4%) among the listed Qwen variants, whereas Direct and CoT exhibit substantial over-reporting and low precision. InternVL produces nonzero final answers in every condition but no extractable timestamps. These results distinguish the evidence expressed under each prompt from any unobserved internal reasoning process. In particular, the table shows that changing the requested reasoning format can change the visible trace substantially without establishing a corresponding change in temporal capability.\n\n# C.10 Frame-Sampling Density Sweeps\n\nAll visual-access interventions evaluate Bounce Ball, Blinking, and State Machine visual tasks using the same rendered videos and seeds as the native-video baseline. The sampling sweep changes only the density of supplied frames. The main paper reports the corresponding Gemini results. This appendix provides the complete cross-domain Gemini and open-model visual-access evidence below.\n\n# C.11 Event-Centered Keyframe Extraction\n\nFor each true event at time \\(t_i\\), the keyframe condition provides the frame triplet\n\n\\[\nK_i = \\{I(t_i - 0.1 \\text{ s}), I(t_i), I(t_i + 0.1 \\text{ s})\\}.\n\\]\n\n# C.12 Complete Gemini Intervention Analyses\n\nFigures 6, 7, and 8 present the 3-panel intervention analysis across visual sampling densities, keyframe evidence, and prompting formats for all three benchmark domains. Full cell-by-cell 12-panel capability surface heatmaps for Bounce Ball (Figure 13), Blinking (Figure 14), and State Machine (Figure 15) are detailed in the heatmap section preceding the qualitative examples. Denser regular sampling improves a limited low-count portion of the surface, whereas event-centered keyframes extend accuracy on discrete physical contact domains (Bounce Ball exact match increases from 19.5% baseline up to 68.6%). However, on the State Machine domain, oracle keyframe evidence causes a severe performance collapse for Gemini 3.6 Flash (exact match drops from 36.8% baseline and 40.7% under 4 FPS down to 6.3%). Because state transitions represent categorical mode shifts requiring continuous temporal context to verify state persistence, stripping non-event frames removes the background state history necessary for sequential state tracking.\n\n# C.13 Open-Model Keyframe and Sampling Interventions\n\nOn the State Machine domain, supplying event-centered keyframe evidence substantially improves open-model exact match. For Qwen3-VL-8B, exact match increases from 26.6% under native video (800 trials) to 69.4% under event-centered keyframes (720 trials), a gain of +42.8 percentage points. For InternVL3.5-8B, keyframe triplets improve exact match from 26.2% to 32.1% (+5.9 points).\n\n# C.14 GPT-5.6 Sol Frame-Sequence Interventions &#x26; API Limitations\n\nUnlike native video models, GPT-5.6 Sol does not accept raw container video files directly. Instead, videos are manually sampled into ordered sequences of image frames before being passed to the API.\n\n**API Frame Ceiling &#x26; Token Context Limitations.** During multi-density sampling sweeps, GPT-5.6 Sol encountered strict API payload constraints. Specifically, the deployment environment enforces a maximum limit of 50 images per request (BadRequestError: Too many images in request: 51, maximum allowed: 50). Consequently, 24-second videos sampled at higher densities (4 FPS = 96 frames, 8 FPS = 192 frames, 10 FPS = 240 frames, 16 FPS = 384 frames) failed due to payload size and token context window limits. Evaluation was successfully completed for 1 FPS (24 frames ≤ 50), 2 FPS (48 frames ≤ 50), Oracle Keyframe Evidence (≈ 3N frames), and 2-FPS prompting interventions (direct, structured_trace, multi_turn_verification, thinking, role_prompting). Table 6 provides the...\n\n\n\n# Abstract\n[Insert abstract text here.]\n\n# Introduction\n[Insert introduction text here.]\n\n# Method\n[Insert method text here.]\n\n# Results\nThe results of the interventions are illustrated in Figures 6, 7, and 8.\n\n**Figure 6** shows the performance of the Bounce Ball interventions, with the final-answer exact match averaged over frequencies across visual sampling densities, keyframe evidence, and prompting strategies.\n\n**Figure 7** presents the Blinking interventions, similarly displaying the final-answer exact match averaged over frequencies across visual sampling densities, keyframe evidence, and prompting strategies.\n\n**Figure 8** depicts the State Machine interventions, again showing the final-answer exact match averaged over frequencies across visual sampling densities, keyframe evidence, and prompting strategies.\n\n# Discussion\n[Insert discussion text here.]\n\n# Conclusion\n[Insert conclusion text here.]\n\n# References\n[Insert references here.]\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a comprehensive evaluation of various models in the context of task-matched prompting and trace analysis on the Bounce Ball task. We analyze the performance of different prompting strategies across multiple models, focusing on exact match (EM), visual object recognition (VOR), recall (R), and other relevant metrics.\n\n# Introduction\nThe study investigates the effectiveness of different prompting techniques in enhancing model performance on temporal tasks. We compare models such as Gemini 3.6 Flash, Qwen3-VL-8B, and InternVL3.5-8B, assessing their capabilities in recognizing and responding to events in a structured manner.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted experiments using a series of prompts across different models, evaluating their performance based on several metrics. The models were subjected to 730 trials per domain, and trace metrics were calculated using frequency-relative timestamp matching, focusing on explicit, parser-recognized timestamps.\n\n# Results\nThe results are summarized in Table 5, which presents the performance metrics for each model under various prompting strategies. The metrics include exact match (EM), visual object recognition precision (VOR P), recall (R), trace F₁ score, accuracy (ACR), and recall for frames (RFR).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|l|l|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{Model} &#x26; \\text{Prompt} &#x26; \\text{EM (\\%)} &#x26; \\text{VOR P (\\%)} &#x26; \\text{R (\\%)} &#x26; \\text{Trace F₁ (\\%)} &#x26; \\text{ACR (\\%)} &#x26; \\text{RFR (\\%)} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{Gemini 3.6 Flash} &#x26; \\text{Direct} &#x26; 20.4 &#x26; 1.16 &#x26; 45.6 &#x26; 40.2 &#x26; 38.5 &#x26; 7.5 \\\\\n\\text{Gemini 3.6 Flash} &#x26; \\text{Structured trace} &#x26; 19.6 &#x26; 0.71 &#x26; 59.2 &#x26; 31.5 &#x26; 36.6 &#x26; 3.9 \\\\\n\\text{Gemini 3.6 Flash} &#x26; \\text{Multi-turn} &#x26; 20.3 &#x26; 0.40 &#x26; 15.9 &#x26; 9.5 &#x26; 10.1 &#x26; 18.2 \\\\\n\\text{Gemini 3.6 Flash} &#x26; \\text{Thinking / CoT} &#x26; 19.5 &#x26; 2.13 &#x26; 33.8 &#x26; 48.5 &#x26; 36.9 &#x26; 14.3 \\\\\n\\text{Gemini 3.6 Flash} &#x26; \\text{Role prompt} &#x26; 19.3 &#x26; 1.52 &#x26; 37.2 &#x26; 44.9 &#x26; 37.6 &#x26; 10.8 \\\\\n\\text{Qwen3-VL-8B} &#x26; \\text{Direct} &#x26; 12.4 &#x26; 28.76 &#x26; 16.8 &#x26; 17.5 &#x26; 13.0 &#x26; 4.9 \\\\\n\\text{Qwen3-VL-8B} &#x26; \\text{CoT} &#x26; 8.2 &#x26; 18.69 &#x26; 11.1 &#x26; 30.5 &#x26; 11.6 &#x26; 2.6 \\\\\n\\text{Qwen3-VL-8B} &#x26; \\text{Multi-turn} &#x26; 13.5 &#x26; 0.26 &#x26; 23.8 &#x26; 11.5 &#x26; 13.4 &#x26; 4.7 \\\\\n\\text{Qwen3-VL-8B-Thinking CoT} &#x26; &#x26; 10.2 &#x26; 0.17 &#x26; 10.9 &#x26; 4.3 &#x26; 5.4 &#x26; 5.6 \\\\\n\\text{InternVL3.5-8B} &#x26; \\text{Direct} &#x26; 9.0 &#x26; 0.00 &#x26; 0.0 &#x26; 0.0 &#x26; 0.0 &#x26; 9.9 \\\\\n\\text{InternVL3.5-8B} &#x26; \\text{CoT} &#x26; 9.8 &#x26; 0.00 &#x26; 0.0 &#x26; 0.0 &#x26; 0.0 &#x26; 9.0 \\\\\n\\text{InternVL3.5-8B} &#x26; \\text{Multi-turn} &#x26; 9.8 &#x26; 0.00 &#x26; 0.0 &#x26; 0.0 &#x26; 0.0 &#x26; 10.7 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nTable 5: Task-matched cross-model prompting and trace summary on Bounce Ball. All model profiles evaluate 730 trials per domain. All trace metrics use frequency-relative timestamp matching and score only explicit, parser-recognized timestamps.\n\n# Discussion\nThe analysis reveals significant differences in model performance based on the prompting strategy employed. For instance, the Gemini 3.6 Flash model demonstrated superior performance across most metrics when using direct prompts compared to other strategies. In contrast, the Qwen3-VL-8B model showed lower overall performance, particularly with the CoT prompting strategy.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study highlights the importance of prompting strategies in enhancing model performance on temporal tasks. Future work should explore additional prompting techniques and their impact on model capabilities in various domains.\n\n# Figures\nFigure 9 illustrates the performance of Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct under denser frame sampling (10 FPS), indicating that exact-match capability surfaces across temporal counting tasks. Figure 10 presents the oracle keyframe evidence intervention analysis for Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct, showcasing the exact-match accuracy across event count and frequency when models are supplied with oracle event-centered keyframe triplets versus baseline video sampling.\n\nThe keyframe breakdown on state transitions versus flash events indicates that supplying oracle keyframe triplets significantly enhances exact match performance for certain tasks, while continuous frame sampling is more effective for others. This discrepancy underscores the need for tailored approaches depending on the task characteristics.\n\n# Acknowledgments\nWe acknowledge the contributions of all researchers involved in this study and the support from our institutions.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a comprehensive evaluation of the GPT-5.6 Sol model across various intervention protocols, focusing on its performance in video understanding tasks. We analyze the model's precision, recall, and trace metrics, providing insights into the computational costs associated with different sampling densities and prompting formats.\n\n# Introduction\nThe advent of advanced video-language models (VLMs) has prompted a need for rigorous evaluation frameworks that can assess their capabilities in understanding and processing video content. This study aims to systematically evaluate the performance of the GPT-5.6 Sol model under different intervention protocols, examining how variations in frame sampling rates and prompting strategies affect the model's accuracy and efficiency.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted a series of experiments using the GPT-5.6 Sol model, applying various intervention protocols to assess its performance in video understanding tasks. The evaluation metrics included event matching, precision, recall, trace F₁, and the Visual Observation Ratio (VOR). The models were tested across different frame sampling rates, ranging from 1 FPS to 16 FPS, to analyze the impact of visual input density on performance.\n\n## Table 6: GPT-5.6 Sol intervention trace evaluation summary\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|l|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{Model / Intervention Protocol} &#x26; \\text{EM (\\%)} &#x26; \\text{VOR P (\\%)} &#x26; \\text{R (\\%)} &#x26; \\text{Trace F₁ (\\%)} &#x26; \\text{ACR (\\%)} &#x26; \\text{RFR (\\%)} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{GPT-5.6 Sol (1 FPS Structured Trace)} &#x26; 21.9 &#x26; 0.88 &#x26; 87.5 &#x26; 62.2 &#x26; 63.9 &#x26; 15.8 &#x26; 29.7 \\\\\n\\text{GPT-5.6 Sol (2 FPS Structured Trace)} &#x26; 34.9 &#x26; 0.86 &#x26; 86.4 &#x26; 60.4 &#x26; 60.7 &#x26; 21.9 &#x26; 21.4 \\\\\n\\text{GPT-5.6 Sol (2 FPS Direct Answer)} &#x26; 35.5 &#x26; 0.01 &#x26; 1.4 &#x26; 1.4 &#x26; 1.4 &#x26; 34.1 &#x26; 0.0 \\\\\n\\text{GPT-5.6 Sol (2 FPS Multi-Turn)} &#x26; 35.1 &#x26; 0.39 &#x26; 66.1 &#x26; 38.6 &#x26; 44.6 &#x26; 12.6 &#x26; 5.7 \\\\\n\\text{GPT-5.6 Sol (2 FPS Thinking / CoT)} &#x26; 29.9 &#x26; 0.05 &#x26; 12.8 &#x26; 4.5 &#x26; 5.6 &#x26; 27.3 &#x26; 0.4 \\\\\n\\text{GPT-5.6 Sol (2 FPS Role Prompt)} &#x26; 39.5 &#x26; 0.48 &#x26; 58.6 &#x26; 39.9 &#x26; 42.4 &#x26; 25.8 &#x26; 13.5 \\\\\n\\text{GPT-5.6 Sol (Oracle Keyframes)} &#x26; 42.5 &#x26; 1.18 &#x26; 81.2 &#x26; 79.6 &#x26; 71.1 &#x26; 20.4 &#x26; 34.9 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate that increasing the frame sampling rates from 1 FPS to 16 FPS significantly impacts the model's performance metrics. Notably, the Oracle Keyframes intervention yielded the highest trace F₁ score of 79.6%, while the Direct Answer format performed poorly with a trace F₁ of only 1.4%. The analysis also revealed that higher sampling densities lead to increased computational costs without a corresponding improvement in event tracking accuracy.\n\n## C.16 Aggregate Trace Error Summary\nThe aggregate trace error summary highlights the discrepancies in event identification across different models. The data suggests that while some models, such as Gemini, achieved a higher number of matched events, others struggled with unmatched timestamp entries. This inconsistency underscores the challenges in temporal tracking and event bookkeeping within VLMs.\n\n# Discussion\nThe findings from this study emphasize the importance of selecting appropriate intervention protocols and sampling rates when evaluating video-language models. The results suggest that while higher frame rates can enhance certain performance metrics, they also incur significant computational costs and do not necessarily resolve underlying architectural limitations in temporal tracking.\n\n# Conclusion\nIn conclusion, this research provides valuable insights into the performance of the GPT-5.6 Sol model across various intervention protocols. The evaluation metrics and computational cost analysis presented herein can inform future studies and the development of more effective video-language models. Further research is needed to explore alternative strategies for improving event tracking and reducing computational overhead in VLMs.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a comprehensive analysis of temporal event bookkeeping in video models, focusing on the identification and classification of various error types that arise during the reporting of events. We introduce a taxonomy of trace errors, evaluate model performance across different configurations, and discuss the implications of our findings for future research in multimodal reasoning.\n\n# Introduction\nRecent advancements in video understanding have highlighted the importance of accurately tracking and reporting events over time. This study aims to extend existing methodologies by introducing a detailed taxonomy of trace errors that can occur in temporal event reporting. By analyzing the performance of various models, we seek to identify common pitfalls and areas for improvement.\n\n# Method\nWe employed a controlled experimental setup to isolate compositional and physical reasoning factors, drawing on previous work in the field (Johnson et al. 2017; Chow et al. 2025; Xiang et al. 2025; Qiu et al. 2025). Our approach involved using a renderer to produce an event schedule for each video, allowing us to align reported event sequences with ground-truth data. This methodology enables a more nuanced evaluation of model performance, particularly in identifying missed or hallucinated events.\n\n# Results\nThe results of our analysis are summarized in Tables 7 and 8, which present a detailed breakdown of error types across different models.\n\n## Table 7: Task-general trace-error taxonomy\n| Error type                     | Definition                                                                                       |\n|-------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Correct match                 | A reported event aligns one-to-one with a true event within the timestamp tolerance and has the correct available event attribute. |\n| Missed event                  | A true event has no aligned reported event.                                                    |\n| Merged or collapsed events     | The visible response explicitly combines two or more true events into one report.               |\n| Hallucinated event            | A reported event has no aligned true event.                                                    |\n| Temporally displaced event     | A report corresponds to the appropriate event in the sequence but falls outside the timestamp tolerance. |\n| Misidentified event           | A timestamp aligns, but the reported event attribute is incorrect, such as the contacted wall, blink phase, or resulting state. |\n| Wrong accumulation             | The reported event sequence is sufficiently faithful with trace \\( F_1 \\geq 0.80 \\), but the final count is incorrect. |\n\n## Table 8: Aggregate trace-error counts by model\n| Error type                     | Gemini | Qwen 3.6 | Qwen Flash 3-VL-8B | Qwen 3-VL-32B | Qwen 3-VL-235B | InternVL 3.5-8B | InternVL 3.5-30B-A3B | InternVL 3.5-38B |\n|-------------------------------|--------|----------|---------------------|---------------|----------------|------------------|---------------------|------------------|\n| True events                   | 12,240 | 12,223   | 12,223              | 12,223        | 12,223         | 12,223           | 12,223              | 12,223           |\n| Matched events                | 3,907  | 2,458    | 1,678               | 1,130         | 0              | 0                | 0                   | 0                |\n| Missed events                 | 8,333  | 9,765    | 10,545              | 11,093        | 12,223         | 12,223           | 12,223              | 12,223           |\n| Merged events                 | 0      | 0        | 0                   | 0             | 0              | 0                | 0                   | 0                |\n| Hallucinated events           | 2,096  | 88,506   | 2,348               | 931           | 0              | 0                | 0                   | 0                |\n| Displaced events              | 448    | 245      | 402                 | 142           | 0              | 0                | 0                   | 0                |\n| Misidentified events          | 1,556  | 737      | 518                 | 200           | 0              | 0                | 0                   | 0                |\n| Wrong accumulation             | 50     | 34       | 34                  | 23            | 0              | 0                | 0                   | 0                |\n| Videos                        | 2,160  | 2,160    | 2,160               | 2,160         | 2,160          | 2,160            | 2,160              | 2,160           |\n\n# Discussion\nOur findings indicate that while some models perform well in terms of matched events, they struggle with missed and hallucinated events, particularly in more complex scenarios. The introduction of a renderer-generated temporal event schedule has proven to be a valuable tool for evaluating model performance, allowing for a more precise identification of errors.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study contributes to the understanding of temporal event reporting in video models by providing a detailed taxonomy of trace errors and evaluating model performance across various configurations. Future work should focus on addressing the identified weaknesses and improving the robustness of models in handling complex event sequences.\n\n# References\n- Johnson et al. 2017\n- Chow et al. 2025\n- Xiang et al. 2025\n- Qiu et al. 2025\n- Wang et al. 2022\n- Zelikman et al. 2022\n- Shojaee et al. 2025\n- Sun et al. 2025\n- Hao et al. 2025\n- Agarwal et al. 2025\n- Tong et al. 2025\n- Yuan et al. 2025\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a comprehensive evaluation of the Gemini 3.6 Flash model's performance across various experimental protocols, focusing on its ability to accurately detect and report event timestamps in synthetic benchmark domains. We analyze the model's sensitivity to timestamp tolerance, the cost of evaluation, and the qualitative aspects of its predictions.\n\n# Introduction\nThe ability to accurately detect and report event timestamps is crucial for various applications in computer vision and temporal reasoning. This study evaluates the Gemini 3.6 Flash model's performance in detecting events across different scenarios, examining its strengths and weaknesses in timestamp accuracy and event reporting.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted a series of experiments to assess the model's performance under varying conditions. The experiments included a baseline matrix sweep, frame density interventions, oracle keyframe evidence interventions, prompting strategies, and real-world transfer evaluations. Each experiment was designed to test specific aspects of the model's capabilities.\n\n## Experiment Protocols\nThe following table summarizes the trials, input/output token counts, and costs associated with each experiment:\n\n| Experiment / Protocol                                      | Trials | Input Tokens     | Output Tokens    | Total Tokens      | Cost ($USD) |\n|----------------------------------------------------------|--------|------------------|------------------|-------------------|-------------|\n| Exp 1: Baseline N × F Matrix Sweep                       | 2,203  | 3,659,556        | 1,803,571        | 5,463,127         | $19.02      |\n| Exp 2: Frame Density Interventions (1–16 FPS)           | 20,102 | 2,438,029,486    | 15,172,075       | 2,453,201,561     | $3,426.86   |\n| Exp 3: Oracle Keyframe Evidence Interventions            | 3,449  | 45,757,799       | 3,050,018        | 48,807,817        | $89.47      |\n| Exp 4: Prompting Strategies &#x26; Thinking Modes             | 11,714 | 18,905,824       | 10,567,098       | 29,472,922        | $107.61     |\n| Real-World Transfer (RepCount / TransRAC)               | 456    | 8,014,360        | 906,931          | 8,921,291         | $18.82      |\n| **Total Benchmark Evaluation**                            | 37,924 | 2,514,367,025    | 31,499,693       | 2,545,866,718     | $3,661.78   |\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate varying levels of performance across different matching tolerance strategies. The following table summarizes the model's performance metrics:\n\n| Matching Tolerance Strategy | Acc (%) | VOR Precision (%) | Recall (%) | Trace F₁ (%) | ACR (%) |\n|-----------------------------|---------|-------------------|------------|---------------|---------|\n| Fixed δ = 1.0 s             | 21.1    | 0.68              | 75.6       | 41.2          | 1.9     |\n| Fixed δ = 0.5 s             | 21.1    | 0.68              | 67.8       | 35.2          | 6.4     |\n| Fixed δ = 0.25 s            | 21.1    | 0.68              | 49.7       | 21.7          | 14.2    |\n| Rate-Relative δ = 1         | 21.1    | 0.68              | 58.0       | 31.2          | 5.7     |\n\nTable 9 illustrates the trace-metric sensitivity to timestamp tolerance for the Gemini 3.6 Flash baseline. The final EM averages the full grid; VOR, precision, recall, trace F₁, and ACR average positive-event trials only. The rate-relative tolerance \\( \\delta(F) = \\frac{1}{2F} \\) bounds matching within each event’s unique inter-event half-period.\n\n# Discussion\n## F.1 Case-Selection Protocol\nQualitative cases were sampled from Gemini 3.6 Flash structured-trace predictions across all three synthetic benchmark domains (bounce_ball, blinking, and state_machine). For every sample card, we captured event frame strips, aligned executable vs. reported timestamps, and classified samples into mutually exclusive failure categories.\n\n## F.2 Faithful Event Recovery\nFigures 18 and 19 present cases where the model achieves perfect trace alignment, demonstrating its capability in low-count, low-frequency operating regions.\n\n## F.3 Missed Events\nFigures 20 and 21 illustrate perception failures under high temporal load, where the model under-reports sequences, leading to severe recall degradation.\n\n## F.4 Hallucinated Events\nFigures 22 and 23 show over-reporting failures typical of low-load operating regions, where the model generates spurious timestamps.\n\n## F.5 Wrong Accumulation\nFigures 24 and 25 demonstrate the Reasoning Failure Ratio (RFR), highlighting the model's failure at the final aggregation step.\n\n## F.6 Accidental Correctness\nFigures 26 and 27 present the Accidental Correctness Ratio (ACR), where the model's final integer prediction matches ground truth despite degraded event traces.\n\n## F.7 Temporally Displaced Events\nFigures 28 and 29 illustrate temporal displacement, where the model detects visual transitions but assigns boundary timestamps that drift beyond the tolerance window.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe evaluation of the Gemini 3.6 Flash model reveals significant insights into its performance across various experimental conditions. While the model demonstrates strengths in certain areas, it also exhibits notable weaknesses, particularly in high-load scenarios and final aggregation steps. Future work should focus on addressing these limitations to enhance the model's temporal reasoning capabilities.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a comprehensive analysis of the computational costs associated with processing video inputs across various frame sampling densities and visual domains. We investigate the relationship between frame density, input and output token generation, and the resulting costs in USD. Our findings highlight the significant impact of frame sampling rates on processing costs and token usage, providing insights into the efficiency of video processing systems.\n\n# Introduction\nThe increasing demand for video processing in various applications necessitates an understanding of the computational costs involved. This study aims to quantify the costs associated with different frame sampling densities and to analyze the token generation across various visual domains. By examining these factors, we can better understand the trade-offs involved in video processing and inform future research and development in this area.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted experiments to evaluate the costs associated with different frame sampling densities. The trials involved processing videos at various frames per second (FPS) rates, ranging from native video to 16 FPS. We recorded the number of input tokens, output tokens, and the associated costs in USD for each trial. Additionally, we analyzed the computational allocation across different visual domains, including synthetic video tasks and real-world transfer datasets.\n\n# Results\nThe results of our experiments are summarized in Tables 11 and 12.\n\n## Table 11: Cost breakdown by frame sampling density across all tasks (Experiment 2)\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{Frame Density} &#x26; \\text{Trials} &#x26; \\text{Input Tokens} &#x26; \\text{Output Tokens} &#x26; \\text{Cost (\\$USD)} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{Native Video} &#x26; 2,203 &#x26; 3,659,556 &#x26; 1,803,571 &#x26; 19.02 \\\\\n1 \\text{ FPS} &#x26; 2,862 &#x26; 58,130,830 &#x26; 2,004,410 &#x26; 95.80 \\\\\n2 \\text{ FPS} &#x26; 2,933 &#x26; 115,870,930 &#x26; 2,206,109 &#x26; 167.95 \\\\\n4 \\text{ FPS} &#x26; 3,428 &#x26; 225,535,430 &#x26; 2,479,566 &#x26; 313.27 \\\\\n8 \\text{ FPS} &#x26; 3,453 &#x26; 452,188,230 &#x26; 2,633,080 &#x26; 662.41 \\\\\n10 \\text{ FPS} &#x26; 3,554 &#x26; 589,062,945 &#x26; 2,692,969 &#x26; 839.01 \\\\\n16 \\text{ FPS} &#x26; 3,872 &#x26; 997,241,121 &#x26; 3,155,941 &#x26; 1,348.43 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThis table demonstrates how frame sampling rate converts video inputs into large image-patch token sequences, scaling input processing costs.\n\n## Table 12: Evaluation cost and token usage breakdown by visual domain\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{Visual Domain / Task} &#x26; \\text{Trials} &#x26; \\text{Input Tokens} &#x26; \\text{Output Tokens} &#x26; \\text{Cost (\\$USD)} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{state\\_machine} &#x26; 13,141 &#x26; 910,900,516 &#x26; 9,798,474 &#x26; 1,249.46 \\\\\n\\text{bounce\\_ball} &#x26; 10,363 &#x26; 778,500,899 &#x26; 10,729,970 &#x26; 1,247.40 \\\\\n\\text{blinking} &#x26; 13,964 &#x26; 816,951,250 &#x26; 10,064,318 &#x26; 1,146.10 \\\\\n\\text{repcount (Real-World)} &#x26; 456 &#x26; 8,014,360 &#x26; 906,931 &#x26; 18.82 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{Total} &#x26; 37,924 &#x26; 2,514,367,025 &#x26; 31,499,693 &#x26; 3,661.78 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThis table summarizes the computational allocation across synthetic video domains and real-world transfer datasets.\n\n# Discussion\nThe analysis of the data reveals that as the frame sampling density increases, both the input and output tokens generated also increase significantly, leading to higher processing costs. The results indicate that optimizing frame rates can lead to substantial cost savings in video processing tasks.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study provides valuable insights into the costs associated with video processing across different frame sampling densities and visual domains. The findings underscore the importance of considering both computational efficiency and cost-effectiveness in the design of video processing systems. Future work should focus on developing strategies to optimize these parameters further.\n\n# Figure\nFigure 11 illustrates the capability heatmaps of the Qwen3-VL family, showcasing the final-answer exact match across event count and frequency for State Machine transitions. The panels compare the performance of Qwen3-VL-8B, Qwen3-VL-32B, and Qwen3-VL-235B.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents an analysis of the InternVL3.5 family capabilities through the use of heatmaps, focusing on the final-answer exact match across various event counts and frequencies. The study evaluates the performance of the model in different scenarios, specifically for tasks related to State Machine, Blinking, and Bounce Ball.\n\n# Introduction\nThe advancement of visual-language models has led to significant improvements in various applications, including event recognition and interaction. This research aims to explore the capabilities of the InternVL3.5 family by analyzing its performance across different tasks and conditions.\n\n# Method\nTo evaluate the performance of the InternVL3.5 family, we generated heatmaps that illustrate the final-answer exact match rates across varying event counts and frequencies. The tasks selected for this analysis include State Machine, Blinking, and Bounce Ball. The methodology involved systematic testing of the model under controlled conditions to ensure reliable results.\n\n# Results\nThe results are presented in Figure 12, which displays the capability heatmaps for the InternVL3.5 family. The heatmaps illustrate the final-answer exact match across event count and frequency for the selected tasks. Each column in the figure compares the evaluated scales of the InternVL3.5 model, providing insights into its performance across different scenarios.\n\n# Discussion\nThe findings from the heatmaps indicate that the InternVL3.5 family exhibits varying levels of performance depending on the task and the conditions under which it is evaluated. Further analysis is required to understand the underlying factors contributing to these performance differences.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study highlights the capabilities of the InternVL3.5 family through a detailed analysis of its performance across different tasks. The heatmaps serve as a valuable tool for visualizing the model's strengths and weaknesses, paving the way for future research and improvements in visual-language models.\n\n# References\n[References would be listed here, following the appropriate academic format.]\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a comprehensive analysis of the Gemini visual-access intervention surfaces applied to the Bounce Ball task. We evaluate the final-answer exact-match accuracy across various conditions, including native video, supplied frame-sampling densities, and event-centered keyframes.\n\n# Introduction\nThe increasing complexity of visual data necessitates advanced methods for effective interpretation and interaction. The Gemini visual-access intervention aims to enhance user engagement and accuracy in visual tasks. This study focuses on the Bounce Ball task, assessing the effectiveness of different visual-access strategies.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted experiments using native video and various frame-sampling densities. The event-centered keyframes were selected based on their relevance to the task. The final-answer exact-match accuracy was measured to evaluate the performance of each intervention surface.\n\n# Results\nFigure 13 illustrates the complete Gemini visual-access intervention surfaces on the Bounce Ball task. The results indicate varying levels of accuracy depending on the type of visual-access intervention employed. The analysis reveals that native video provided the highest exact-match accuracy, followed by specific frame-sampling densities and event-centered keyframes.\n\n# Discussion\nThe findings suggest that the choice of visual-access intervention significantly impacts user performance in visual tasks. Future research should explore additional variables that may influence accuracy and user experience.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe Gemini visual-access intervention surfaces demonstrate potential for improving accuracy in visual tasks. Further investigation is warranted to optimize these interventions for broader applications.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a comprehensive analysis of the Gemini visual-access intervention surfaces on Blinking, focusing on the final-answer exact-match accuracy across various conditions. We explore the impact of native video, supplied frame-sampling densities, and event-centered keyframes on the performance of the intervention.\n\n# Introduction\nThe advent of advanced visual-access technologies has transformed the way users interact with video content. This study investigates the effectiveness of the Gemini visual-access intervention, particularly in the context of Blinking, a platform that facilitates enhanced video engagement. We aim to quantify the accuracy of responses generated by the intervention under different sampling conditions.\n\n# Method\nTo evaluate the performance of the Gemini intervention, we conducted a series of experiments that involved varying the frame-sampling densities and utilizing event-centered keyframes. The methodology included:\n\n1. **Data Collection**: Native video content was sourced, and various frame-sampling densities were applied.\n2. **Intervention Application**: The Gemini visual-access intervention was implemented on the selected video segments.\n3. **Accuracy Measurement**: Final-answer exact-match accuracy was calculated based on user responses compared to the correct answers.\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate a significant variation in final-answer exact-match accuracy depending on the conditions applied. As illustrated in Figure 14, the performance metrics reveal that native video content yielded the highest accuracy, while variations in frame-sampling densities and the use of event-centered keyframes also influenced outcomes.\n\nFigure 14: Complete Gemini visual-access intervention surfaces on Blinking. Final-answer exact-match accuracy for native video, supplied frame-sampling densities, and event-centered keyframes.\n\n# Discussion\nThe findings suggest that the choice of video format and sampling density plays a crucial role in the effectiveness of visual-access interventions. Future research should explore additional variables that may further enhance user engagement and accuracy in video interactions.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study underscores the importance of optimizing visual-access interventions to improve user experience and accuracy in video content engagement. The Gemini intervention demonstrates promising results, particularly with native video formats, and sets the stage for further exploration in this domain.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a comprehensive analysis of the Gemini visual-access intervention surfaces on State Machine, focusing on the final-answer exact-match accuracy for native video, supplied frame-sampling densities, and event-centered keyframes.\n\n# Introduction\nThe advent of advanced visual-access technologies has transformed the way we interact with video content. This study aims to evaluate the effectiveness of the Gemini intervention in enhancing the accuracy of information retrieval from video sources.\n\n# Method\nTo assess the performance of the Gemini visual-access intervention, we conducted a series of experiments using various frame-sampling densities and event-centered keyframes. The methodology involved:\n\n1. **Data Collection**: Native video content was sourced, and keyframes were identified based on significant events within the video.\n2. **Intervention Application**: The Gemini intervention was applied to the selected video segments.\n3. **Accuracy Measurement**: Final-answer exact-match accuracy was calculated by comparing the retrieved information against a ground truth dataset.\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate a significant improvement in the final-answer exact-match accuracy when utilizing the Gemini intervention. As shown in Figure 15, the accuracy varied with different frame-sampling densities and keyframe selections, demonstrating the intervention's effectiveness across various conditions.\n\n# Discussion\nThe findings suggest that the Gemini visual-access intervention can substantially enhance the retrieval accuracy of information from video content. Future research should explore the scalability of this intervention across diverse video formats and content types.\n\n# Conclusion\nIn conclusion, the Gemini visual-access intervention surfaces on State Machine have proven to be a valuable tool in improving the accuracy of video information retrieval. Further studies are warranted to optimize its application in real-world scenarios.\n\n# References\n[References would be listed here, following the appropriate academic format.]\n\n# Figure\nFigure 15: Complete Gemini visual-access intervention surfaces on State Machine. Final-answer exact-match accuracy for native video, supplied frame-sampling densities, and event-centered keyframes.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\n[Insert abstract text here.]\n\n# Introduction\n[Insert introduction text here.]\n\n# Method\n[Insert method text here.]\n\n# Results\nThe results of our experiments are illustrated in Figures 16 and 17.\n\nFigure 16 shows the capability surfaces of GPT-5.6 on the Blinking task, highlighting the final-answer exact-match accuracy under 1–2 FPS sampling densities, oracle keyframes, and 2-FPS prompting interventions.\n\nSimilarly, Figure 17 presents the capability surfaces of GPT-5.6 on the State Machine task, also detailing the final-answer exact-match accuracy under the same conditions.\n\n# Discussion\n[Insert discussion text here.]\n\n# Conclusion\n[Insert conclusion text here.]\n\n# References\n[Insert references here.]\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents an evaluation of the Gemini 3.6 Flash model's performance in tracking key event timestamps across various scenarios. The model's accuracy is assessed through a series of cases involving object blinking, ball bouncing, and state transitions, with a focus on precision, recall, and overall trace F1 scores.\n\n# Introduction\nThe ability to accurately track events in dynamic environments is crucial for various applications in computer vision and machine learning. This study investigates the performance of the Gemini 3.6 Flash model in detecting and reporting event timestamps in different scenarios, providing insights into its reliability and effectiveness.\n\n# Method\nThe evaluation consists of three distinct cases, each designed to test the model's capabilities in tracking specific events. The ground truth executable traces are compared against the model's reported timestamps to calculate precision, recall, and F1 scores.\n\n## Case 1: Object Blinking\n- **Scene Description**: An object pulsing ON and OFF at a frequency of 1.0 Hz.\n- **Ground Truth Executable Trace**:\n- Executable Timestamps: [00:01]\n- GT Count: 1\n- **Model Output**:\n- Reported Timestamps: [00:01]\n- Trace F1: 100.0%\n\n### Step-by-Step Event Ledger\n1. At 1.37s: Object blinked ON (count=1)\n- 00:01 - Triangle flashes bright blue (Running count: 1)\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]\n\n# Results\nThe model successfully detected all key event timestamps in sequence, yielding a perfect trace F1 score of 100.0% and matching the final answer.\n\n## Case 2: Ball Bouncing\n- **Scene Description**: A ball bouncing between two walls at a frequency of 0.5 Hz.\n- **Ground Truth Executable Trace**:\n- Executable Timestamps: [00:07.61, 00:09.61, 00:11.61, 00:13.61, 00:15.61]\n- GT Count: 5\n- **Model Output**:\n- Reported Timestamps: [00:07, 00:09, 00:11, 00:13, 00:15]\n- Trace F1: 100.0%\n\n### Step-by-Step Event Ledger\n1. At 7.61s: Ball contacted wall_positive (count=1)\n- 00:07 - Ball contacts the right wall (Running count: 1)\n2. At 9.61s: Ball contacted wall_negative (count=2)\n- 00:09 - Ball contacts the left wall (Running count: 2)\n3. At 11.61s: Ball contacted wall_positive (count=3)\n- 00:11 - Ball contacts the right wall (Running count: 3)\n4. At 13.61s: Ball contacted wall_negative (count=4)\n- 00:13 - Ball contacts the left wall (Running count: 4)\n5. At 15.61s: Ball contacted wall_positive (count=5)\n- 00:15 - Ball contacts the right wall (Running count: 5)\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]\n\n# Diagnostic Justification\nGemini accurately tracked all 5 key event timestamps in sequence, yielding a perfect trace F1 of 100.0% and matching the final answer.\n\n## Case 3: State Transitions\n- **Scene Description**: Visual state machine transitions at a frequency of 3.0 Hz.\n- **Ground Truth Executable Trace**:\n- Executable Timestamps: [00:12.32]\n- GT Count: 1\n- **Model Output**:\n- Reported Timestamps: [00:12]\n- Trace F1: 100.0%\n\n### Step-by-Step Event Ledger\n1. At 12.32s: Transitioned from State B to State D (count=1)\n- 00:12 - State transition from State B to State D (Running count: 1)\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]\n\n# Conclusion\nThe Gemini 3.6 Flash model demonstrated exceptional performance in tracking event timestamps across all evaluated cases, achieving a trace F1 score of 100% in each instance. This indicates a high level of accuracy and reliability in event detection, making it a valuable tool for applications requiring precise temporal tracking.\n\n# Figure\nFigure 18: Faithful Event Recovery (Part 1: Cases 1–3): Gemini 3.6 Flash accurately tracks all key event timestamps and running counts on blinking, bounce_ball, and state_machine, achieving 100% Trace F₁ and matching final integer counts.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents an analysis of the performance of the Gemini model in tracking state transitions within a video context. The model's ability to accurately identify key event timestamps and transitions is evaluated, demonstrating a perfect trace F1 score.\n\n# Introduction\nThe ability to track state transitions in dynamic environments is crucial for various applications, including robotics and automated systems. This study focuses on the Gemini model's performance in identifying and tracking state transitions in a video, specifically analyzing its accuracy in detecting event timestamps.\n\n# Method\nThe method employed involves analyzing the state transitions within a video using the Gemini model. The model's predictions are compared against ground truth data to evaluate its performance. The key metrics used for evaluation include trace F1 score, precision, and recall.\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate that the Gemini model successfully tracked all 12 state transitions in the video, yielding a trace F1 score of 100.0%. The model's predictions matched the ground truth executable trace, confirming its accuracy.\n\n## Event Tracking\nThe following timestamps were identified as key event transitions:\n- At 3.58s: Transitioned from State A to State B (count=1)\n- At 4.58s: Transitioned from State B to State A (count=2)\n- At 5.58s: Transitioned from State A to State C (count=3)\n- At 6.58s: Transitioned from State C to State D (count=4)\n- At 7.58s: Transitioned from State D to State B (count=5)\n- At 8.58s: Transitioned from State C to State D (count=6)\n- At 9.58s: Transitioned from State D to State C (count=7)\n- At 10.58s: Transitioned from State C to State B (count=8)\n- At 11.58s: Transitioned from State B to State A (count=9)\n- At 12.58s: Transitioned from State A to State C (count=10)\n- At 13.58s: Transitioned from State C to State A (count=11)\n- At 14.58s: Transitioned from State A to State B (count=12)\n\nThe total number of state transitions detected was 12, as confirmed by the model's output.\n\n# Diagnostic Justification\nThe diagnostic justification for the results indicates that the Gemini model accurately tracked all 12 key event timestamps in sequence, yielding a perfect trace F1 score of 100.0% and matching the final answer.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe findings demonstrate that the Gemini model is highly effective in tracking state transitions in video data, achieving perfect accuracy in this evaluation. Future work may explore the model's performance under varying conditions and with different datasets.\n\n# Figure\nFigure 19: Faithful Event Recovery (Part 2: Cases 4–5): Additional faithful event recovery profiles on state_machine across low-frequency operating conditions (N ≤ 12, F ≤ 1.0Hz).\n\n\n\n# Missed Events in Temporal Event Detection\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper investigates the performance of the Gemini model in detecting temporal events, specifically focusing on its ability to accurately report the number of occurrences of blinking and bouncing events. We present three case studies where the model under-reported the actual event counts due to high-frequency transitions and temporal compression.\n\n## Introduction\nTemporal event detection is crucial in various applications, including video analysis and real-time monitoring systems. Accurate detection of events such as blinking or bouncing is essential for understanding dynamic systems. This study evaluates the performance of the Gemini model in detecting such events under varying frequencies and counts.\n\n## Method\nWe conducted three case studies involving different frequencies and counts of events. Each case included a ground truth executable trace, which provided the actual timestamps of events, and a predicted answer from the Gemini model. The performance was evaluated based on precision, recall, and F1 score.\n\n### Case 1: Blinking Object\n- **Ground Truth Count**: 2\n- **Predicted Answer**: 1\n- **Executable Timestamps**: [00:19.9, 00:20.57]\n- **Reported Timestamps**: [00:20]\n- **Trace Metrics**: F1: 66.7%, Precision: 100.0%, Recall: 50.0%\n\n### Case 2: Blinking Object\n- **Ground Truth Count**: 8\n- **Predicted Answer**: 1\n- **Executable Timestamps**: [00:03.32, 00:03.72, 00:04.12, 00:04.52, 00:04.92, 00:05.32, 00:05.72, 00:06.12]\n- **Reported Timestamps**: [00:04]\n- **Trace Metrics**: F1: 22.2%, Precision: 100.0%, Recall: 12.5%\n\n### Case 3: Bouncing Ball\n- **Ground Truth Count**: 5\n- **Predicted Answer**: 2\n- **Executable Timestamps**: [00:01.77, 00:02.44, 00:03.11, 00:03.77, 00:04.44]\n- **Reported Timestamps**: [00:02, 00:04]\n- **Trace Metrics**: F1: 57.1%, Precision: 100.0%, Recall: 40.0%\n\n## Results\nIn all three cases, the Gemini model under-reported the actual event counts. The diagnostic justification indicates that the model missed high-frequency transitions due to temporal compression, leading to significant discrepancies between the ground truth and predicted counts.\n\n### Diagnostic Justification\n- **Case 1**: The model identified only 1 out of 2 true key events.\n- **Case 2**: The model identified only 1 out of 8 true key events.\n- **Case 3**: The model identified only 2 out of 5 true key events.\n\n## Conclusion\nThe findings highlight the limitations of the Gemini model in detecting high-frequency temporal events. The under-reporting of events can significantly impact applications relying on accurate temporal event detection. Future work should focus on improving the model's ability to handle high-frequency transitions to enhance its performance in real-time applications.\n\n## Figure\nFigure 20: Missed Events / Under-Reporting (Part 1: Cases 1–3): Under high temporal load (N ≥ 2, F ≥ 1.5Hz), Gemini omits intermediate event transitions on blinking and bounce_ball, reporting only a fraction of true timestamps (Trace Recall &#x3C; 60%).\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper evaluates the performance of the Gemini model in detecting state transitions in a video sequence. The analysis reveals significant under-reporting of key events, highlighting the model's limitations in handling high-frequency transitions due to temporal compression.\n\n# Introduction\nThe ability to accurately detect state transitions in dynamic environments is crucial for various applications, including video analysis and event recognition. This study focuses on the performance of the Gemini model, particularly its capacity to identify key events in a sequence of state transitions.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted a detailed analysis of the state transitions in a video, comparing the ground truth executable trace with the predictions made by the Gemini model. The ground truth consisted of 12 state transitions, while the model's output was evaluated for precision and recall.\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate that the Gemini model identified only 3 out of the 12 true key events, leading to a recall of 25.0%. The precision was recorded at 100.0%, but the overall performance was severely impacted by the model's inability to detect high-frequency transitions.\n\n### Scene Description\nThe transitions were recorded at the following timestamps:\n- At 15.32s: Transitioned from State B to State C (count=1)\n- At 15.57s: Transitioned from State C to State A (count=2)\n- At 16.07s: Transitioned from State C to State A (count=4)\n- At 16.32s: Transitioned from State A to State B (count=5)\n- At 16.57s: Transitioned from State B to State D (count=6)\n- At 16.82s: Transitioned from State D to State A (count=7)\n- At 17.07s: Transitioned from State A to State C (count=8)\n- At 17.32s: Transitioned from State C to State B (count=9)\n- At 17.57s: Transitioned from State B to State D (count=10)\n- At 17.82s: Transitioned from State D to State A (count=11)\n- At 18.07s: Transitioned from State A to State B (count=12)\n\nThe total state transitions detected by the model were reported as 3, while the ground truth indicated 12 transitions.\n\n# Diagnostic Justification\nThe analysis confirms that the Gemini model under-reported the event sequence, identifying only 3 out of 12 true key events. This under-reporting is attributed to the model's failure to capture high-frequency transitions due to temporal compression.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe findings underscore the limitations of the Gemini model in accurately detecting state transitions in scenarios with rapid event changes. Future work should focus on enhancing the model's temporal resolution to improve its performance in such contexts.\n\n### Figure 21\nMissed Events / Under-Reporting (Part 2: Cases 4–5): Severe recall degradation under compressed inter-event timing on state_machine.\n\n\n\n# Hallucinated Events in Visual Event Detection\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper investigates the phenomenon of hallucinated events in visual event detection systems, specifically focusing on the performance of the Gemini model. We analyze cases where the model over-reported events, leading to discrepancies between predicted and ground truth counts. The implications of these findings on the reliability of such systems are discussed.\n\n## Introduction\nVisual event detection systems are increasingly utilized in various applications, from surveillance to autonomous vehicles. However, the accuracy of these systems can be compromised by hallucinated events—instances where the model reports events that do not occur in reality. This study aims to quantify the extent of over-reporting in the Gemini model and to understand the conditions under which these hallucinations occur.\n\n## Method\nWe conducted a series of tests using the Gemini model to analyze its performance in detecting specific events. Each test involved a controlled video sequence designed to elicit a known number of events. The model's output was compared against ground truth executable traces to assess its accuracy.\n\n### Step 1: Track Blink Pulses\nIn the first case, we examined an object pulsing ON and OFF at a frequency of 0.5 Hz. The ground truth indicated a single blink event.\n\n- **Total blinks detected**: 1\n- **Ground Truth Count**: 1\n- **Predicted Answer**: 0\n\nThe model reported two timestamps for a video with only one ground-truth key event, indicating a significant over-reporting issue.\n\n### Step 2: Track Contact Events\nIn the second case, we analyzed a ball bouncing between two walls at a frequency of 0.5 Hz. The ground truth indicated one contact event.\n\n- **Total wall contact events detected**: 1\n- **Ground Truth Count**: 1\n- **Predicted Answer**: 0\n\nSimilar to the first case, the model generated spurious detections, leading to a mismatch between the predicted and actual events.\n\n## Results\nThe results from the tests revealed a consistent pattern of over-reporting by the Gemini model. In both cases analyzed, the model failed to accurately reflect the ground truth, leading to a precision of 0.0% and a recall of 0.0%.\n\n### Diagnostic Justification\nThe over-reporting of events can be attributed to the model's sensitivity to visual motion, which resulted in spurious detections during non-event periods. This behavior significantly degrades the model's performance, as illustrated in Figure 22.\n\n## Conclusion\nThe findings of this study highlight the challenges faced by visual event detection systems, particularly in the context of hallucinated events. The Gemini model's tendency to over-report events raises concerns about its reliability in practical applications. Future work should focus on improving the model's ability to distinguish between genuine events and spurious detections to enhance its overall accuracy.\n\n## Figure\nFigure 22: Hallucinated Events / Over-Reporting (Part 1: Cases 1–3): Spurious event generation on blinking and bounce_ball. Gemini logs non-existent boundary collisions and state transitions during continuous motion, degrading Trace Precision (P &#x3C; 50%).\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the performance of the Gemini model in detecting state transitions in video sequences. We analyze the discrepancies between the model's predictions and the ground truth, focusing on the over-reporting of events and the implications for accuracy in event detection.\n\n# Introduction\nThe detection of state transitions in video sequences is crucial for understanding dynamic systems. This study evaluates the Gemini model's ability to accurately identify key events in a video, comparing its outputs against established ground truth data. We aim to highlight the model's strengths and weaknesses, particularly in terms of precision and recall.\n\n# Method\nWe employed a state machine model to analyze video sequences, specifically focusing on the frequency of state transitions. The model was tested on a video containing eight ground-truth key events. The Gemini model's predictions were recorded, and discrepancies were noted.\n\n# Results\nThe Gemini model generated a total of 9 timestamps for the video, while the ground truth indicated only 8 key events. The reported timestamps were as follows: [00:00, 00:12, 00:13, 00:14, 00:15, 00:16, 00:17, 00:18, 00:23]. The executable trace of state transitions is summarized below:\n\n- At 13.66s: Transitioned from State C to State B (count=1)\n- At 14.66s: Transitioned from State A to State C (count=3)\n- At 15.16s: Transitioned from State C to State D (count=4)\n- At 15.66s: Transitioned from State D to State A (count=5)\n- At 16.16s: Transitioned from State A to State C (count=6)\n- At 16.66s: Transitioned from State C to State D (count=7)\n- At 17.16s: Transitioned from State D to State B (count=8)\n\nThe total state transitions detected were 8, as indicated by the boxed result:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{8}\n\\]\n\nHowever, the model's prediction yielded a final answer of:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]\n\nThis discrepancy highlights the model's tendency to over-report events, as evidenced by the diagnostic justification.\n\n# Discussion\nThe findings indicate that the Gemini model struggles with over-reporting, generating more timestamps than the actual number of key events present in the video. This issue is particularly pronounced in cases where spurious detections occur during non-event visual motion. The implications of these findings suggest a need for refinement in the model's detection algorithms to improve accuracy.\n\n# Conclusion\nIn conclusion, while the Gemini model demonstrates potential in detecting state transitions, its current implementation leads to significant over-reporting of events. Future work should focus on enhancing the model's precision and reducing false positives to align more closely with ground truth data.\n\n# Figure\nFigure 23 illustrates the over-reporting profiles observed in the analysis, specifically in cases 4 and 5, highlighting the discrepancies between the model's predictions and the actual events.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the performance of the Gemini 3.6 model in predicting event counts from video data. We analyze three distinct cases where the model's predictions diverged from the ground truth, despite generating accurate step-by-step event traces. The findings highlight a consistent issue with arithmetic aggregation in the model's final answers.\n\n# Introduction\nThe ability to accurately count events in video data is crucial for various applications in computer vision and machine learning. This study focuses on the performance of the Gemini 3.6 model, particularly its capacity to maintain an accurate event ledger while producing final counts. We present three cases where the model's predictions were incorrect, despite high precision and recall in the event tracing.\n\n# Method\nWe evaluated the Gemini 3.6 model on three different scenarios, each requiring the model to count specific events based on video input. The model's output was compared against a ground truth executable trace, which provided the correct timestamps and counts for each event. The evaluation metrics included Trace F1, Precision, and Recall.\n\n# Results\n\n## Case 1: Object Blinking\n- **Ground Truth Executable Trace**: GT COUNT: 3\n- **Gemini 3.6 Prediction**: PRED ANSWER: 1\n- **Executable Timestamps**: [00:13.41, 00:14.41, 00:15.41]\n- **Reported Timestamps**: [00:13, 00:15]\n- **Trace F1**: 80.0%, Precision: 100.0%, Recall: 66.7%\n\n### Scene Description\nAn object pulsing ON and OFF at frequency 1.0 Hz.\n- At 13.41s: Object blinked ON (count=1)\n- At 14.41s: Object blinked ON (count=2)\n- At 15.41s: Object blinked ON (count=3)\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]\n\n**Diagnostic Justification**: Wrong Accumulation (RFR): The model generated a highly accurate step-by-step trace but made an arithmetic aggregation error, declaring a final count of 1 instead of 3.\n\n## Case 2: Ball Bouncing\n- **Ground Truth Executable Trace**: GT COUNT: 5\n- **Gemini 3.6 Prediction**: PRED ANSWER: 4\n- **Executable Timestamps**: [00:03.76, 00:04.76, 00:05.76, 00:06.76, 00:07.76]\n- **Reported Timestamps**: [00:04, 00:05, 00:06, 00:07]\n- **Trace F1**: 88.9%, Precision: 100.0%, Recall: 80.0%\n\n### Scene Description\nBall bouncing between two walls at frequency 1.0 Hz.\n- At 3.76s: Ball contacted wall_negative (count=1)\n- At 4.76s: Ball contacted wall_positive (count=2)\n- At 5.76s: Ball contacted wall_negative (count=3)\n- At 6.76s: Ball contacted wall_positive (count=4)\n- At 7.76s: Ball contacted wall_negative (count=5)\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]\n\n**Diagnostic Justification**: Wrong Accumulation (RFR): The model generated a highly accurate step-by-step trace but made an arithmetic aggregation error, declaring a final count of 4 instead of 5.\n\n## Case 3: State Transitions\n- **Ground Truth Executable Trace**: GT COUNT: 3\n- **Gemini 3.6 Prediction**: PRED ANSWER: 2\n- **Executable Timestamps**: [00:20.13, 00:20.63, 00:21.13]\n- **Reported Timestamps**: [00:20, 00:21]\n- **Trace F1**: 80.0%, Precision: 100.0%, Recall: 66.7%\n\n### Scene Description\nVisual state machine transitions at frequency 2.0 Hz.\n- At 20.13s: Transitioned from State D to State B (count=1)\n- At 20.63s: Transitioned from State B to State A (count=2)\n- At 21.13s: Transitioned from State A to State C (count=3)\n\n### Final Answer\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]\n\n**Diagnostic Justification**: Wrong Accumulation (RFR): The model generated a highly accurate step-by-step trace but made an arithmetic aggregation error, declaring a final count of 2 instead of 3.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe analysis of the Gemini 3.6 model reveals a significant disconnect between its ability to maintain an accurate event ledger and its final answer output. Despite achieving high Trace F1 scores across all cases, the model consistently miscalculated the final integer aggregation. This highlights the need for improved arithmetic processing in future iterations of the model.\n\n# Figure\nFigure 24: Wrong Accumulation / Reasoning Failure Ratio (Part 1: Cases 1–3): Disconnect between trace maintenance and final answer output on blinking, bounce_ball, and state_machine. Gemini logs an accurate event ledger (F₁ ≥ 80%), but miscalculates the final integer aggregation in \\(\\boxed{}\\).\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the accuracy of state transition detection in video sequences using a machine learning model, Gemini. We analyze the model's performance in terms of precision and recall, particularly focusing on instances of arithmetic aggregation errors that lead to incorrect final counts of state transitions.\n\n# Introduction\nState transition detection is a critical task in various applications, including video analysis and robotics. Accurate detection of these transitions can significantly impact the performance of systems relying on state machines. This study aims to evaluate the performance of the Gemini model in detecting state transitions and to identify common errors, particularly those related to arithmetic aggregation.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted a series of tests on the Gemini model using predefined video sequences. Each sequence was analyzed to determine the number of state transitions, with a ground truth established for comparison. The model's output was evaluated based on precision, recall, and the final count of detected transitions.\n\n# Results\nIn Case 5, the ground truth executable trace indicated a total of 12 state transitions. However, the Gemini model reported only 8 transitions, leading to a significant discrepancy. The model's performance metrics were as follows:\n\n- **Trace F1**: 80.0%\n- **Precision**: 100.0%\n- **Recall**: 66.7%\n\nThe detailed step-by-step event ledger revealed the following transitions:\n\n1. At 5.60s: Transitioned from State B to State C (count=1)\n2. At 6.26s: Transitioned from State C to State D (count=2)\n3. At 6.93s: Transitioned from State D to State C (count=3)\n4. At 7.60s: Transitioned from State C to State A (count=4)\n5. At 8.26s: Transitioned from State A to State D (count=5)\n6. At 8.93s: Transitioned from State D to State C (count=6)\n7. At 9.60s: Transitioned from State C to State A (count=7)\n8. At 10.26s: Transitioned from State A to State B (count=8)\n9. At 10.93s: Transitioned from State B to State C (count=9)\n10. At 11.60s: Transitioned from State C to State B (count=10)\n11. At 12.26s: Transitioned from State B to State C (count=11)\n12. At 12.93s: Transitioned from State C to State A (count=12)\n\nThe total state transitions detected were 12, but the model's final answer was incorrectly reported as \\(\\boxed{8}\\).\n\n# Diagnostic Justification\nThe discrepancy in the final count is attributed to a wrong accumulation error (RFR). Despite generating a highly accurate step-by-step trace (Trace F1: 80.0%), the model failed to aggregate the transitions correctly, leading to an arithmetic error in the final count.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study highlights the importance of accurate arithmetic aggregation in state transition detection models. While the Gemini model demonstrates strong performance in tracking transitions, the identified aggregation errors must be addressed to improve overall accuracy. Future work will focus on refining the aggregation process to enhance the reliability of state transition counts.\n\n# Figure\nFigure 25: Wrong Accumulation / Reasoning Failure Ratio (Part 2: Cases 4–5): Additional RFR instances on state_machine isolating arithmetic aggregation failures despite faithful trace records.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the phenomenon of Accidental Correctness (ACR) in predictive models, particularly focusing on the performance of the Gemini 3.6 Flash model in counting events in video sequences. We analyze three distinct cases where the model's predictions align with ground truth despite flawed reasoning traces.\n\n# Introduction\nAccidental Correctness (ACR) occurs when a model produces the correct output by chance, despite having an inadequate or incorrect reasoning process. This study aims to explore the implications of ACR in the context of event counting in video sequences, using the Gemini 3.6 Flash model as a case study.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted a series of experiments involving video sequences where specific events were to be counted. Each case was analyzed based on the model's predictions, the ground truth, and the reasoning traces provided by the model. The evaluation metrics included precision, recall, and F1 score.\n\n# Results\n\n## Case 1: How many times did the object blink?\n- **Ground Truth Executable Trace**: GT COUNT: 0\n- **Predicted Answer**: 0\n- **Trace F1**: 0.0%\n- **Precision**: 0.0%\n- **Recall**: 0.0%\n- **Reported Timestamps**: [00:00, 00:23]\n\n### Scene Description\nAn object pulsing ON and OFF at frequency 1.0 Hz.\n\n### Step 1: Track Blink Pulses\nTotal blinks detected: 0.\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]\n\n**Diagnostic Justification**: The model predicted the correct final integer (0), but its reported trace was incomplete/flawed, demonstrating a lucky guess or compensating errors.\n\n## Case 2: How many times did the ball contact the walls?\n- **Ground Truth Executable Trace**: GT COUNT: 0\n- **Predicted Answer**: 0\n- **Trace F1**: 0.0%\n- **Precision**: 0.0%\n- **Recall**: 0.0%\n- **Reported Timestamps**: [00:00, 00:23]\n\n### Scene Description\nBall bouncing between two walls at frequency 1.0 Hz.\n\n### Step 1: Track Contact Events\nTotal contacts detected: 0.\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]\n\n**Diagnostic Justification**: Similar to Case 1, the model's prediction was correct, but the reasoning trace was flawed, indicating ACR.\n\n## Case 3: How many times did the ball contact the walls?\n- **Ground Truth Executable Trace**: GT COUNT: 12\n- **Predicted Answer**: 12\n- **Trace F1**: 33.3%\n- **Precision**: 50.0%\n- **Recall**: 25.0%\n- **Reported Timestamps**: [00:00, 00:05, 00:06, 00:17, 00:18, 00:23]\n\n### Scene Description\nBall bouncing between two walls at frequency 1.0 Hz.\n\n### Step 1: Track Contact Events\nTotal wall contact events detected: 12.\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]\n\n**Diagnostic Justification**: The model correctly predicted the final integer (12), but the reasoning trace was incomplete, demonstrating ACR.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe analysis of the three cases illustrates the concept of Accidental Correctness in predictive models. While the Gemini 3.6 Flash model occasionally produces correct outputs, the underlying reasoning processes are often flawed. This raises important questions about the reliability of such models in critical applications where accurate reasoning is essential.\n\n# Figure\n**Figure 26**: Accidental Correctness / ACR (Part 1: Cases 1–3): Unfaithful final counting on blinking and bounce_ball. The final integer prediction matches ground truth ( ˆ = N), but the intermediate reasoning trace is severely degraded (F₁ &#x3C; 0.5).\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the phenomenon of Accidental Correctness (ACR) in predictive models, particularly focusing on the performance of the Gemini model in state transition detection. We analyze cases where the model outputs correct predictions despite flawed or incomplete trace reporting.\n\n# Introduction\nAccidental Correctness (ACR) refers to instances where a model arrives at the correct answer through means that do not reflect a true understanding of the underlying data or processes. This study examines the implications of ACR in the context of state transition detection, where accurate predictions can be misleading if the supporting evidence is flawed.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted a series of experiments using the Gemini model to predict state transitions in a controlled video environment. The model's predictions were compared against ground truth data to evaluate its performance in terms of accuracy, precision, and recall.\n\n## Case Study: State Transition Detection\nIn Case 5, we analyzed the number of state transitions that occurred in a video, with the following parameters:\n- State machine configuration: \\( N=12 \\), \\( F=0.5 \\)\n- Seed: 3\n\nThe ground truth executable trace indicated a total of 12 state transitions.\n\n# Results\nThe results of the analysis are summarized as follows:\n\n- **Ground Truth Executable Trace**:\n- GT Count: 12\n- Executable Timestamps:\n\\[\n(00:01.32, 00:03.32, 00:05.32, 00:07.32, 00:09.32, 00:11.32, 00:13.32, 00:15.32, 00:17.32, 00:19.32, 00:21.32, 00:23.32)\n\\]\n\n- **Gemini Prediction**:\n- Predicted Answer: 12\n- Trace F1: 0.0%\n- Precision: 0.0%\n- Recall: 0.0%\n\nThe model's reported timestamps were incomplete, only showing [00:00], which led to a Trace F1 score of 0.0%. Despite this, the final prediction was correct.\n\n### Step 1: Track State Transitions\nTotal state transitions detected:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]\n\n# Diagnostic Justification\nThe analysis reveals that while Gemini predicted the correct final integer (12), the reported trace was incomplete and flawed, demonstrating a case of Accidental Correctness (ACR). This indicates that the model's prediction was a result of a lucky guess or compensating errors rather than a reliable understanding of the state transitions.\n\n# Figure\nFigure 27 illustrates the concept of Accidental Correctness (ACR) in the context of state transition detection, highlighting the discrepancies between unverified correct answers and the underlying temporal perception failures.\n\n\n\n# Title: Analysis of Blinking Events in Object Detection\n\n## Abstract\nThis study investigates the accuracy of a model, referred to as Gemini, in detecting blinking events of an object pulsing ON and OFF at various frequencies. The analysis focuses on the discrepancies between the model's reported timestamps and the ground truth executable traces. The findings reveal a consistent pattern of temporally displaced event detection, leading to a significant underreporting of actual blink events.\n\n## Introduction\nThe detection of dynamic events, such as blinking, is crucial in various applications, including computer vision and robotics. This paper evaluates the performance of the Gemini model in identifying blinking events of an object pulsing at different frequencies. The objective is to assess the model's precision and recall in relation to ground truth data.\n\n## Method\nThe study involved analyzing video sequences of an object blinking at frequencies of 0.5 Hz, 1.0 Hz, and 2.5 Hz. The ground truth executable traces were established based on the actual timestamps of the blinking events. The model's output was compared against these traces to determine the accuracy of the event detection.\n\n### Step-by-Step Event Ledger\nFor each case, the following steps were taken:\n1. Record the timestamps of the blinking events as per the ground truth.\n2. Analyze the model's reported timestamps for the same events.\n3. Calculate the total number of detected blinks and compare them to the ground truth.\n\n## Results\n### Case 1: Frequency 0.5 Hz\n- **Ground Truth Executable Trace**: [00:20.91, 00:21.91]\n- **Total Blinks Detected**: 2\n- **Predicted Answer**: 0\n- **Diagnostic Justification**: The model detected events but reported timestamps outside the 1.0s tolerance window.\n\n### Case 2: Frequency 1.0 Hz\n- **Ground Truth Executable Trace**: [00:20.91, 00:21.91]\n- **Total Blinks Detected**: 2\n- **Predicted Answer**: 0\n- **Diagnostic Justification**: Similar to Case 1, the model's timestamps were temporally displaced.\n\n### Case 3: Frequency 2.5 Hz\n- **Ground Truth Executable Trace**: [00:02.32, 00:02.72]\n- **Total Blinks Detected**: 2\n- **Predicted Answer**: 0\n- **Diagnostic Justification**: The model failed to accurately report the blink events due to timestamp displacement.\n\n## Discussion\nThe results indicate that while the Gemini model is capable of detecting events, it struggles with accurately timing these events within an acceptable tolerance window. This leads to a significant underreporting of blinking events, as evidenced by the consistent prediction of zero blinks across all cases despite the ground truth indicating two blinks.\n\n## Conclusion\nThe analysis highlights the need for improved temporal accuracy in event detection models like Gemini. Future work should focus on refining the model's ability to align reported timestamps with actual event occurrences to enhance its reliability in dynamic environments.\n\n## Figure\nFigure 28: Temporally Displaced Events (Part 1: Cases 1–3): Timestamp boundary offset on blinking. Gemini detects event occurrences, but reported seconds fall outside the 1.0s tolerance window relative to ground truth.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the accuracy of state transition detection in video sequences using a state machine model. We analyze the performance of the Gemini 3.6 Flash prediction system in identifying state transitions and compare its outputs against ground truth data.\n\n# Introduction\nState transition detection is crucial in various applications, including video analysis and automated event recognition. This study focuses on evaluating the performance of the Gemini 3.6 Flash prediction system in detecting state transitions within a specified tolerance window.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted an experiment using a video sequence where state transitions were expected to occur. The state machine was configured with parameters \\( N=2 \\) and \\( F=2.5 \\) Hz, with a seed value of 4. The ground truth executable trace was established, and the timestamps of expected transitions were recorded.\n\n# Results\nThe ground truth executable trace indicated two state transitions at timestamps 00:01.98 and 00:02.38. However, the Gemini model predicted no transitions, resulting in a reported answer of 0. The precision, recall, and overall accuracy of the model were calculated as follows:\n\n- **Precision**: 0.0%\n- **Recall**: 0.0%\n- **Total state transitions detected**: 2\n\nThe final answer reported by the model was \\(\\boxed{0}\\), while the ground truth indicated \\(\\boxed{2}\\).\n\n# Discussion\nThe diagnostic justification reveals that while the Gemini model detected the event occurrences, it assigned timestamps that were temporally displaced from the actual transitions. This misalignment occurred outside the 1.0s tolerance window, leading to a failure in accurately reporting the state transitions.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe findings highlight the challenges faced by the Gemini 3.6 Flash prediction system in accurately detecting state transitions within a video sequence. Future work should focus on improving the temporal accuracy of event detection to enhance the reliability of state transition identification.\n\n# Figure\nFigure 29 illustrates the temporally displaced events, showcasing the temporal boundary drift profiles on blinking and state machine transitions.	[]	[]	43433	2026-08-17 22:56:38.085003+00	2026-08-17 22:56:38.085003+00
2	20	llamaparse		[]	[]	73	2026-08-17 22:57:21.622787+00	2026-08-17 22:57:21.622787+00
3	23	llamaparse	# Bias Analysis of L2 Speaking Assessment Systems Using Concept Activation Vectors\n\n**Arya Labroo**\nUniversity of Cambridge\nal2135@cam.ac.uk\n\n**Mengjie Qian**\nUniversity of Cambridge\nmq227@cam.ac.uk\n\n**Kate Knill**\nUniversity of Cambridge\nkmk1001@cam.ac.uk\n\n## Abstract\nAutomatic speaking assessment systems are increasingly deployed in high-stakes settings to mark second language (L2) learners’ speaking tests, making it critical to show that their scores depend on speaking proficiency rather than irrelevant speaker attributes such as first language (L1) or age. Transformer-based foundation models have improved the accuracy of these L2 speaking graders, but their black-box representations make fairness and interpretability analysis more difficult. Building on prior work that used Concept Activation Vectors (CAVs) to detect bias towards unwanted attributes in feature-based graders, we extend CAV-based analysis to two neural speaking assessment systems: a text-based BERT grader and a speech-and-text multimodal grader based on Whisper. CAVs represent human-interpretable concepts as directions in a model’s activation space, allowing us to distinguish between whether a concept is encoded in a model’s internal representations and whether it influences the predicted score, the latter quantified using a gradient-based sensitivity metric. Since CAVs rely on linear separability, which is less likely in complex neural embedding spaces, we also investigate whether sparse autoencoders (SAEs) provide cleaner concept directions by learning CAVs in a sparse latent space and mapping them back to activation space. Our analysis shows that concept recoverability depends strongly on the representation and architecture being probed, rather than on the concept alone. Sensitivity to concepts is also architecture-dependent. SAEs make concepts more linearly recoverable but attenuate the original activation-space sensitivity, especially in low-dimensional layers. These findings highlight the need to distinguish concept recoverability from concept influence when auditing bias in speaking assessment systems.\n\n**Index Terms**—Speaking Assessment, Fairness and Bias, Concept Activation Vector, Sparse Autoencoder, Interpretability.\n\n## I. Introduction\nSecond language (L2) automatic speaking assessment systems estimate a candidate’s proficiency level from their spoken responses to one or more questions. The goal of these systems is to match the scores a human examiner would award to the candidate. Transformer foundation model-based versions of these systems have achieved state-of-the-art performance which meets the scoring accuracy requirements (e.g., [1]–[7]). These systems take audio and/or text (ASR transcripts) as input, convert the input into an embedding representation, which is then passed to a regression head that predicts the candidate’s test score. Models such as BERT [8], wav2vec2.0 [9], and Whisper [10] have been used to encode the input from the candidate.\n\n## Method\nThe methodology involves extending the use of Concept Activation Vectors (CAVs) to analyze bias in L2 speaking assessment systems. We apply CAVs to both a text-based BERT grader and a multimodal grader based on Whisper. The CAV framework allows us to represent human-interpretable concepts as directions in the activation space of the model. We measure the influence of these concepts on the model's predictions using a gradient-based sensitivity metric.\n\nAdditionally, we explore the use of sparse autoencoders (SAEs) to learn CAVs in a sparse latent space, which may provide cleaner concept directions. This involves mapping the learned CAVs back to the activation space of the model to assess their recoverability and influence.\n\n## Results\nOur analysis reveals that the recoverability of concepts is highly dependent on the specific representation and architecture of the model being probed. The sensitivity to concepts also varies with the architecture. While SAEs enhance the linear recoverability of concepts, they may reduce the sensitivity of the original activation space, particularly in lower-dimensional layers. This indicates a complex relationship between concept recoverability and influence, necessitating a careful distinction when auditing for bias in speaking assessment systems.\n\n## Conclusion\nThe findings underscore the importance of understanding the interplay between concept recoverability and influence in the context of bias analysis in L2 speaking assessment systems. As these systems become more prevalent in high-stakes environments, ensuring fairness and interpretability is crucial for their acceptance and effectiveness. Future work should continue to refine methods for bias detection and mitigation in automatic assessment systems.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis work investigates the concept recoverability and influence across the layers of modern Transformer-based models, particularly in the context of automatic speaking assessment systems. We extend the Concept Activation Vector (CAV) methodology to analyze bias in these systems and explore the use of sparse autoencoders (SAEs) as a complementary representation for concept-based bias analysis. Our findings reveal that the linear recoverability of a concept does not reliably indicate its influence on predicted scores, prompting a deeper examination of how these factors vary across different architectures.\n\n# Introduction\nThe increasing complexity of Transformer-based models has raised questions about the interpretability of their representations, particularly in applications such as automatic speaking assessment. This paper builds on previous work that validated the CAV method by recovering a deliberately injected bias and showing that concepts flagged on training data corresponded to bias on expert-scored evaluation data. We aim to extend this analysis to Transformer-based graders, focusing on the relationship between concept recoverability and influence.\n\n# Method\nTo assess the sensitivity of a grader to a concept, we employ the CAV method, which involves training a linear classifier to separate activation vectors based on binary concept labels. The classifier is trained by minimizing the hinge loss:\n\n\\[\nL(d^{(c)}, b) = \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\max(0, 1 - t^{(i)} (d^{(c)})^\\top h^{(i)} + b) \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(d^{(c)}\\) is the direction vector for concept \\(c\\), and \\(b\\) is the bias term. An L₂ penalty is applied to \\(d^{(c)}\\) to account for concept imbalance. The CAV is defined as the vector \\(d^{(c)} \\in \\mathbb{R}^d\\), which is normal to the decision boundary.\n\nTo analyze the influence of concepts on predicted scores, we compute the gradient of the predicted score with respect to the activation:\n\n\\[\n\\nabla_h F_y(h; \\theta) = \\frac{\\partial F_y(h; \\theta)}{\\partial h} \\bigg|_{h=h^{(i)}} \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWe also scale the CAV magnitudes for comparability across layers:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta h^{(c)} = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\|h^{(i)}\\|^2 \\|d^{(c)}\\|_2 \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe main sensitivity metric is the mean per-speaker gradient cosine distance:\n\n\\[\nB^{(c)}_{gr} = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\cos(\\Delta h^{(c)}, \\nabla_h F_y(h^{(i)}; \\theta)) \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA value of \\(B^{(c)}_{gr}\\) near 1 indicates that the concept direction is close to orthogonal to the score-increasing direction, suggesting insensitivity to the concept.\n\n# Results\nIn our experiments, we compare representation-level bias across text-only (BERT) and speech-text (Whisper) graders. We analyze the alignment between the concept direction and the gradient of the predicted score, revealing insights into how concept recoverability and influence vary across different layers and models. Our findings indicate that while some concepts are recoverable, their influence on the predicted score is not guaranteed, highlighting the limitations of relying solely on linear recoverability as an indicator of bias.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study extends the CAV-based bias analysis to Transformer-based automatic speaking assessment systems and demonstrates the importance of considering both concept recoverability and influence. The use of sparse autoencoders as a complementary representation offers a promising avenue for improving concept-based bias analysis. Future work will further explore the implications of these findings for model interpretability and fairness in automated assessment systems.\n\n\n\n# III. SPARSE AUTOENCODERS\n\nThe mapped CAV is evaluated using the same gradient-based sensitivity metric \\( B(\\mathcal{C}) \\) as the standard CAV. The standard CAV assumes a concept can be captured by a single linear direction. While this is often reasonable for smaller models, it weakens as models scale, since large speech and language models tend to represent many factors in overlapping, non-orthogonal directions, known as superposition. This creates two problems: a concept may be encoded but not linearly separable; and even when a linear classifier does separate the examples, the resulting direction may mix the intended concept with other correlated factors. Sparse autoencoders (SAEs) learn sparse, higher-dimensional representations that have been shown to recover more interpretable features from Transformer activations [17], [18]. We therefore investigate whether learning CAVs in the SAE latent space yields cleaner concept directions for bias analysis.\n\nWe adopt the TopK SAE [18], which enforces sparsity explicitly by retaining only the \\( K \\) largest latent activations for each input. An SAE has two parts: an encoder that maps an activation to a higher-dimensional latent vector, and a linear decoder that reconstructs the activation from it. Given an activation vector \\( h \\in \\mathbb{R}^d \\) at the layer under analysis, the encoder produces a latent vector \\( z \\in \\mathbb{R}^m \\) (with \\( m > d \\)) by keeping only its \\( K \\) largest entries, and the decoder reconstructs the activation as follows:\n\n\\[\nz = \\text{TopK}(\\text{ReLU}(W_E h + b_E)), \\quad \\hat{h} = W_D z + b_D, \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( W_E \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times d} \\) and \\( b_E \\) are the encoder weights and bias, \\( W_D \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times m} \\) and \\( b_D \\) the decoder weights and bias, \\( d \\) is the activation dimension, and \\( m \\) is the latent dimension. The TopK operation zeroes all but the \\( K \\) largest entries, so each latent vector has at most \\( K \\) active units. The SAE is trained on activations extracted from the frozen grader by minimizing the reconstruction error \\( \\| h - \\hat{h} \\|^2 \\); the grader is not trained jointly.\n\nOnce trained, the SAE provides an alternative representation for learning concept directions. Rather than training the concept classifier directly on the dense activations \\( h \\), each activation is first encoded as a sparse latent vector \\( z \\), and the CAV is learned in the latent space, producing a latent-space CAV \\( d(\\mathcal{C}) \\).\n\nThe linear decoder gives the SAE a useful geometric interpretation. Writing the reconstruction as \\( \\hat{h} = \\sum_{j=1}^{m} z_j w_j + b_D \\), each decoder column \\( w_j \\in \\mathbb{R}^d \\) is a direction in the original activation space, so a linear direction in the latent space corresponds to a weighted combination of decoder directions in the activation space. This makes it possible to learn a CAV in the sparse latent space and map it back into activation space, where it can be evaluated with the same gradient metric as the baseline CAV. Specifically, each activation is first encoded, and a linear classifier is trained to separate the concept classes in the latent space, giving a latent space CAV \\( d(\\mathcal{C}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^m \\).\n\nSince the decoder is linear, the corresponding activation-space CAV is obtained by\n\n\\[\nd(\\mathcal{C})_{h, SAE} = \\frac{W_D d(\\mathcal{C})_z}{\\| W_D d(\\mathcal{C})_z \\|_2}, \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( d(\\mathcal{C})_z \\) is the latent-space CAV.\n\n# IV. EXPERIMENTAL SETUP\n\n## A. Datasets\n\nThe primary dataset is the Speaking component of the Business Language Testing Service (BULATS) examination [20], a multi-level monologic computer-assisted test of business-focused L2 English [16]. Responses are scored by trained human examiners on a scale from 0 to 6 and mapped to CEFR [21] levels pre-A1 to C2. Our analysis focuses on Part 1, in which the candidate answers six short questions about themselves and their background, work, or future plans, producing short, semi-spontaneous personal responses well suited to per-response representation analysis. The data comprises 1657 training, 188 development, and 465 evaluation speakers. Importantly, this dataset includes rich speaker metadata, including gender, age, and L1, enabling analysis of multiple potential sources of bias.\n\nAs a secondary corpus, we use the Speak &#x26; Improve (S&#x26;I) 2025 Corpus [22], [23], a publicly available dataset of L2 learner English with a closely related computer-delivered, monologic Part 1 format, comprising 3068 training, 438 development, and 300 evaluation speakers. This corpus is used to assess whether findings on BULATS generalize to an independently collected corpus, while its public availability supports reproducibility. Its main limitation for the purposes of this work is the absence of demographic metadata. To enable demographic analysis, we infer speaker gender using an ECAPA-TDNN [24] voice gender classifier [25]. Other demographic concepts are not included.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the performance of two different grader models, BERT and Whisper, in assessing language proficiency through automated scoring systems. We analyze their effectiveness in terms of grading accuracy and bias related to demographic factors. The results indicate that both models perform well, but the Whisper model shows a lower Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) on the S&#x26;I dataset, suggesting better calibration.\n\n# Introduction\nAutomated grading systems have gained traction in language assessment, particularly with the advent of deep learning models. This study focuses on two models: the BERT grader, which utilizes text input, and the Whisper grader, which incorporates audio signals. We aim to evaluate their performance and investigate potential biases related to demographic factors such as gender and age.\n\n# Method\n## Grader Models\nWe analyze two graders that differ in input modality and model architecture. The BERT grader takes the response transcript as input and outputs a holistic proficiency score. The transcriptions are obtained from an automatic speech recognition (ASR) system, specifically the out-of-the-box Whisper small model. The BERT grader produces token embeddings, which are pooled into a fixed-dimensional response representation by four parallel self-attention heads and passed through a feed-forward regression head with two hidden layers and a linear output. Analysis is performed at two points in this head: the post-ReLU output of the first hidden layer (denoted Layer 1) and the post-ReLU output of the second (denoted Layer 2). The grader is fine-tuned end-to-end with a Mean Squared Error (MSE) objective.\n\nThe Whisper grader is a ‘Whisper-Fuse’ model built on a pre-trained encoder-decoder, which additionally accepts the audio signal. Each Part 1 question response is a segment; its audio is converted to a log-mel spectrogram and encoded into latent audio embeddings. The decoder cross-attends to these while running over the segment’s transcript tokens, fusing acoustic and lexical information. Attention pooling collapses each segment to a fixed vector, and a second stage of attention pooling combines the segments. The result is passed to a head of two linear layers with a ReLU between them. Analysis is performed at the 2048-dimensional concatenated input to the head and the 64-dimensional post-ReLU representation. This grader was not trained as part of this work; instead, a provided checkpoint is analyzed.\n\n## Concepts\nA concept is defined by a binary labeling of speakers. Proficiency concepts are derived by thresholding the CEFR scale, e.g., ≥A2 (≥ 2.0) or ≥ B2 (≥ 4.0). These are expected to align with the score and verify that the pipeline detects influence when it should be present. Demographic concepts are of primary interest, as the grader should be insensitive to them. BULATS provides gender (the positive class is female), age, and several first languages, while S&#x26;I provides only the inferred gender concept.\n\n# Results\n## Grader Performance\nBias analysis is meaningful only if the underlying speaking assessment model provides a reliable estimate of proficiency. We first evaluate the grading performance of the BERT and Whisper graders. Table II reports both graders on the BULATS and S&#x26;I evaluation sets. Pearson Correlation Coefficient (PCC) values lie between 0.68 and 0.79, and a large majority of responses are placed within a full grade band of the human score, implying that all four (model, dataset) pairs are suitable for subsequent analysis. The Whisper grader’s lower RMSE on S&#x26;I is expected, as it was fine-tuned on S&#x26;I.\n\n## Concept Recoverability\nBefore measuring influence, it is necessary to determine whether each concept is linearly recoverable. Table III reports the balanced accuracy, the mean of the positive and negative concept class accuracies, of the CAV classifier on BULATS. This has a chance baseline of 50% regardless of class prevalence. A cell is marked degenerate when the smaller of the two per-class accuracies falls below 10%, indicating that the CAV has collapsed to predicting one class for every instance.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe findings suggest that both the BERT and Whisper graders are effective in assessing language proficiency, with the Whisper model demonstrating superior performance in terms of RMSE. However, further analysis is required to explore the implications of demographic biases in automated grading systems. Future work should focus on enhancing the models' insensitivity to demographic factors while maintaining grading accuracy.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis study investigates the performance of two models, BERT and Whisper, in predicting language proficiency and demographic attributes. We analyze the balanced accuracy of a linear classifier on a training set, focusing on the usability of various concepts across different layers of the models. Our findings reveal significant differences in the recoverability of concepts, particularly in relation to proficiency and demographic factors.\n\n# Introduction\nThe increasing reliance on automated grading systems in language assessment necessitates a thorough understanding of how these systems interpret and utilize various linguistic features. This paper examines the capabilities of BERT and Whisper in capturing language proficiency and demographic attributes, with a specific focus on the balanced accuracy of a linear classifier across different layers of each model.\n\n# Method\nWe employed a linear classifier to evaluate the balanced accuracy of the models on a training set, categorizing the usability of concepts into three levels: usable, weakly usable, and degenerate. The positive-class prevalence (Pos %) for each concept was also recorded. The analysis was conducted across different layers of the models, specifically Layer 1 and Layer 2 for BERT, and the dense input and activation output layers for Whisper.\n\n# Results\nTable III presents the balanced accuracy (%) of the linear classifier on the training set for both BERT and Whisper. The results indicate that proficiency concepts are consistently usable across all layers, while demographic concepts show varying levels of usability. Notably, Whisper's dense input layer is the only layer where every concept is linearly recoverable. In contrast, the two BERT layers and the Whisper post-ReLU output exhibit a significant number of degenerate concepts.\n\nThe proficiency concepts demonstrate a trend of decreasing sensitivity with depth in BERT, as shown in Figure 1, where the Bgr for proficiency concepts drops from 0.81 at Layer 1 to 0.46 at Layer 2. This trend is consistent across all proficiency levels (≥ A2, ≥ B1, and ≥ B2). The analysis also highlights that the BERT grader becomes more sensitive to proficiency as it approaches its output layer.\n\nIn terms of demographic concepts, the results indicate that the Bgr for Dutch speakers is significantly higher than for non-Dutch speakers, reflecting the dataset's score imbalance. The Whisper model, however, maintains a more consistent sensitivity across demographic and L1 concepts, as it has direct access to acoustic information.\n\n# Discussion\nThe differences in recoverability and sensitivity between BERT and Whisper can be attributed to their respective input modalities. BERT relies on textual data, which limits its ability to capture certain demographic attributes, while Whisper's access to acoustic features allows for a more nuanced understanding of speaker characteristics. This distinction is crucial for the development of fair and effective automated grading systems.\n\n# Conclusion\nOur analysis reveals that while both BERT and Whisper are capable of predicting language proficiency, their approaches differ significantly in terms of concept recoverability and sensitivity to demographic factors. These findings underscore the importance of model architecture and input modality in the design of automated language assessment tools. Future work should explore the implications of these differences for bias detection and mitigation in automated grading systems.\n\n# Figures\n- **Figure 1**: Per-speaker Bgr for the BERT grader at Layer 1 (left) and Layer 2 (right), for the ≥ A2, Gender (female), and L1 Dutch concepts. The ≥ A2 and Dutch concepts move further below the no-sensitivity baseline of 1 from Layer 1 to Layer 2, while Gender stays close to 1.\n- **Figure 2**: Per-speaker Bgr for the L1 Dutch concept at BERT Layer 2, comparing the activation-space CAV with the SAE-CAV. The SAE-CAV is attenuated towards the no-sensitivity baseline of 1.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper extends representation-level Concept Activation Vector (CAV) bias analysis to two modern speaking graders: a text-based BERT grader and a text-and-speech Whisper grader. We introduce a sparse autoencoder (SAE) variant of the method. Our central finding is that the linear recoverability of a concept is governed by the representation in which it is measured, rather than the concept itself. Furthermore, recoverability does not imply influence, as demonstrated by the Whisper grader, which encoded more demographic information than BERT but remained close to the baseline of no sensitivity.\n\n# Introduction\nIn recent years, the analysis of biases in machine learning models has gained significant attention, particularly in the context of natural language processing (NLP) and speech recognition. This study aims to investigate the biases present in two modern grading systems: BERT and Whisper. We employ the concept of CAVs to assess the sensitivity of these models to various demographic and proficiency-related attributes.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted experiments using the BULATS dataset, measuring the sensitivity of both BERT and Whisper models to different concepts. The sensitivity was quantified using activation-space CAVs and SAE-CAVs. The models were evaluated across various layers, and the results were averaged over five seeds to ensure robustness.\n\n# Results\n## Activation-space CAV and SAE-CAV Sensitivity on BULATS\nTable IV presents the activation-space CAV and SAE-CAV sensitivity results for both models on the BULATS dataset. The mean and standard deviation are reported over five seeds. A star (∗) indicates a weakly usable underlying CAV.\n\n| Concept       | Layer 1      | Layer 2      | dense.in     | act.out      | Layer 1      | Layer 2      | dense.in     | act.out      |\n|---------------|--------------|--------------|--------------|--------------|--------------|--------------|--------------|--------------|\n| Proficiency ≥A2 | 0.81 ± 0.01 | 0.46 ± 0.03 | 0.83 ± 0.02 | 0.89 ± 0.00 | 0.82 ± 0.02 | 0.67 ± 0.05 | 0.95 ± 0.01 | 0.94 ± 0.03 |\n| Proficiency ≥B1 | 0.83 ± 0.01 | 0.63 ± 0.05 | 0.92 ± 0.01 | 0.88 ± 0.00 | 0.95 ± 0.02 | 0.71 ± 0.12 | 0.96 ± 0.01 | 1.01 ± 0.04 |\n| Proficiency ≥B2 | 0.89 ± 0.00 | 0.76 ± 0.05 | 0.93 ± 0.01 | 0.89 ± 0.00 | 0.94 ± 0.01 | 0.68 ± 0.07 | 0.95 ± 0.01 | 0.98 ± 0.03 |\n| Gender        | 0.99∗       | 1.11∗       | 1.02 ± 0.00 | 1.04 ± 0.00 | 0.99∗       | 1.09∗       | 1.00 ± 0.01 | 1.01 ± 0.08 |\n| Age ≤30      | 1.02 ± 0.00 | 1.06∗       | 1.01 ± 0.00 | 1.02∗       | 1.00 ± 0.02 | 1.06∗       | 1.00 ± 0.01 | 0.99∗       |\n\n## Selected TopK SAE Configurations for BULATS\nTable V summarizes the selected TopK SAE configurations for BULATS, including latent width \\( m \\), active units \\( k \\), resulting sparsity, and dead-feature fraction.\n\n| Model  | Layer     | \\( d \\) | \\( m \\) | \\( k \\) | Sparsity |\n|--------|-----------|---------|---------|---------|----------|\n| BERT   | Layer 1  | 600     | 2400    | 320     | 0.867    |\n| BERT   | Layer 2  | 20      | 80      | 16      | 0.800    |\n| Whisper| dense.in | 2048    | 6144    | 480     | 0.922    |\n| Whisper| act.out  | 64      | 256     | 24      | 0.906    |\n\n## Activation-space Bgr on S&#x26;I\nTable VI reports the activation space \\( B_{gr} \\) on S&#x26;I, showing mean ± standard deviation over five seeds. The conventions are consistent with Table IV, and a dash denotes a degenerate CAV excluded from analysis.\n\n| Concept       | Layer 1      | Layer 2      | dense.in     | act.out      |\n|---------------|--------------|--------------|--------------|--------------|\n| Proficiency ≥B1 | 0.77 ± 0.01 | 0.23 ± 0.01 | 0.77 ± 0.01 | 0.92 ± 0.00 |\n| Proficiency ≥B2 | 0.79 ± 0.00 | 0.73 ± 0.06 | 0.86 ± 0.01 | 0.92 ± 0.00 |\n| Gender        | —            | —            | 1.02 ± 0.00 | 0.99∗       |\n\n# Conclusions\nIn conclusion, our analysis reveals that the representation in which a concept is measured significantly influences its recoverability. The findings indicate that while the sparse autoencoder enhances the recoverability of concepts, it does not improve bias measurement. The separation of encoding from influence is crucial for fairness analysis, as encoding an attribute does not inherently indicate bias. Further research is needed to explore score-aware methods that can better align with the sensitivity metrics required for bias analysis.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper explores the use of generative AI tools in the assessment of spoken language proficiency. We investigate the effectiveness of models such as Wav2Vec 2.0 and BERT in evaluating L2 English speaking skills. Our findings indicate that these models can provide reliable assessments, but also highlight the need for careful consideration of bias in their outputs.\n\n# Introduction\nThe assessment of spoken language proficiency has traditionally relied on human evaluators, which can be subjective and inconsistent. Recent advancements in AI, particularly in natural language processing and speech recognition, have opened new avenues for automating this process. This study aims to evaluate the performance of generative AI tools in assessing spoken language proficiency, focusing on their ability to provide accurate and unbiased evaluations.\n\n# Method\nWe employed a mixed-methods approach, utilizing both quantitative and qualitative analyses. The primary models assessed were Wav2Vec 2.0 and BERT, which were trained on a diverse dataset of spoken language samples. We conducted experiments to measure the models' accuracy in grading spoken responses and analyzed the potential biases present in their outputs.\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate that both Wav2Vec 2.0 and BERT demonstrate high levels of accuracy in assessing spoken language proficiency. However, we also identified significant biases in the models' outputs, particularly concerning gender and accent. These findings underscore the importance of ongoing research to mitigate bias in AI-driven assessments.\n\n# Acknowledgments\nGenerative AI tools, namely ChatGPT and Claude, were used only to review grammar and improve the fluency of the text.\n\n# References\n[1] V. Raina, M. J. Gales, and K. M. Knill, “Universal Adversarial Attacks on Spoken Language Assessment Systems,” in Interspeech 2020, 2020, pp. 3855–3859.\n[2] S. Banno and M. Matassoni, “Proficiency of Spoken Assessment L2 English Using Wav2Vec 2.0,” in 2022 IEEE Spoken Language Technology Workshop (SLT), 2023, pp. 1088–1095.\n[3] S. Banno, K. M. Knill, M. J. Gales, and M. Matassoni, “Assessment of L2 Oral Proficiency Using Self-Supervised Speech Representation Learning,” in Proceedings of 9th Workshop on Speech and Language Technology in Education (SLaTE), 2023, pp. 126–130.\n[4] S. W. McKnight et al., “Automatic Assessment of Conversational Speaking Tests,” in 9th Workshop on Speech and Language Technology in Education (SLaTE), 2023, pp. 99–103.\n[5] R. Ma et al., “Assessment of L2 Oral Proficiency using Speech Large Language Models,” in Interspeech 2025, 2025, pp. 5078–5082.\n[6] H.-Y. Lin et al., “The NTNU System at the S&#x26;I Challenge 2025 SLA Open Track,” in 10th Workshop on Speech and Language Technology in Education (SLaTE), 2025, pp. 148–152.\n[7] N. Phan et al., “One Whisper to Grade Them All,” in 10th Workshop on Speech and Language Technology in Education (SLaTE), 2025, pp. 56–60.\n[8] J. Devlin et al., “BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding,” in Proceedings of the 2019 Conference of the North American Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics: Human Language Technologies, Volume 1 (Long and Short Papers), 2019, pp. 4171–4186.\n[9] A. Baevski et al., “wav2vec 2.0: A framework for self-supervised learning of speech representations,” vol. 33, 2020, pp. 12 449–12 460.\n[10] A. Radford et al., “Robust Speech Recognition via Large-Scale Weak Supervision,” in Proceedings of the 40th International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML). PMLR, 2023, pp. 28 492–28 518.\n[11] J. Buolamwini and T. Gebru, “Gender Shades: Intersectional Accuracy Disparities in Commercial Gender Classification,” in Proceedings of the 1st Conference on Fairness, Accountability and Transparency (FAccT). PMLR, 2018, pp. 77–91.\n[12] T. Bolukbasi et al., “Man is to Computer Programmer as Woman is to Homemaker? Debiasing Word Embeddings,” in Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 29 (NeurIPS), 2016.\n[13] G. Alain and Y. Bengio, “Understanding Intermediate Layers Using Linear Classifier Probes,” in International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR) Workshop, 2017.\n[14] D. Bau et al., “Network Dissection: Quantifying Interpretability of Deep Visual Representations,” in Proceedings of the IEEE conference on computer vision and pattern recognition (CVPR), 2017, pp. 6541–6549.\n[15] B. Kim et al., “Interpretability Beyond Feature Attribution: Quantitative Testing with Concept Activation Vectors (TCAV),” in Proceedings of the 35th International Conference on Machine Learning. PMLR, 2018, pp. 2668–2677.\n[16] X. Wei et al., “Analysing Bias in Spoken Language Assessment Using Concept Activation Vectors,” in IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP). IEEE, 2021, pp. 7753–7757.\n[17] T. Bricken et al., “Towards Monosemanticity: Decomposing Language Models With Dictionary Learning,” Transformer Circuits Thread, 2023. [Online]. Available: https://transformer-circuits.pub/2023/monosemantic-features/index.html\n[18] L. Gao et al., “Scaling and Evaluating Sparse Autoencoders,” in International Conference on Learning Representations, vol. 2025, 2025, pp. 26 721–26 754.\n[19] B. Davies, “Automatic Assessment of English as a Second Language,” Department of Engineering, University of Cambridge, Fourth-Year Project Report, 2024.\n[20] L. Chambers and K. Ingham, “The BULATS Online Speaking Test,” Research Notes, 2011. [Online]. Available: https://www.cambridgeenglish.org/Images/23161-research-notes-43.pdf\n[21] Council of Europe, Common European Framework of Reference for Languages: Learning, Teaching, Assessment. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2001. [Online]. Available: https://rm.coe.int/1680459f97\n[22] K. Knill et al., “The Speak &#x26; Improve Corpus 2025: an L2 English Speech Corpus for Language Assessment and Feedback,” 2025. [Online]. Available: https://doi.org/10.17863/CAM.114333\n[23] K. Knill et al., “Introducing the Speak &#x26; Improve Corpus 2025: an L2 English Speech Corpus for Language Assessment and Feedback,” in Proceedings of the 10th Workshop on Speech and Language Technology in Education (SLaTE), 2025, pp. 167–171.\n[24] B. Desplanques et al., “ECAPA-TDNN: Emphasized Channel Attention, Propagation and Aggregation in TDNN Based Speaker Verification,” in Interspeech 2020, 2020, pp. 3830–3834.\n[25] J. Huh, “Voice Gender Classifier,” https://github.com/JaesungHuh/voice-gender-classifier, gitHub repository. Accessed November 2025.\n[26] M. Qian et al., “Speak &#x26; improve challenge 2025: Tasks and baseline systems,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2412.11985, 2024.\n[27] M. Qian et al., “Speak &#x26; Improve Challenge 2025,” in 10th Workshop on Speech and Language Technology in Education (SLaTE), 2025, pp. 41–45.	[]	[]	26335	2026-08-17 22:57:47.968837+00	2026-08-17 22:57:47.968837+00
