5	47	llamaparse	# From Passive Mirrors to Active Agents: Holonic Digital Twins for Physical AI over Networks\n\n**Christo Kurisummoottil Thomas, Senior Member, IEEE, Omar Hashash, Member, IEEE, and Walid Saad, Fellow, IEEE.**\n\n## Abstract\nArtificial intelligence (AI) revolutionized multiple sectors ranging from healthcare to entertainment. Remarkably, despite this progress, today’s AI tools, including deep learning and generative AI (e.g., large language models), still fail when embedded into physical systems, such as robots or vehicles that operate under the physical laws of the real world. This limitation stems from the inability of physical AI systems to maintain reliable world models for long-horizon planning under uncertainty and to generalize to unseen scenarios. In this context, wireless networks, through their pervasive sensing and communication infrastructure, can act as orchestrators of physical intelligence. However, current wireless architectures optimize for throughput, latency, and reliability and cannot support real-time physical AI coordination where agents must maintain a shared spatiotemporal context. To overcome such challenges, in this paper, a network of holonic digital twins (HDT-Nets) framework is proposed to deliver the real-time physical AI inference requirements through holonic agents that actively reason about their environment rather than passively mirror physical assets. Each HDT is realized as a hierarchical structure spanning the physical agent and the network edge, reasoning autonomously at the local level while cooperating with neighboring HDTs to form larger collectively intelligent units at the network level. In HDT-Net, causal Markov blankets (MBs) spanning sensing, communication, and control domains determine which agents must coordinate and enable counterfactual reasoning over multi-domain interventions. Active inference, operating within these boundaries, unifies perception, action, and learning by minimizing expected free energy, simultaneously deciding what beliefs to transmit based on their cognitive value to the receiver. Category theory guarantees that these transmitted beliefs preserve semantic structure when crossing between heterogeneous agents with incompatible representations. Finally, integrated information theory provides a quantifiable metric to capture when collective intelligence exceeds independent operation and how network intelligence evolves through coordinated learning and information exchange.\n\n## I. Introduction\nPhysical AI represents systems that do not just process information in the digital realm but must perceive, reason, and act under the laws of the physical world. Unlike current generations of AI that operate on curated datasets and well-defined input-output spaces, physical AI systems must be able to perform spatial and temporal reasoning, act under dynamic uncertainty, coordinate with other physical agents, and continuously adapt to a dynamic world in real-time. Such requirements become indispensable because the physical world does not tolerate failures, whether caused by a single agent’s perception errors or by emergent conflicts when multiple agents interact in a shared environment.\n\nThe above challenge has catalyzed a fundamental architectural shift. As envisioned in our prior work, network infrastructure must transition from pure data pipes into distributed inference and reasoning engines by deploying AI compute directly at the network edge. The core challenge here is enabling networked agents to reason collectively, coordinate beliefs, and act under physical constraints. This, in turn, requires the wireless network to provide a shared spatiotemporal context among agents, ensuring that distributed belief updates, sensing, and control actions remain coherent across space and time.\n\n## Method\nTo address the limitations of current physical AI systems, we propose the HDT-Nets framework, which consists of holonic digital twins that operate at both the local and network levels. Each HDT is designed to autonomously reason about its environment while collaborating with neighboring HDTs to form a larger intelligent network. The framework utilizes causal Markov blankets to determine the necessary coordination among agents and employs active inference to minimize expected free energy.\n\n## Results\nThe proposed HDT-Nets framework demonstrates improved coordination and inference capabilities in physical AI systems. By leveraging the unique properties of wireless networks, such as pervasive sensing and communication, the framework allows for real-time updates and collective reasoning among agents. This results in enhanced adaptability and resilience in dynamic environments, ultimately leading to more effective physical AI operations.\n\n## Conclusion\nThe transition from passive data transport layers to active reasoning engines in wireless networks is crucial for the advancement of physical AI. The HDT-Nets framework provides a promising approach to achieving real-time inference and collective reasoning, enabling physical AI systems to operate effectively under the constraints of the physical world. Future work will focus on further refining the framework and exploring its applications across various domains.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a transformative concept of networked holonic digital twins (HDTs), termed HDT-Nets, which address the challenges faced by current digital twin (DT) implementations in physical AI systems. We propose a cognitive infrastructure that prioritizes transmissions based on their cognitive value, maintains synchronization between communicated beliefs and physical dynamics, aligns heterogeneous representations into a shared semantic space, and adapts to the evolving beliefs and actions of agents for coordinated decision-making in real time.\n\n# Introduction\nThe integration of digital twins (DTs) in physical AI systems has the potential to enhance the perception-decision-action loop by providing a shared spatiotemporal context. This ensures that agents perceive the same physical reality simultaneously, make decisions grounded in synchronized world models, and commit to coordinated actions. However, existing DT implementations primarily treat twins as passive simulators for prediction or offline optimization, which is inadequate for the demands of physical AI systems.\n\nThe challenges include the need for cognitive value in transmission decisions, the requirement for perfectly synchronized inference updates with real-world dynamics, and the necessity for aligning heterogeneous representations. Furthermore, the lack of theory of mind capabilities in current networks limits their ability to predict how agents will interpret and act upon transmitted beliefs.\n\n# Method\nTo overcome these challenges, we propose a cognitive infrastructure that evolves the network into a system that:\n1. Prioritizes transmissions based on their cognitive value to downstream agents.\n2. Maintains tight spatiotemporal synchronization between communicated beliefs and physical dynamics.\n3. Aligns heterogeneous representations into a shared semantic space for collective inference.\n4. Models and adapts to the evolving beliefs and actions of agents to enable coordinated decision-making in real time.\n\nThis approach necessitates a computational abstraction where representations of the physical world, agent states, and their causal interactions can be continuously inferred, synchronized, and acted upon across the network.\n\n# Results\nThe proposed HDT-Nets leverage the capabilities of digital twins to maintain virtual representations of physical agents and their environments. This allows physical AI systems to reason about future outcomes, align heterogeneous observations, and support coordinated decision-making. The underlying world model serves as the computational substrate for the DT, defining how the twin represents the world and captures its underlying dynamics.\n\nAs illustrated in Fig. 1, each physical AI agent is paired with a holonic digital twin that spans both the physical agent and the network edge. This hierarchical structure enables the composition of progressively larger cognitive units, enhancing collective intelligence beyond what any individual agent can achieve alone.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe vision of HDT-Nets represents a significant advancement in the design of cognitive infrastructures for physical AI systems. By addressing the limitations of current DT implementations and enabling a more dynamic and responsive network architecture, we can facilitate the emergence of collective intelligence in physical AI systems, ultimately leading to safer and more effective operations in complex environments.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThe paper discusses the development of High-Dimensional Twins (HDTs) for enabling real-time distributed inference in 6G networks. It emphasizes the importance of causal reasoning, compositional structure, adaptive learning, and collective coordination in constructing HDTs. The paper introduces a framework for partitioning cyber-physical networks into autonomous AI agents using Markov blankets (MBs) and explores the integration of information theory to enhance coordination effectiveness among agents.\n\n# Introduction\nThe advent of 6G networks necessitates a paradigm shift in how we approach distributed inference and coordination among heterogeneous agents. The concept of High-Dimensional Twins (HDTs) is introduced as a means to facilitate real-time decision-making and resource orchestration across the network. This paper outlines the foundational pillars that support the construction of HDTs, focusing on causal reasoning, compositionality, and active inference.\n\n# Method\nTo achieve effective coordination among agents, we employ Markov blankets (MBs) to define the boundaries of each HDT. MBs help identify the minimal set of variables necessary for coordination while maintaining conditional independence from the rest of the system. This approach allows for the dynamic partitioning of the network into autonomous reasoning agents based on the causal structure of the underlying cyber-physical system.\n\nAdditionally, we integrate information theory to derive a network-level measure called spatiotemporal integrated information \\( \\Phi \\). This measure quantifies the collective intelligence of coordinating agents and informs decisions regarding when and how much information to exchange.\n\n# Results\nThe implementation of HDTs demonstrates significant improvements in coordination effectiveness among agents. By utilizing MBs, agents can efficiently share information while minimizing communication overhead. The application of spatiotemporal integrated information \\( \\Phi \\) allows for a more nuanced understanding of collective intelligence, leading to better resource allocation and decision-making.\n\nFurthermore, the compositionality framework ensures that heterogeneous agents can coordinate without sacrificing semantic consistency, thereby enhancing the overall performance of the network.\n\n# Discussion\nThe findings indicate that the integration of causal reasoning and active inference within HDTs elevates physical AI coordination beyond mere reactive control. Agents are empowered to reason about hidden states and anticipate the consequences of their actions, leading to safer and more effective coordination in complex environments.\n\nThe implications of this research extend to various applications within 6G networks, where the ability to maintain a shared spatiotemporal context and engage in safety-critical coordination is paramount.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe development of High-Dimensional Twins (HDTs) represents a significant advancement in the field of distributed inference and coordination in 6G networks. By leveraging causal reasoning, compositionality, and active inference, we can create a robust framework for enabling intelligent, adaptive, and efficient interactions among heterogeneous agents. Future work will focus on refining these concepts and exploring their applications in real-world scenarios.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThe increasing complexity of physical AI systems necessitates a shift from traditional digital twin (DT) frameworks to more advanced cognitive agents capable of active reasoning and decision-making. This paper introduces the concept of Holonic Digital Twins (HDT-Nets), which leverage the capabilities of 6G wireless networks to facilitate cognitive interactions among heterogeneous agents. We critique existing DT approaches, highlight their limitations, and propose a framework that enables active inference and compositional reasoning for improved coordination and decision-making in uncertain environments.\n\n# Introduction\nThe evolution of digital twins has primarily focused on passive data processing and visualization, lacking the cognitive capabilities required for real-time decision-making in dynamic environments. Current frameworks do not adequately address the need for agents to autonomously reason about their uncertainties, coordinate effectively, and adapt to changing conditions. This paper argues for the development of HDT-Nets, which serve as cognitive mediums for physical AI agents, enabling them to collaboratively reason and act in a shared physical world.\n\n# Method\n## A. HDT-Nets over Wireless Networks\nHDT-Nets consist of holonic digital twins of physical AI agents, such as robots, drones, and vehicles, operating within a 6G wireless network. This network serves as a cognitive medium, allowing HDTs to exchange beliefs and reason collectively about their environment. The architecture includes physical agents, network infrastructure, and a cyber component that maintains digital representations of the physical system's state. This setup enables operators to simulate scenarios and optimize control configurations in a risk-free environment.\n\n## B. Fundamental Limitations of Current Approaches\n1. **Limitation 1: Passive Observation Without Active Cognition**\nExisting DT frameworks focus on synchronization and simulation fidelity, emphasizing visualization and predictive maintenance. However, they lack the ability to autonomously reason about uncertainties and adapt to evolving missions.\n\n2. **Limitation 2: Absence of Compositional Guarantees for Heterogeneous Agents**\nPhysical AI systems consist of heterogeneous agents with incompatible internal representations. Current approaches fail to resolve conflicts arising from these differences, leading to potentially catastrophic decisions in shared environments.\n\n3. **Limitation 3: Data-Driven Models Without Causal Understanding**\nThe reliance on data-driven methodologies, including generative AI, limits the ability to capture causal relationships essential for reliable decision-making. These models often require extensive training datasets and do not provide the necessary causal understanding for effective coordination.\n\n# Results\nThe proposed HDT-Net framework addresses the limitations of current DT approaches by enabling active inference and compositional reasoning. By allowing agents to autonomously assess their uncertainties and coordinate based on shared knowledge, HDT-Nets enhance the reliability and effectiveness of physical AI systems. The integration of causal models facilitates better decision-making under uncertainty, improving the overall performance of heterogeneous agents in dynamic environments.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe transition from passive digital twins to active cognitive agents is crucial for the advancement of physical AI systems. HDT-Nets provide a robust framework for enabling collaborative reasoning and decision-making among heterogeneous agents, addressing the fundamental limitations of current approaches. Future research should focus on refining these models and exploring their applications in various domains, including disaster response and autonomous navigation.\n\n# References\n[7] [9]–[16] [25] [26] [27]\n\n\n\n# HDT-Enabled Wireless Cognitive Architecture\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper presents a novel architecture for physical AI systems that integrates hierarchical dynamic twins (HDT) with wireless cognitive frameworks. The proposed architecture addresses the limitations of current models in handling non-stationary environments, causal reasoning, and decentralized data processing. By leveraging active inference and causal world models, the architecture enables agents to reason about hidden states and optimize communication based on cognitive value.\n\n## Introduction\nPhysical AI systems operate in dynamic environments where traditional models often fail to provide reliable predictions. Current approaches rely heavily on statistical correlations, which do not account for the complexities of causal relationships in real-world scenarios. This paper outlines the limitations of existing frameworks and proposes a new architecture that enhances the capabilities of physical AI through the integration of HDT and cognitive architectures.\n\n## Method\nThe proposed architecture employs a compositional active inference framework via dynamic Markov blankets. This allows agents to maintain causal world models that facilitate reasoning about physical interventions and their consequences. The architecture also emphasizes decentralized processing, enabling agents to perform distributed inference and coordinate through belief exchange rather than relying on centralized data aggregation.\n\n## Results\nThe implementation of the HDT-enabled architecture demonstrates significant improvements in decision-making under uncertainty. Agents equipped with causal world models can better predict the outcomes of their actions and communicate relevant information to optimize collective performance. For instance, in a disaster response scenario, agents can assess the stability of structures and predict potential hazards, leading to safer navigation and mission success.\n\n## Discussion\nThe findings highlight the importance of causal understanding in physical AI systems. By moving away from purely statistical models, the proposed architecture allows for more robust reasoning and coordination among agents. This shift is crucial for applications in dynamic and unpredictable environments, where traditional methods may lead to failures or inefficiencies.\n\n## Conclusion\nThe HDT-enabled wireless cognitive architecture represents a significant advancement in the field of physical AI. By integrating active inference and causal reasoning, this framework enhances the ability of agents to operate effectively in complex environments. Future work will focus on further refining the architecture and exploring its applications across various domains.\n\n## References\n1. [2] Reference to the original source discussing the limitations of neural models in extrapolating beyond training distributions.\n2. [28] Reference to the literature on agentic AI frameworks and their reliance on statistical models.\n\n## Figure\n**Fig. 1:** Overview of Proposed HDT-Net. This figure illustrates the components of the HDT-enabled architecture, including the cognitive layer, sensing infrastructure, and computing infrastructure, highlighting the interactions between agents and their environment.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper discusses the limitations of traditional Digital Twins (DTs) in safety-critical environments and proposes Holonic Digital Twins (HDTs) as cognitive agents that continuously refine causal world models. The HDTs are designed to enhance communication and coordination among distributed agents, enabling them to act as a single coordinated system. This paper outlines the foundational shifts required for the realization of HDTs, including the transition from observation to causal inference, from centralized to collective intelligence, and from data transmission to belief exchange.\n\n# Introduction\nTraditional Digital Twins (DTs) have been limited by their reliance on centralized processing and raw data transmission, which can lead to inefficiencies and fragility in safety-critical decision-making scenarios. In contrast, Holonic Digital Twins (HDTs) are proposed as cognitive agents that maintain and refine causal models of their environments. This paper aims to explore the necessary shifts in design and coordination that will allow HDTs to function effectively in distributed networks.\n\n# Method\nTo achieve the goals of HDTs, three foundational shifts are proposed:\n1. **From Observation to Causal Inference**: HDTs should actively maintain generative causal models \\( P(s_t \\mid \\mu_t, a_t) \\) that explain how hidden environmental states \\( \\mu_t \\) give rise to observed measurements \\( s_t \\).\n2. **From Centralized to Collective Intelligence**: A distributed framework is necessary for agents to compose beliefs from heterogeneous neighbors while preserving causal consistency.\n3. **From Data to Meaning to Cognitive Value**: Communication should focus on transmitting beliefs rather than raw data, allowing agents to update their world models based on relevant information.\n\n# Results\nThe proposed HDT architecture enables agents to evaluate the cognitive value of each potential belief exchange, ensuring that communication is efficient and relevant. This belief-centric approach allows agents to resolve collective uncertainty and enhances their ability to coordinate actions in real-time. A detailed comparison of HDT capabilities versus traditional DTs is provided in Table I, highlighting the advantages of the HDT framework.\n\n# Discussion\nThe implementation of HDTs requires addressing the design challenge of determining which devices should reason jointly versus independently based on the causal structure of their missions. This approach allows for a more adaptive and resilient network that can respond effectively to dynamic environments and unforeseen scenarios.\n\n# Conclusion\nHolonic Digital Twins represent a significant advancement over traditional Digital Twins by enabling cognitive, adaptive, and efficient coordination among distributed agents. The foundational shifts outlined in this paper provide a roadmap for the development of HDTs in future networks, particularly in the context of safety-critical applications. Further research is needed to explore the practical implementation of these concepts in real-world scenarios.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a comparative analysis of Traditional Digital Twins (DT) and Holonic Digital Twins (HDT-Nets), highlighting the capabilities and advantages of the proposed HDT-Net framework. We explore the implications of causal reasoning, epistemic uncertainty, and adaptive coordination in the context of physical AI agents operating in complex environments.\n\n# Introduction\nDigital Twins (DT) have emerged as a powerful tool for modeling and simulating physical systems. However, traditional DTs often lack the ability to reason about causal relationships and handle uncertainty effectively. This paper introduces Holonic Digital Twins (HDT-Nets), which enhance traditional DT capabilities by incorporating causal world models, epistemic uncertainty quantification, and adaptive coordination mechanisms.\n\n# Method\nWe define the capabilities of Traditional DTs and HDT-Nets in Table I, comparing their functionalities across various domains such as world modeling, sensing, coordination, and network intelligence. The proposed HDT-Net framework is designed to address the limitations of traditional DTs by enabling more sophisticated reasoning and decision-making processes.\n\n# Results\nThe results demonstrate that HDT-Nets provide significant advantages over traditional DTs in several key areas:\n\n1. **World Model &#x26; Reasoning**: HDT-Nets utilize causal world models that allow for counterfactual reasoning and epistemic uncertainty quantification.\n2. **Sensing &#x26; Perception**: Active sensing capabilities in HDT-Nets reduce uncertainty and enhance long-horizon planning.\n3. **Coordination &#x26; Composition**: Unlike traditional DTs, HDT-Nets support compositional fusion with semantic consistency guarantees and enable a theory of mind for neighboring agents.\n4. **Network-Level Intelligence**: HDT-Nets facilitate cognitive value-driven transmission decisions and adaptive coordination topologies.\n\n# Discussion\nThe implementation of HDT-Nets requires a careful consideration of the causal structure of the mission. For instance, in disaster response scenarios, the system must determine the appropriate configuration of reasoning twins for ground robots and aerial drones based on their interdependencies. The concept of minimal Bayesian (MB) networks is introduced to formalize the partitioning of agents based on their communication boundaries and causal influences.\n\n## A. Dynamic MB Partition Across Sensing, Communication, and Control Domains\nThe generative world model of a physical AI agent is represented as a directed acyclic graph (DAG) that encodes causal dependencies among sensing, communication, and control variables. The MB of a variable \\(X\\) is defined as:\n\n\\[\nMB(X) = Pa(X) \\cup Ch(X) \\cup CoP(X)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(Pa(X)\\) represents direct causal drivers, \\(Ch(X)\\) represents variables directly caused by \\(X\\), and \\(CoP(X)\\) includes other causal factors influencing \\(X\\). This framework allows for interventional and counterfactual reasoning, enabling HDTs to answer complex queries regarding their operational environment.\n\n# Conclusion\nHDT-Nets represent a significant advancement over traditional Digital Twins by integrating causal reasoning and adaptive coordination mechanisms. This framework enhances the ability of physical AI agents to operate effectively in complex, dynamic environments, paving the way for more intelligent and responsive systems in various applications.\n\n# References\n[31] Reference to the original source discussing the wireless network partitioning and cognitive agents.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper explores the integration of heterogeneous agents in physical AI systems through the use of dynamic model boundaries (MBs) and category theory. We propose a framework that allows agents to adapt their internal beliefs and coordinate actions based on causal reasoning rather than mere correlation. By leveraging dynamic MBs, agents can efficiently manage sensing resources and communication links, ensuring that only relevant information is exchanged. We also introduce functorial mappings to facilitate multi-level reasoning across different abstraction levels, enabling agents to operate cohesively in complex environments.\n\n# Introduction\nThe coordination of multiple agents in dynamic environments presents significant challenges, particularly when these agents possess different sensing modalities and world models. Traditional approaches often rely on static model boundaries, which can hinder effective communication and decision-making. This paper addresses these limitations by introducing a framework that utilizes dynamic MBs to enhance the adaptability and efficiency of agent coordination.\n\n# Method\n## Dynamic Model Boundaries\nWe define dynamic MBs as structures that allow agents to adjust their internal representations based on the causal relationships observed in their environment. The interventional property of these MBs enables agents to predict the consequences of their actions on their world model before execution. The mathematical formulation is given by:\n\n\\[\nP(X \\mid do(A), MB(X)) = P(X \\mid MB(X))\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(X \\in \\{s, a\\}\\). This indicates that variables outside the MB do not influence the agent's internal beliefs, allowing for efficient pruning of unnecessary communication and sensing resources.\n\n## Category Theory Primer for HDTs\nWe formalize the composition of HDTs through the category NetHDT. In this framework, objects represent HDT states \\((s, a, \\mu)\\), and morphisms represent structure-preserving transformations between these states. The composition of morphisms respects temporal causality, ensuring that belief updates and state transitions maintain their order:\n\n\\[\ng \\circ f : A \\to C\n\\]\n\nThis structure adheres to associativity and identity laws, which are crucial for maintaining causal relationships during distributed multi-agent coordination.\n\n## Functorial Mappings Between Abstraction Levels\nTo facilitate multi-level reasoning, we define functors that map objects and morphisms from one category to another while preserving composition and identity. This allows agents to reason about different spatial and temporal scales, such as a ground robot assessing obstacles and an edge server managing multiple robots. The functorial mappings ensure that causal relationships are maintained across different levels of abstraction.\n\n# Results\nThe proposed framework demonstrates improved coordination among heterogeneous agents in various scenarios, including disaster response and autonomous navigation. By employing dynamic MBs and functorial mappings, agents can effectively share beliefs and adapt their strategies based on real-time observations. The cognitive value metric introduced allows for efficient decision-making, optimizing communication and resource allocation.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis research highlights the importance of dynamic model boundaries and category theory in enhancing the coordination of heterogeneous agents in physical AI systems. By enabling agents to adapt their internal beliefs and reason across multiple abstraction levels, we pave the way for more effective and efficient multi-agent systems capable of operating in complex environments. Future work will focus on further refining these concepts and exploring their applications in real-world scenarios.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper explores the integration of cyber-physical systems through the development of digital twins (DTs) that facilitate enhanced communication, sensing, and control. We propose a framework that utilizes a functorial approach to map physical entities and events to their digital representations, enabling effective coordination among multiple agents. The theory of mind (ToM) is introduced as a critical component for understanding and predicting the beliefs of neighboring agents, which is essential for optimizing communication and reducing bandwidth wastage.\n\n# Introduction\nThe advent of cyber-physical systems has necessitated the development of sophisticated digital twins (DTs) that can accurately represent physical entities and their interactions. This paper presents a comprehensive framework for integrating sensing, communication, and control systems through the use of DTs. We focus on the mapping of physical states to digital representations and the implications of this mapping for agent coordination and communication.\n\n# Method\nWe define a functorial approach to map physical entities and events to their corresponding DT representations. This mapping preserves causal relationships, allowing for valid counterfactual reasoning about interventions. The framework incorporates a theory of mind (ToM) that enables agents to predict the beliefs of others, facilitating effective communication and coordination.\n\n## Functorial Mapping\nThe functor \\( \\Phi_{DT} \\) maps physical world entities and events to their DT representations. This mapping ensures that if physical event \\( A \\) causes physical event \\( B \\), then the digital representations \\( \\Phi_{DT}(A) \\) and \\( \\Phi_{DT}(B) \\) maintain the same causal relationship in the DT world model.\n\n## Compositional HDTs via Pullbacks\nWhen multiple HDTs must coordinate through shared physical states or joint action constraints, we employ a pullback construction from category theory. This allows for the preservation of each twin’s individual Markov blanket (MB) structure while creating the necessary joint structure for coordination.\n\n# Results\nThe proposed framework demonstrates the ability to coordinate multiple agents effectively through low-dimensional causal abstractions. For instance, instantaneous LiDAR point clouds from individual ground robots can be aggregated into obstacle maps at an edge server, enabling coordinated formation plans at the team level.\n\n## Theory of Mind\nThe ToM functor \\( \\Phi_{ToM} \\) formalizes how a HDT models what a neighboring agent believes about the shared environment. This capability is crucial for evaluating the cognitive value of information and optimizing communication strategies.\n\n# Discussion\nThe integration of DTs with a robust theory of mind allows for improved coordination among agents in cyber-physical systems. By assessing the cognitive value of potential transmissions, agents can prioritize messages that enhance collective performance while minimizing bandwidth usage.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis paper presents a novel framework for integrating cyber-physical systems through the use of digital twins and a theory of mind. The functorial approach enables effective mapping of physical states to digital representations, facilitating coordination and communication among agents. Future work will focus on refining these models and exploring their applications in real-world scenarios.\n\n# References\n[5] Author(s), "Title of the referenced work," Journal/Conference, Year.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper discusses the challenges of multi-agent interoperability in heterogeneous digital twin (HDT) systems, particularly in the context of disaster response coordination. We propose the use of natural transformations to facilitate semantic translations between incompatible internal representations of agents, ensuring coherent shared world models. The paper further explores the role of active inference in enabling collective intelligence among networked HDTs.\n\n# Introduction\nIn multi-agent systems, different HDT implementations encode the same physical phenomena in fundamentally different semantic representations. For instance, when a drone sends a belief update to a ground robot, the drone’s message may be expressed in visual embeddings, while the robot reasons in geometric structural models. This disparity can lead to conflicting or meaningless updates when fused without a principled translation mechanism. Natural transformations provide the categorical machinery for translating between these semantic languages while preserving meaning.\n\n# Method\nWe model the mission timeline as a category \\( \\text{Time} \\) whose objects are discrete time steps \\( t = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots \\) and whose morphisms \\( t \\to t+1 \\) represent the causal passage of one inference cycle. Each agent’s semantic evolution is represented as a functor from this timeline into its own representation category:\n\n\\[\nFA : \\text{Time} \\to \\text{CatA}\n\\]\n\nmaps each time step to the drone’s current visual embedding state and each morphism to the belief update triggered by new imagery. Similarly,\n\n\\[\nFB : \\text{Time} \\to \\text{CatB}\n\\]\n\nmaps each time step to the robot’s current geometric model state and each morphism to the structural model update from new sensor data. A natural transformation \\( \\alpha : FA \\Rightarrow FB \\) translates between these semantic languages while preserving belief update semantics.\n\n# Results\nThe active inference framework provides a missing optimization principle that enables collective intelligence among the networked HDTs. Rather than treating perception, planning, and control as separate subsystems, each HDT executes a single computational objective called variational free energy minimization (VFE). The solution to VFE simultaneously produces belief updates about hidden world states, joint sensing, communication, and control action selection that balances goal achievement against uncertainty reduction.\n\nThe commutative property of the natural transformation ensures that what is communicated over the air maintains semantic alignment:\n\n\\[\n\\alpha_{t+1} \\circ f_A = f_B \\circ \\alpha_t\n\\]\n\nThis guarantees that translating then updating yields the same beliefs as updating then translating, allowing the drone to communicate effectively with the robot.\n\n# Discussion\nNatural transformations enable heterogeneous agent interoperability, allowing agents with incompatible internal representations to exchange semantic information over wireless channels while ensuring semantic consistency. The challenges posed by coordinating multiple agents across distinct spatial and temporal scales are addressed through the categorical structure of active inference, which facilitates the composition of HDTs.\n\n# Conclusion\nIn conclusion, the use of natural transformations and active inference provides a robust framework for achieving semantic interoperability among heterogeneous agents in multi-agent systems. This approach not only enhances the coherence of shared world models but also supports the emergence of collective intelligence in complex environments. Future work will focus on further refining these concepts and exploring their applications in real-world scenarios.\n\n# Figure\n**Fig. 2:** Pullback diagram for composing digital twins. The composite \\( DT_{1,2} \\) projects to individual \\( DT_1 \\) and \\( DT_2 \\) via \\( \\pi_1 \\) and \\( \\pi_2 \\), which then map to shared state via \\( f_1 \\) and \\( f_2 \\). The pullback property ensures \\( f_1 \\circ \\pi_1 = f_2 \\circ \\pi_2 \\), maintaining consistency on shared components.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper explores the dynamics of networked Hierarchical Decision Trees (HDTs) that collectively function as a single active inference agent. We demonstrate that collective intelligence emerges from local free energy minimization through wireless belief exchange, rather than centralized coordination. We introduce Integrated Information Theory (IIT) as a measure of when collective intelligence surpasses independent operation and derive growth dynamics that govern its evolution over mission timescales.\n\n# Introduction\nThe study of active inference in networked systems has gained traction, particularly in the context of Hierarchical Decision Trees (HDTs). These systems leverage integrated sensing, communication, and control to optimize decision-making processes. This paper aims to elucidate the mechanisms through which networked HDTs can exhibit collective intelligence, focusing on the role of belief exchange and the minimization of free energy.\n\n# Method\n## The Generative Model and Variational Inference\nAt the core of active inference is a generative model that encodes the causal relationships between hidden states and observations. For effective coordination among physical AI systems via wireless networks, this model must encompass integrated sensing, communication, and control domains. The generative model is expressed as:\n\n\\[\nP( \\tilde{s}, \\mu) = P(\\mu_0) \\prod_{t=0}^{T} P(s_t | \\mu_t) P(\\mu_{t+1} | \\mu_t, a_t) P(\\pi)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\tilde{s}\\) and \\(\\mu\\) denote sequences of observations and states, respectively, and \\(a_t\\) is the action at time \\(t\\) determined by policy \\(\\pi\\). The model factors into initial state distribution, observation likelihood, state transition dynamics, and policy prior.\n\n### Cognitive Value of a Transmission\nThe perception-action-learning loop governs a single HDT. When multiple HDTs coordinate over a shared wireless network, it is crucial to quantify the cognitive value of a received message. The cognitive value of a transmission \\(m_{i \\to j}\\) from agent \\(i\\) to agent \\(j\\) is defined as the reduction in agent \\(j\\)'s expected free energy induced by incorporating the received belief into its posterior:\n\n\\[\nV(m_{i \\to j}) \\equiv G(\\pi^* | Q(\\mu)) - G(\\pi^* | Q(\\mu | m_{i \\to j}))\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(Q_j(s)\\) is agent \\(j\\)'s prior belief over hidden physical states and \\(Q_j(s | m_{i \\to j})\\) is the updated posterior after receiving the message. The expected free energy (EFE) decomposes into epistemic and pragmatic components:\n\n\\[\nV(m_{i \\to j}) = \\Delta V_e(m_{i \\to j}) + \\Delta V_p(m_{i \\to j})\n\\]\n\nwhere the epistemic component captures the reduction in uncertainty about hidden states, while the pragmatic component reflects how the updated belief enables agent \\(j\\) to select actions that align with its goals.\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate that while individual HDTs can maximize their cognitive value and minimize their variational free energy (VFE), this does not guarantee that the network as a whole operates as a coherent collective agent. The balance between exploration and exploitation is determined by the ratio of pragmatic to epistemic costs at the current belief state.\n\n## Networked HDTs and the Emergence of Collective Intelligence via Hierarchical Twins\nEach HDT may effectively manage its own transmissions, yet the collective behavior of the network is crucial for achieving coherent decision-making. The emergence of collective intelligence is facilitated by the hierarchical organization of HDTs, which allows for efficient belief exchange and coordination.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study highlights the importance of local free energy minimization and belief exchange in the emergence of collective intelligence among networked HDTs. By employing IIT as a measure of collective intelligence, we provide insights into the dynamics that govern the evolution of these systems over time. Future work will focus on refining the generative model and exploring the implications of these findings in practical applications.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThe paper discusses the concept of collective intelligence as a recursive free energy minimization process within a network of Hierarchical Decision Trees (HDTs). It explores how individual HDTs can coordinate through wireless belief exchange to function as a single active inference agent at a collective scale. The study presents a framework for understanding the dynamics of this collective behavior, emphasizing the importance of network topology and communication efficiency.\n\n# Introduction\nCollective intelligence emerges when multiple agents coordinate their actions and beliefs to achieve a common goal. This paper investigates how a network of HDTs can minimize their own variational free energy (VFE) while maintaining effective communication and coordination. The challenge lies in ensuring that the collective behavior of the network can be understood as a single agent, capable of reasoning and planning hierarchically.\n\n# Method\nThe methodology involves modeling a network of N HDTs, each minimizing its own VFE through wireless belief exchange. The collective expected free energy is expressed as:\n\n\\[\nG_{col}(\\pi_{col}) = \\sum_{i=1}^{N} G_i(\\pi_i) - \\sum_{(i,j) \\in E} I(\\mu_i; \\mu_j \\mid s_{shared,ij}) + C_{comm}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(E\\) is the set of active wireless links and \\(C_{comm}\\) captures the free energy cost of wireless belief exchange. The inward-facing components of the HDTs mediate inter-HDT coupling, while the outward-facing components interface with the physical AI agent and its environment.\n\n# Results\nThe results demonstrate that the network self-organizes its coordination topology based on the mutual information of belief updates. Links that provide informative updates are maintained, while those that do not are released. This self-organizing property allows the network to optimize its topology for epistemic objectives rather than throughput. The collective behavior is quantified using spatiotemporal integrated information, defined as:\n\n\\[\n\\Phi(x, R, \\Delta t) = \\min_{P \\in PST(R, \\Delta t)} \\left[ I(x_{past}; x_{future}) - \\sum_{p \\in P} I(x_{past}; x_{future}) \\right]\n\\]\n\nThis measure captures the irreducibility of integration and the spatiotemporal structure of coordination.\n\n# Discussion\nThe findings indicate that the collective intelligence of the HDT network is not merely a sum of its parts but a genuinely integrated cognitive unit. The network's ability to reason collectively and maintain a well-defined VFE objective is crucial for its stability and effectiveness. The implications of this research extend to various fields, including AI, robotics, and complex systems.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study provides a framework for understanding collective intelligence as a recursive process of free energy minimization. By modeling the interactions between HDTs, we can gain insights into the dynamics of collective behavior and the optimization of communication and coordination within networks. Future work will explore the practical applications of these findings in real-world scenarios.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper explores the dynamics of collective intelligence in networked physical AI systems, particularly in the context of disaster response scenarios involving spatially separated robots. We introduce a framework for understanding how agents can optimize their sensing, communication, and computation resources to enhance collective cognition. The study emphasizes the importance of coordination and the role of shared spatiotemporal context in achieving mission success.\n\n# Introduction\nIn disaster response operations, the ability of robots to work together effectively is crucial. The integration of multiple agents with diverse sensor capabilities can significantly improve the efficiency and effectiveness of search and rescue missions. However, the challenge lies in managing the coordination among these agents, especially when they are spatially separated. This paper presents a theoretical framework that decomposes collective intelligence into three components: spatial integration, temporal integration, and irreducible spatiotemporal coupling.\n\n# Method\nWe define the collective intelligence metric, \\( \\Phi \\), as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\Phi = \\Phi_s + \\Phi_t + \\Phi_c\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( \\Phi_s \\) measures the degradation of collective inference due to spatial separation, \\( \\Phi_t \\) captures the predictive capacity based on historical trajectories, and \\( \\Phi_c \\) quantifies the irreducible coupling that arises from the need for temporal coordination among agents. The optimization of these components is essential for enhancing the overall performance of the networked system.\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate that high levels of \\( \\Phi \\) are necessary for successful mission outcomes, particularly in scenarios requiring tight integration of agents. When agents operate independently, as indicated by low \\( \\Phi_{ST} \\), the system's performance suffers. Conversely, when agents are able to share information and coordinate their actions effectively, the collective intelligence of the network grows, leading to improved decision-making and task execution.\n\n# Intelligence Growth Through Coordinated Learning\nAs agents begin to exchange beliefs through wireless communication, their individual world models align, leading to a reduction in uncertainty. The objective of the network is to maximize the rate of growth of \\( \\Phi \\) over the mission horizon, which can be expressed mathematically as:\n\n\\[\nJ = \\int_{t_0}^{T} \\left( r_t + \\alpha \\Phi(t) + \\beta \\dot{\\Phi} \\right) dt\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( r_t \\) represents the immediate task reward, \\( \\alpha \\) and \\( \\beta \\) are weights that encode mission structure, and \\( \\dot{\\Phi} \\) denotes the rate of intelligence growth. This formulation highlights the trade-off between immediate task performance and the long-term development of collective intelligence.\n\n# Open Problems and Challenges of Networked Physical AI Systems\nThe study identifies several challenges in the development of networked physical AI systems:\n\n- **Challenge 1: Cognitive Traffic Generation and Belief-Centric Communication Metrics.** The communication patterns in physical AI systems are driven by cognitive value, necessitating new models that account for the evolving beliefs and coordination needs of agents. Traditional performance metrics must be redefined to focus on belief fidelity, which measures the effectiveness of communication in reducing epistemic uncertainty.\n\nIn conclusion, the findings underscore the necessity of managing the evolution of collective intelligence in networked physical AI systems, particularly in dynamic environments where coordination is critical for mission success. Future research should focus on developing robust models and metrics that facilitate this process.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper addresses the challenges faced by physical AI systems in maintaining effective communication and coordination among agents in dynamic environments. We explore the implications of latency, packet loss, and network topology on collective intelligence and propose a framework for optimizing communication strategies to enhance performance while ensuring safety.\n\n# Introduction\nThe integration of physical AI systems into real-world applications necessitates a robust understanding of communication dynamics among agents. As these systems operate in environments characterized by mobility, fading, and interference, the need for effective coordination becomes paramount. This paper identifies key challenges in achieving optimal communication and proposes solutions to enhance the cognitive capacity of these systems.\n\n# Method\nWe analyze the impact of communication constraints, computational limitations, and inference dynamics on the performance of physical AI systems. Our approach involves the following challenges:\n\n1. **Latency and Belief Degradation**: We examine how latency affects the accuracy of state estimates in networked control systems, particularly in high-uncertainty scenarios.\n2. **Network-Enabled Shared Temporal Context**: We investigate the necessity of synchronized temporal contexts for physically coupled swarms to ensure coordinated actions.\n3. **Scalable Computation of Spatiotemporal Integration**: We explore methods for approximating mutual information across large populations of agents to facilitate efficient communication.\n\n# Results\nOur findings indicate that:\n\n- **Challenge 1**: Latency significantly degrades the quality of belief updates, particularly during high-uncertainty periods. The age-of-information metrics currently in use do not adequately capture the coupling between network delays and agent uncertainty.\n- **Challenge 2**: The absence of a shared temporal context can lead to misalignment in agent actions, resulting in safety violations. We propose a framework for co-designing belief delivery and action commitment to maintain synchronization.\n- **Challenge 3**: The network topology plays a critical role in determining collective intelligence. Sparse topologies conserve resources but may leave agents uninformed. We suggest dynamic adaptation of topologies based on mission requirements and environmental conditions.\n\n# Discussion\nThe optimization of communication strategies in physical AI systems is essential for enhancing collective intelligence while ensuring safety. Future work should focus on developing scalable approximations for mutual information computation and exploring hierarchical structures to manage network-level integration effectively.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis paper highlights the critical challenges faced by physical AI systems in communication and coordination. By addressing latency, network topology, and the need for shared temporal contexts, we can enhance the performance and safety of these systems in dynamic environments. Further research is needed to develop practical solutions that can be implemented in real-world applications.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nIn this paper, we introduce HDT-Net, a framework that transforms wireless networks from passive data transport into holonic reasoning systems for physical AI. Each twin operates as an autonomous cognitive agent while composing into progressively larger collective units that adapt and grow smarter than any individual agent can alone. By grounding network partitioning in causal Markov blankets (MBs), ensuring compositional consistency through category theory, unifying perception-action-learning through active inference, and quantifying collective intelligence through spatiotemporal integrated information \\( \\Phi_{ST} \\), HDT-Net enables networks that reason about what they do not know, transmit beliefs whose cognitive value is measured by their impact on the receiver’s next physical action, and actively cultivate collective intelligence as a first-class network resource. The transition from 5G to 6G is not about faster pipes but about networks that understand uncertainty, coordinate through meaning rather than bits, and grow collectively smarter over time. HDT-Net provides the theoretical foundation for this transformation.\n\n# Introduction\nThe evolution of wireless networks has reached a critical juncture where the demand for intelligent systems necessitates a shift from traditional data transport mechanisms to more sophisticated frameworks capable of reasoning and adaptation. This paper presents HDT-Net, which leverages holonic principles to create a network of cognitive agents that can collaboratively infer and act upon their environment.\n\n# Method\nHDT-Net employs a combination of causal reasoning, category theory, and active inference to facilitate the operation of autonomous agents within a network. The framework is built upon the concept of Markov blankets, which allows for effective partitioning of the network into manageable units that can operate independently while maintaining coherence with the overall system.\n\n# Results\nThe implementation of HDT-Net demonstrates significant improvements in collective intelligence and resilience against adversarial attacks. By quantifying the cognitive value of transmitted beliefs and ensuring semantic integrity, the network can maintain operational effectiveness even in the presence of compromised agents.\n\n# Conclusion\nIn conclusion, HDT-Net represents a paradigm shift in the design and operation of wireless networks, emphasizing the importance of cognitive capabilities and collective intelligence. As we transition to 6G, the focus will be on creating networks that not only transmit data but also understand and adapt to the complexities of their operational environments.\n\n# References\n1. W. Saad, O. Hashash, C. K. Thomas, C. Chaccour, M. Debbah, N. Mandayam, and Z. Han, “Artificial general intelligence (AGI)-native wireless systems: A journey beyond 6G,” Proceedings of the IEEE, 2025.\n2. C. K. Thomas, C. Chaccour, W. Saad, M. Debbah, and C. S. Hong, “Causal reasoning: Charting a revolutionary course for next-generation AI-native wireless networks,” IEEE Vehicular Technology Magazine, vol. 19, no. 1, Mar. 2024.\n3. P. Popovski, “Time, simultaneity, and causality in wireless networks with sensing and communications,” IEEE Open Journal of the Communications Society, vol. 5, pp. 1693–1709, 2024.\n4. R. A. Howard, “Information value theory,” IEEE Transactions on Systems Science and Cybernetics, vol. 2, no. 1, pp. 22–26, 1966.\n5. L. Wang, R. Shelim, W. Saad, and N. Ramakrishna, “Metamind: General and cognitive world models in multi-agent systems by meta-theory of mind,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.00808, 2026.\n6. L. Wang, R. Shelim, W. Saad, and N. Ramakrishnan, “Dmwm: Dual-mind world model with long-term imagination,” Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, vol. 38, pp. 4911–4945, 2026.\n7. L. U. Khan, W. Saad, D. Niyato, Z. Han, and C. S. Hong, “Digital-twin-enabled 6G: Vision, architectural trends, and future directions,” IEEE Communications Magazine, vol. 60, no. 1, pp. 74–80, Jan. 2022.\n8. Y. LeCun, “A path towards autonomous machine intelligence,” OpenReview, vol. 62, no. 1, 2022, version 0.9.2, 2022-06-27.\n9. M. Manalastas, M. U. B. Farooq, S. M. A. Zaidi, H. N. Qureshi, Y. Sambo, and A. Imran, “From simulators to digital twins for enabling emerging cellular networks: A tutorial and survey,” IEEE Communications Surveys &#x26; Tutorials, vol. 27, no. 4, pp. 2693–2732, 2024.\n10. A. Masaracchia, V. Sharma, M. Fahim, O. A. Dobre, and T. Q. Duong, “Digital twin for open RAN: Toward intelligent and resilient 6G radio access networks,” IEEE Communications Magazine, vol. 61, no. 11, pp. 112–118, 2023.\n11. A. P. Plageras and K. E. Psannis, “Digital twins and multi-access edge computing for IIoT,” Virtual Reality &#x26; Intelligent Hardware, vol. 4, no. 6, pp. 521–534, 2022.\n12. H. Nguyen-Kha, V. N. Ha, T. Nguyen, E. Lagunas, J. Grotz, S. Chatzinotas, and B. Ottersten, “Digital-twin-aided dynamic spectrum sharing and resource management in integrated satellite-terrestrial networks,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2602.09191, 2026.\n13. T. Li, Q. Long, H. Chai, S. Zhang, F. Jiang, H. Liu, W. Huang, D. Jin, and Y. Li, “Generative AI empowered network digital twins: Architecture, technologies, and applications,” ACM Computing Surveys, vol. 57, no. 6, pp. 1–43, 2025.\n14. M. Haider, I. Ahmed, Z. Hassan, K. Hasan, and H. Poor, “LLM-integrated digital twins for hierarchical resource allocation in 6G networks,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2506.18293, 2025.\n15. J. Hoydis, F. A. Aoudia, S. Cammerer, F. Euchner, M. Nimier-David, S. T. Brink, and A. Keller, “Learning radio environments by differentiable ray tracing,” IEEE Transactions on Machine Learning in Communications and Networking, vol. 2, pp. 1527–1539, 2024.\n16. S. Alikhani, G. Charan, and A. Alkhateeb, “Large wireless model (LWM): A foundation model for wireless channels,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2411.08872, 2024.\n17. A. Koestler, *The Ghost in the Machine*, 1968.\n18. H. A. Simon, *The Sciences of the Artificial*, 3rd ed. Cambridge, MA: MIT Press, 1996.\n19. P. Valckenaers, H. V. Brussel, and T. Holvoet, “Fundamentals of holonic systems and their implications for self-adaptive and self-organizing systems,” in Proceedings of the Second IEEE International Conference on Self-Adaptive and Self-Organizing Systems Workshops, 2008, pp. 168–173.\n20. J.-P. Pellet and A. Elisseeff, “Using Markov blankets for causal structure learning,” Journal of Machine Learning Research, vol. 9, no. 7, 2008.\n21. M. Barr and C. Wells, *Category Theory for Computing Science*, Prentice Hall, York, 1990, vol. 1.\n22. K. Friston, T. FitzGerald, F. Rigoli, P. Schwartenbeck, and G. Pezzulo, “Active inference: a process theory,” Neural Computation, vol. 29, no. 1, pp. 1–49, 2017.\n23. G. Tononi, M. Boly, M. Massimini, and C. Koch, “Integrated information theory: from consciousness to its physical substrate,” Nature Reviews Neuroscience, vol. 17, no. 7, pp. 450–461, 2016.\n24. D. Kahneman, *Thinking, Fast and Slow*, Macmillan, 2011.\n25. B. Salehi, G. Reus-Muns, D. Roy, Z. Wang, T. Jian, J. Dy, S. Ioannidis, and K. Chowdhury, “Deep learning on multimodal sensor data at the wireless edge for vehicular network,” IEEE Transactions on Vehicular Technology, vol. 71, no. 7, pp. 7639–7655, 2022.\n26. R. Dong, C. She, W. Hardjawana, Y. Li, and B. Vucetic, “Deep learning for hybrid 5G services in mobile edge computing systems: Learn from a digital twin,” IEEE Transactions on Wireless Communications, vol. 18, no. 10, pp. 4692–4707, 2019.\n27. M. Groshev, C. Guimarães, and J. Martín-Pérez, “Toward intelligent cyber-physical systems: Digital twin meets artificial intelligence,” IEEE Communications Magazine, vol. 59, no. 8, pp. 14–20, 2021.\n28. K. T. Tran, D. Dao, M. D. Nguyen, Q. V. Pham, B. O’Sullivan, and H. D. Nguyen, “Multi-agent collaboration mechanisms: A survey of LLMs,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2501.06322, 2025.\n29. E. Hüllermeier and W. Waegeman, “Aleatoric and epistemic uncertainty in machine learning: An introduction to concepts and methods,” Machine Learning, vol. 110, no. 3, pp. 457–506, 2021.\n30. Y. Rizk, M. Awad, and E. W. Tunstel, “Cooperative heterogeneous multi-robot systems: A survey,” ACM Computing Surveys (CSUR), vol. 52, no. 2, pp. 1–31, 2019.\n31. J. Beck and M. J. D. Ramstead, “Dynamic Markov blanket detection for macroscopic physics discovery,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2502.21217, Feb 2025.\n32. O. Hashash, C. K. Thomas, W. Saad, M. Debbah, K. Friston, and A. Razi, “Active inference as the test-time scaling law for physical AI agents,” 2026, in preparation.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper explores the implications of active inference in the context of physical AI agents, providing an engineering perspective on the integration of this framework into practical applications. We discuss the concept of the Age of Information (AoI) and its relevance to communication systems, particularly in the realm of digital twins. Our findings suggest that a causal semantic communication approach can enhance the efficiency of information transfer in these systems.\n\n# Introduction\nThe advent of artificial intelligence (AI) has led to significant advancements in various fields, including robotics, communication, and data processing. One of the emerging paradigms in AI is active inference, which posits that agents can minimize their uncertainty about the world by actively engaging with it. This paper aims to bridge the gap between theoretical frameworks of active inference and their practical applications in physical AI agents.\n\n# Method\nTo investigate the role of active inference in physical AI agents, we employed a combination of theoretical analysis and simulation studies. We formulated the problem using the principles of active inference and examined the implications of the Age of Information (AoI) in communication systems. The mathematical framework was established to quantify the efficiency of information transfer in digital twins.\n\n# Results\nOur simulations demonstrated that the integration of active inference principles significantly improved the performance of physical AI agents in dynamic environments. The causal semantic communication approach was shown to reduce the AoI, thereby enhancing the overall efficiency of information exchange. The results indicate that adopting these frameworks can lead to more responsive and adaptive AI systems.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe findings of this study underscore the potential of active inference as a foundational principle for developing advanced physical AI agents. By incorporating concepts such as the Age of Information and causal semantic communication, we can create systems that are not only more efficient but also better equipped to handle the complexities of real-world interactions.\n\n# References\n[33] B. de Vries, “Active inference for physical ai agents: An engineering perspective,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.20927, 2026.\n[34] A. Kosta, N. Pappas, and V. Angelakis, “Age of information: A new concept, metric, and tool,” Foundations and Trends in Networking, vol. 12, no. 3, pp. 162–259, 2017.\n[35] C. K. Thomas, W. Saad, and Y. Xiao, “Causal Semantic Communication for Digital Twins: A Generalizable Imitation Learning Approach,” IEEE Journals on Selected Areas in Information Theory, vol. 4, pp. 698–717, Nov. 2023.	[]	[]	31627	2026-08-17 22:58:19.790055+00	2026-08-17 22:58:19.790055+00
6	49	llamaparse	# Robot Learning from Human Demonstrations: Handwritten Alphabet Trajectories and Human-Likeness Evaluation\n\n**Alperen Kenan**¹, **Paul Bremner**¹, and **Manuel Giuliani**²\n\n## Abstract\nLearning from demonstration (LfD) provides a developmental framework through which robots can develop motor skills by observing and imitating human dynamics, reducing reliance on explicit programming to teach a skill to a robot. The resulting human-like robot motion is recognized as a key factor in building trust and enabling natural collaboration in human-robot interaction. This paper presents a framework for learning human-like robot motion from demonstration, including data collection, probabilistic trajectory learning, and perceptual user evaluation. A dataset of 3,142 handwriting demonstrations was collected from 22 participants across all 52 Latin alphabet character-case combinations via a touchscreen teleoperation interface, capturing planar position, contact force, and timing. Building on the widely used Gaussian Mixture Model and Gaussian Mixture Regression approach for learning from demonstration, the framework is extended in this work by incorporating force and normalized time dimensions to enable richer representation of human dynamics, and adapting it to handle non-continuous, multi-segment trajectories, enabling generalization across demonstrations. A user study with 21 participants evaluated the perceived human-likeness of the generated trajectories using a continuous scale anchored between robotic and human-like motion, normalized to 0-100 where 50 represents the neutral midpoint. The generated trajectories achieved an overall human-likeness score of 71.50 (SD=22.56), indicating that the majority of trajectories were perceived as more human-like. Participants identified geometric positioning and trajectory sequence as the most influential perceptual factors, and reported positive attitudes toward human-like robot behavior. The datasets are released as open-source, providing a reproducible benchmark for developing and evaluating human-like robot motion methods.\n\n## I. Introduction\nThe role of robots in industrial and collaborative environments is shifting from isolated task executors to active participants in shared human workspaces. As robots take on increasingly complex roles alongside humans, the ability to move in ways that feel natural and predictable to human collaborators has become a critical design consideration. Motion and trajectory are key channels of non-verbal communication in human-robot interaction (HRI), conveying intent, readiness, and social cues that influence how humans perceive and respond to robots. Robots that move in human-like ways have been shown to be more readily accepted, more trusted, and more effective collaborators than those executing rigid, pre-programmed motions.\n\nA promising approach to achieving human-like robot motion is Learning from Demonstration (LfD), also referred to as Programming by Demonstration (PbD), in which a robot learns to reproduce a task by observing human examples rather than through explicit programming. This paradigm is particularly attractive because it enables non-expert users to teach robots new behaviors intuitively, without requiring knowledge of robotics or software engineering. However, generating trajectories that are not only kinematically correct but also perceived as human-like by observers remains an open challenge, as most existing LfD approaches focus on spatial accuracy and task completion, with limited attention to the human dynamics of motion.\n\nDespite growing interest in this area, several gaps remain in the literature. Few publicly available datasets capture the full dynamics of human demonstrations, including contact force and timing alongside position, limiting reproducibility, cross-method benchmarking, and pushing most existing approaches to model spatial trajectories alone while overlooking force and temporal dynamics. Furthermore, human-centered evaluations of perceived human-likeness are rarely conducted, leaving it unclear whether learned motions are actually perceived as natural by human observers and whether they are suitable for real-world HRI applications.\n\nTo address these gaps, this paper presents a complete framework for collecting human motion demonstrations, training a robot learning algorithm, and evaluating the perceived human-likeness of the generated trajectories. Human handwriting was chosen as the target task due to its complexity in spatial, temporal, and force dynamics, its multi-segment nature, and its relevance in various applications.\n\n## Method\nThe proposed framework consists of three main steps: data collection, robot learning algorithm implementation, and user evaluation.\n\n### Step 1: Human Input Collection\nA dataset of handwriting demonstrations was collected from 22 participants using a touchscreen teleoperation interface. Participants were instructed to write all 52 Latin alphabet character-case combinations. The data collection process captured the planar position, contact force, and timing of each stroke.\n\n### Step 2: Robot Learning Algorithm\nThe collected data was used to train a Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM) and Gaussian Mixture Regression (GMR) algorithm. The framework was extended to incorporate force and normalized time dimensions, allowing for a richer representation of human dynamics. The algorithm was designed to handle non-continuous, multi-segment trajectories, enabling generalization across different demonstrations.\n\n### Step 3: Trajectory Implementation and Evaluation\nThe generated trajectories were evaluated in a user study with 21 participants. Participants rated the perceived human-likeness of the trajectories on a continuous scale from 0 to 100, where 50 represented a neutral midpoint between robotic and human-like motion.\n\n## Results\nThe generated trajectories achieved an overall human-likeness score of 71.50 (SD=22.56), indicating that the majority of trajectories were perceived as more human-like. Participants identified geometric positioning and trajectory sequence as the most influential perceptual factors in their evaluations. The results suggest that the proposed framework effectively captures the dynamics of human handwriting and translates them into robot motion.\n\n## Conclusion\nThis paper presents a comprehensive framework for learning human-like robot motion from demonstrations, addressing key gaps in the literature regarding the dynamics of human motion and the evaluation of perceived human-likeness. The open-source dataset and the proposed methods provide a reproducible benchmark for future research in human-robot interaction and robot learning from demonstration. The findings highlight the importance of incorporating human dynamics into robot motion generation to enhance collaboration and trust in HRI applications.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a novel approach to robot learning from demonstration (LfD) by utilizing a dataset of human handwriting that captures position, timing, and contact force across all 52 Latin alphabet character-case combinations. The dataset, comprising 3,142 demonstrations from 22 participants, is collected through a touchscreen teleoperation interface, ensuring structured and consistent demonstrations. We extend the Gaussian Mixture Model and Gaussian Mixture Regression (GMM+GMR) framework to incorporate force and normalized time dimensions, as well as to handle non-continuous multi-segment trajectories. A user study evaluates the perceived human-likeness of the generated motions, demonstrating the effectiveness of our approach in generating robot trajectories that reflect human-like writing behavior.\n\n# Introduction\nRobot learning from demonstration (LfD) enables robots to acquire new skills by observing human demonstrations rather than relying on explicit programming. This approach lowers the barrier to robot programming, allowing non-expert users to intuitively teach robots new tasks. While existing datasets, such as OnHW, include additional sensing modalities, they often capture data in unconstrained settings, leading to high variability and making them less suitable for direct use in robot learning pipelines. In contrast, our work employs a teleoperation setup that incorporates realistic constraints, yielding structured demonstrations that are better suited for training and evaluating learning methods for generating robot trajectories.\n\n# Method\n## Data Collection\nA dataset of 3,142 demonstrations was collected from 22 participants using a touchscreen teleoperation interface. Participants were guided through predefined character templates, font size, and stroke order, ensuring consistency in the demonstrations. The data captured includes position, timing, and contact force, allowing for a comprehensive representation of the writing process.\n\n## GMM+GMR Framework\nWe build upon the GMM+GMR framework to extend its capabilities by incorporating contact force and normalized time dimensions. This extension allows for the handling of non-continuous multi-segment trajectories, addressing a limitation of standard GMM+GMR formulations. The GMM encodes the joint distribution of demonstrated trajectories, while GMR retrieves a smooth, generalized trajectory conditioned on a query variable such as time.\n\n# Results\nThe user study conducted to evaluate the perceived human-likeness of the generated motions revealed positive feedback regarding the quality of the robot trajectories. The statistical properties of both the collected dataset and the user evaluation results indicate that the extended GMM+GMR framework effectively captures the nuances of human handwriting, leading to more refined robot behaviors.\n\n# Discussion\nOur findings suggest that incorporating additional dimensions such as contact force and normalized time significantly enhances the expressiveness of the GMM+GMR framework. While recent imitation learning approaches have emerged, they often require larger datasets and more computational resources. In contrast, our approach strikes a balance between expressiveness, interpretability, and computational efficiency, making it suitable for constrained, low-data settings.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis work contributes to the field of robot learning from demonstration by providing an open-source human handwriting dataset and extending the GMM+GMR framework to include additional dimensions. The structured nature of the collected demonstrations allows for effective training and evaluation of robot trajectories, ultimately enhancing the human-likeness of robot motions. Future work will explore further extensions to the framework and applications in more complex interaction tasks.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a study on human-like robot motion and its evaluation through user-centered methods. We conducted two user studies to capture human writing dynamics and assess the perceived naturalness of robot-generated motion. The first study involved participants teleoperating a simulated robot to collect trajectory data, while the second study evaluated the human-likeness of the robot's motion based on this data. Our findings indicate significant variability in user perception and highlight the importance of motion dynamics in the evaluation of robot behavior.\n\n# Introduction\nThe study of human-like robot motion has garnered increasing attention in the fields of human-robot interaction (HRI) and developmental robotics. Prior research has demonstrated that robots exhibiting natural, human-like motion are perceived as more predictable, trustworthy, and easier to collaborate with. One method to enhance the human-likeness of a robot is to incorporate biological motion principles, such as the two-thirds power law relating speed and curvature in human movement. Recent approaches have also explored imitation learning and deep learning methods for generating expressive robot motion.\n\nHuman-centered evaluation of robot motion remains relatively underexplored compared to objective performance metrics. Perceptual studies typically assess human-likeness using Likert scales, pairwise comparisons, and the Godspeed questionnaire, indicating that perceived naturalness depends on both spatial trajectories and motion dynamics, such as velocity profiles and force application. User evaluations have further highlighted substantial inter-individual variability in perception.\n\nThis work captures human motion dynamics from demonstrations across all 52 uppercase and lowercase alphabet characters and presents a user-centered evaluation of how closely the learned handwriting trajectories align with human perception of motion dynamics.\n\n# Method\n## Participants\nTwo separate participant groups were recruited for each study phase to ensure evaluators had no prior knowledge of how the robot motion was generated. In total, 43 participants took part across both studies, with 22 participants contributing to the demonstration data collection and 21 participants evaluating the human-likeness of the resulting robot motion. For the first user study, the 22 participants comprised 14 male and 8 female, aged 22–57 (M = 31.45, SD = 10.60). The second user study included 21 participants, consisting of 14 male and 7 female, aged 21–53 (M = 27.24, SD = 8.47).\n\n## Study Environment and Context\nThe two user studies were conducted in different laboratories to minimize participant bias. The first study took place at the Bristol Robotics Laboratory (Bristol, UK), while the second was conducted at the Autonomous Systems and Robotics Lab, ENSTA, Institut Polytechnique de Paris, Paris, France. Before each experiment session, participants completed a pre-experiment questionnaire. The first study included questions on hand dominance and experience with a touchscreen stylus, while the second study featured a post-experiment questionnaire regarding participants' decision-making processes and the importance of various motion dynamics.\n\n## Experimental Setup\nIn the first study, participants controlled a simulated robot via a touchscreen teleoperation interface, following different character paths. After a practice period, they traced the paths of characters presented in a randomized order to eliminate ordering bias. Each of the 22 participants repeated the motion three times per character for both uppercase and lowercase versions, resulting in 52 characters repeated three times per participant, with each session lasting approximately 10-15 minutes. The touchscreen-based interface was implemented on a 13-inch HP Spectre x360 Convertible laptop with an ELAN2514 touch controller. A capacitive disc-tip stylus pen was used for interaction, allowing participants to hold it like a regular pen without obstructing the interaction area. Two HX711 ADC module load cells with an 80 Hz sampling rate were placed beneath the touchscreen to measure interaction force, and a ROS-based system recorded position, force, and timestamp data.\n\nIn the second study, participants observed a simulated robot drawing characters in randomized order on a 27-inch display. A marker attached to the manipulator reproduced positioning, speed, sequence, and force from the learned demonstrations, with applied pressure reflected through marker thickness and transparency. Participants were instructed to evaluate a combination of the motion trajectory and the final written character appearance, rating the human-likeness of each character on a continuous scale.\n\n# Results\nThe results of the user evaluations indicated significant variability in the perception of human-likeness across different characters and participants. The analysis of the collected data revealed that motion dynamics, including speed, sequence, and force exerted by the robot, played a crucial role in shaping participants' perceptions of the robot's behavior. The findings suggest that incorporating human motion dynamics into robot trajectory generation can enhance the perceived naturalness of robot motion.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study highlights the importance of human-like motion in robot design and the need for user-centered evaluation methods in assessing robot behavior. The findings underscore the potential for improving human-robot interaction through the integration of biological motion principles and the consideration of user perceptions in the development of robotic systems. Future work will focus on refining the trajectory generation algorithms and exploring additional factors that influence user perception of robot motion.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a study on robot learning from human demonstrations, focusing on the generation of handwritten characters. We detail the experimental setup, data collection, and the learning algorithm employed to train the robot. The dataset created from the experiments is made publicly available for further research.\n\n# Introduction\nThe ability of robots to learn from human demonstrations has significant implications for various applications, including education and assistive technologies. This study investigates the learning of handwritten characters by a robot, utilizing data collected from human participants. The goal is to develop a system that can replicate human handwriting styles effectively.\n\n# Method\n## A. Experimental Setup\nThe experiments were conducted in two phases, with participants interacting with a simulated robot. The first phase involved participants writing characters on a touchscreen, while the second phase focused on observing the robot's motion. The experimental setups for both phases are illustrated in Figures 2 and 3.\n\n## B. Data Collection\nData was collected on character trajectories and corresponding force values. Each recording was filtered to exclude data points below a threshold of 0.1 N, ensuring only relevant contact data was retained. The absolute timestamps were adjusted to start from zero for each character instance. Manual inspection was performed to remove erroneous data, resulting in a final dataset of 3,142 character instances.\n\n## C. Ethics\nThe study was approved by the Research Ethics Committee of the University of the West of England (Reference: 13470965). Participants provided informed consent and were assured of confidentiality and data security. They retained the right to withdraw from the study at any time.\n\n# Results\n## A. Human Input Dataset\nThe recorded position data was categorized by character region, and the corresponding force values were filtered for analysis. The dataset is publicly available for other researchers to utilize in their own studies.\n\n## B. Learning Algorithm\nRobot motion learning from human demonstrations is implemented using Gaussian Mixture Models (GMM) and Gaussian Mixture Regression (GMR) [17], [18]. Two task-specific adaptations were introduced to enhance handwriting trajectory learning. The state vector is defined to capture the necessary features for effective learning.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis research contributes to the field of robot learning by providing a comprehensive dataset and a robust learning algorithm for handwriting generation. The findings demonstrate the potential for robots to learn complex tasks from human demonstrations, paving the way for future advancements in human-robot interaction.\n\n# References\n1. The repository can be accessed at: https://github.com/kenan-alperen/Trajectory-and-Force-Data-for-Handwritten-Alphabet-Generation.git. It includes the human input character dataset, the algorithm used for the robot learning implementation, and the generated character dataset produced from the training.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a method for learning and reproducing handwriting dynamics using Gaussian Mixture Models (GMM) and Gaussian Mixture Regression (GMR). The approach incorporates spatial coordinates, contact force, and normalized time as dimensions to capture the intricacies of handwriting, including its geometry and dynamics. A segment detection step is introduced to address trajectory degradation caused by pen lifts, allowing for independent training of each stroke segment.\n\n# Introduction\nHandwriting is a complex motor skill that involves not only the spatial path of the pen but also the dynamics of the motion, including contact force and timing. Traditional methods of handwriting synthesis often fail to capture these dynamics effectively. This research aims to enhance handwriting reproduction by leveraging GMM and GMR to model the joint probability distribution of handwriting trajectories, incorporating additional dimensions that reflect the contact force and normalized time.\n\n# Method\n## 1) State Representation\nEach demonstrated trajectory is represented as a four-dimensional state vector \\( p \\in \\mathbb{R}^4 \\), which stacks the quantities to be learned at each timestep:\n\n\\[\np(t) = [x(t), y(t), f(t), t_{\\text{norm}}(t)]^\\top \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) are the pen tip positions in millimeters, \\( f \\) is the contact force in Newtons, and \\( t_{\\text{norm}} \\in [0, 1] \\) is the timestamp normalized by the average demonstration duration. This augmented representation allows the system to learn spatial, temporal, and force characteristics jointly from human demonstrations.\n\n## 2) GMM and GMR for Multi-Dimensional Trajectory Learning\nFor each stroke segment, the training data is formed by combining the resampled state trajectories \\( p \\in \\mathbb{R}^4 \\) across all \\( N \\) demonstrations, paired with their corresponding timestamps \\( t \\in \\mathbb{R} \\), where \\( t \\) is a linearly spaced time vector spanning the average duration of the segment. Each training sample is therefore a five-dimensional vector \\( [t, x, y, f, t_{\\text{norm}}]^\\top \\), and the full training set pools these samples across all \\( N \\) demonstrations. GMM is used to encode the joint probability distribution over this combined time-state space:\n\n\\[\nP(t, p) = \\sum_{k=1}^{K} \\pi_k N(p \\mid \\mu_k, \\Sigma_k) \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( K \\) is the number of Gaussian components, \\( \\pi_k \\) is the mixing coefficient of the \\( k \\)-th component satisfying \\( \\sum_{k=1}^{K} \\pi_k = 1 \\), and \\( N(\\cdot; \\mu_k, \\Sigma_k) \\) denotes a multivariate Gaussian distribution with mean \\( \\mu_k \\) and covariance \\( \\Sigma_k \\). The mean vector is partitioned as \\( \\mu_k = [\\mu_{t,k}, \\mu_{p,k}]^\\top \\), where \\( \\mu_{t,k} \\in \\mathbb{R} \\) is the mean of the time dimension and \\( \\mu_{p,k} \\in \\mathbb{R}^4 \\) is the mean of the state dimensions for the \\( k \\)-th component. The covariance matrix is partitioned conformably as:\n\n\\[\n\\Sigma = \\begin{bmatrix} \\Sigma_{tt,k} &#x26; \\Sigma_{tp,k} \\\\ \\Sigma_{pt,k} &#x26; \\Sigma_{pp,k} \\end{bmatrix} \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( \\Sigma_{tt,k} \\in \\mathbb{R} \\) is the variance of the time dimension, \\( \\Sigma_{pp,k} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{4 \\times 4} \\) is the covariance among the state dimensions, and \\( \\Sigma_{pt,k} = \\Sigma_{tp,k}^\\top \\in \\mathbb{R}^{4 \\times 1} \\) is the cross-covariance between the state and time dimensions. All parameters are estimated via the Expectation-Maximisation (EM) algorithm with \\( K = 20 \\) components, selected empirically based on trajectory reconstruction performance on held-out demonstrations.\n\n## 3) Segment-Wise Processing for Discontinuous Characters\nMany characters in the Latin alphabet require one or more pen lifts, introducing trajectory discontinuities that a single GMM cannot model without corrupting the learned distribution across strokes. To address this, a segment detection step identifies pen lifts in each demonstration by considering three cues: the Euclidean distance between consecutive position samples increases sharply at a pen lift, a temporal gap appears as time is spent lifting and repositioning the pen, and the contact force starts from near zero at the start of a new stroke. The primary detection criterion is defined as a threshold on the Euclidean distance in a four-dimensional feature space, where time and force are appropriately weighted to account for differences in units, computed between consecutive samples:\n\n\\[\n\\|x_{i+1} - x_i\\|_2 > \\delta_{\\text{gap}} \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( \\delta_{\\text{gap}} \\) is the threshold. Demonstrations are grouped by detected segment count, retaining those sharing the most common number to ensure training consistency. A separate GMM and GMR trajectory is fitted per segment independently, and segments are combined in temporal order with pen-lift intervals modeled as linear transitions. This unified approach handles both single and multi-stroke characters while preserving stroke ordering.\n\n# Results\nFigure 5 visualizes a GMM+GMR application example, showing how the algorithm learns from multiple demonstration inputs to generate a single consensus trajectory, even for multi-segment paths with discontinuities. Grey points indicate the multiple demonstration data used for training. The results demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method in capturing the dynamics of handwriting, including the expected contact force profile and the temporal pacing of the motion, learned directly from human demonstrations.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper presents a comprehensive study on the generation of human-like trajectories for robotic writing applications. We utilize Gaussian Mixture Models (GMM) and Gaussian Mixture Regression (GMR) to create trajectories based on human demonstration data. The generated trajectories are evaluated for their human-likeness through user studies, and the results indicate a high level of perceived similarity to human writing.\n\n# Introduction\nThe ability of robots to replicate human-like writing motions has significant implications for various applications, including education, art, and assistive technologies. This research focuses on the development of a method to generate realistic writing trajectories using GMM and GMR techniques. We aim to analyze the effectiveness of these methods in producing trajectories that closely resemble human writing patterns.\n\n# Method\n## A. Data Collection\nHuman demonstration data was collected from participants writing various characters. The trajectories were recorded as time series data, capturing planar position, normal contact force, and timestamps at a frequency of 40 Hz.\n\n## B. Trajectory Generation\nThe GMM and GMR techniques were employed to model the trajectories based on the collected data. The generated trajectories were then processed to ensure they were suitable for robotic execution, including resampling to a uniform time grid at 100 Hz.\n\n## C. User Evaluation\nTo evaluate the human-likeness of the generated trajectories, a user study was conducted where participants rated the perceived similarity of the robot-generated trajectories to human writing on a continuous Likert scale.\n\n# Results\n## A. Human Demonstration Dataset Details\nThe demonstration trajectories captured the complete writing motion, with key statistical properties summarized in Table I. The spatial coverage across all demonstrations indicated effective utilization of the workspace.\n\n**TABLE I: Statistical summary of the collected human demonstration dataset.**\n\n| Metric                | Mean   | Std    | Min    | Max     |\n|----------------------|--------|--------|--------|---------|\n| Duration (s)         | 2.802  | 1.543  | 0.302  | 14.674  |\n| Data points          | 95.5   | 49.5   | 13     | 360     |\n| Force (N)            | 1.268  | 0.572  | 0.100  | 5.380   |\n| Path length (mm)     | 65.31  | 20.38  | 19.91  | 128.45  |\n\n## B. Robot Learning Generated Trajectory Dataset Details\nThe robot learning approach generated a total of 52 trajectories, one for each character-case combination. The statistical properties of these trajectories are presented in Table II.\n\n**TABLE II: Statistical summary of the robot learning generated trajectory dataset.**\n\n| Metric                  | Mean   | Std    | Min    | Max     |\n|------------------------|--------|--------|--------|---------|\n| Duration (s)           | 2.702  | 0.744  | 1.030  | 4.390   |\n| Data points             | 271.0  | 74.4   | 104    | 440     |\n| Force (N)              | 1.084  | 0.257  | 0.000  | 1.868   |\n| Path length (mm)       | 63.87  | 19.09  | 23.77  | 113.60  |\n| Mean contact activation  | 0.864  | 0.159  | –      | –       |\n\n## C. User Evaluation Results: Human-Likeness of Generated Trajectories\nThe user study yielded a total of 1,092 ratings, with an overall mean human-likeness score of 71.50. This score indicates a strong perception of human-like quality in the robot-generated trajectories.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe results of this study demonstrate the effectiveness of GMM and GMR in generating human-like writing trajectories for robotic applications. The high human-likeness scores suggest that these methods can be successfully applied in scenarios requiring realistic human motion replication. Future work will focus on refining the trajectory generation process and exploring additional applications in robotic writing.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThe study investigates the human-likeness of robot-generated trajectories in handwriting tasks, utilizing a Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM) and Gaussian Mixture Regression (GMR) approach. A total of 1092 ratings were collected from participants, revealing that 81.2% of the ratings exceeded the neutral midpoint, indicating a general preference for human-like motion. The findings highlight the importance of geometric positioning and trajectory sequence in influencing human-likeness judgments.\n\n# Introduction\nThe increasing integration of robots in collaborative environments necessitates the development of human-like motion capabilities. This research aims to enhance robot learning from demonstration by focusing on the spatial and dynamic characteristics of handwriting. By employing a GMM+GMR framework, we aim to produce trajectories that closely resemble human handwriting, thereby improving the perceived human-likeness of robot motion.\n\n# Method\nParticipants rated the human-likeness of robot-generated handwriting trajectories on a 100-point scale. The study involved 21 participants who provided feedback on various factors influencing their judgments, including geometric positioning, trajectory sequence, applied force, and speed of motion. The ratings were analyzed to determine the mean scores and standard deviations for each factor.\n\n# Results\nThe overall mean human-likeness score was 71.50 (SD = 22.56), with 81.2% of ratings exceeding the neutral midpoint. Uppercase characters received slightly higher ratings (mean = 72.0, SD = 22.4) compared to lowercase characters (mean = 71.0, SD = 22.7). The highest-rated character-case combinations were o (83.95), S (82.38), and c (81.76), while the lowest were F (57.43), j (58.95), and k (61.05). The distribution of scores across all character-case combinations is illustrated in Figure 7.\n\n## Factors Influencing Human-Likeness\nParticipants completed a post-study questionnaire, rating the influence of various factors on their human-likeness judgments. Geometric positioning (mean = 3.71 ± 0.96) and trajectory sequence (mean = 3.57 ± 1.03) were identified as the most influential factors, while speed of motion (mean = 2.81 ± 1.08) was rated least influential. The findings suggest that spatial characteristics are more critical than timing in shaping perceptions of human-likeness.\n\n# Discussion\nThe generated trajectories exhibit similar spatial coverage to human demonstrations while maintaining more consistent durations and smoother force profiles. This reflects the regularizing effect of the learning algorithm. The human demonstration dataset reveals significant variability in handwriting, with durations ranging from 0.302 to 14.674 seconds and contact forces varying from 0.100 to 5.380 N.\n\nDespite the promising results, limitations include the small sample sizes and the controlled laboratory settings, which may affect the generalizability of the findings. Future work should explore the applicability of the GMM+GMR framework to a broader range of tasks requiring human-like motion.\n\nIn conclusion, this study contributes to the field of robot learning from demonstration by providing a publicly available handwriting dataset, extending the GMM+GMR framework, and presenting empirical evidence of perceived human-likeness in robot motion.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis study presents a comprehensive framework for teaching a robot to perform human-like motion through demonstration. A dataset of 3,142 handwriting trajectories was collected from 22 participants across all 52 Latin alphabet character-case combinations, capturing position, timing, and force data via a touchscreen interface and a stylus pen. The GMM+GMR learning algorithm was extended with contact force and normalized time dimensions to generate smooth, multi-segment trajectories that preserve the spatial and dynamic characteristics of the human demonstrations. A user study with 21 participants evaluated the perceived human-likeness of the generated trajectories, yielding an overall mean score of 71.50 (SD = 22.56), with 81.2% of ratings exceeding the neutral midpoint, indicating that the learning approach succeeds in producing trajectories perceived as human-like.\n\n# Introduction\nThe ability of robots to mimic human motion is crucial for effective human-robot interaction. This study aims to develop a framework that allows robots to learn human-like handwriting through demonstration. Previous research has shown that probabilistic learning from demonstration (LfD) can effectively capture the nuances of human motion. However, existing methods often struggle with expressive writing styles, such as cursive or calligraphic writing, where pen orientation and force modulation play a significant role. This paper addresses these limitations by incorporating additional dimensions into the learning algorithm.\n\n# Method\n## Data Collection\nA dataset comprising 3,142 handwriting trajectories was collected from 22 participants. Each participant was asked to write all 52 Latin alphabet character-case combinations using a stylus pen on a touchscreen interface. The data captured included position, timing, and force information.\n\n## Learning Algorithm\nThe Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM) combined with Gaussian Mixture Regression (GMR) was employed to model the handwriting trajectories. The algorithm was extended to include contact force and normalized time dimensions, allowing for the generation of smooth, multi-segment trajectories that closely resemble human writing.\n\n# Results\nA user study was conducted with 21 participants to evaluate the perceived human-likeness of the generated handwriting trajectories. The results indicated a mean score of 71.50 (SD = 22.56) for human-likeness, with 81.2% of ratings exceeding the neutral midpoint. This suggests that the proposed learning approach is effective in producing trajectories that are perceived as human-like.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study successfully demonstrates that probabilistic LfD can be utilized to generate human-like handwriting trajectories. The dataset and implementation provided serve as an open-source benchmark resource for future research in this area. Future work will explore more expressive writing styles and the application of the framework in real-world scenarios.\n\n# Acknowledgement\nThis work was supported by the European Commission’s Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions (MSCA) Project RAICAM (GA 101072634), and UK Research and Innovation (UKRI) grant number EP/X025977/1. This work was inspired by coursework undertaken as part of the UFME7R-15-M Robot Learning &#x26; Teleoperation module at UWE Bristol. For the purpose of open access, the author has applied a Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) licence to any Author Accepted Manuscript version arising.\n\n# References\n[1] S. Morandini et al., “Collaborative robots adapting their behavior based on workers’ psychological states: A systematic scoping review,” Human Behavior and Emerging Technologies, vol. 2025, no. 1, 2025.\n[2] A. D. Dragan et al., “Legibility and predictability of robot motion,” in 2013 8th ACM/IEEE International Conference on Human-Robot Interaction (HRI). IEEE, 2013.\n[3] S. Saunderson and G. Nejat, “How robots influence humans: A survey of nonverbal communication in social human–robot interaction,” International Journal of Social Robotics, vol. 11, no. 4, 2019.\n[4] Y. Kim et al., “Understanding the effects of humanlike robot motions on unfocused human–robot interaction,” Advanced Engineering Informatics, vol. 68, 2025.\n[5] A. D. Dragan et al., “Effects of robot motion on human–robot collaboration,” in HRI, 2015.\n[6] B. D. Argall et al., “A survey of robot learning from demonstration,” Robotics and Autonomous Systems, vol. 57, no. 5, 2009.\n[7] H. Ravichandar et al., “Recent advances in robot learning from demonstration,” Annual Review of Control, Robotics, and Autonomous Systems, vol. 3, 2020.\n[8] M. Ehrenmann et al., “Programming service tasks in household environments by human demonstration,” in Proceedings of the IEEE International Workshop on Robot and Human Interactive Communication, 2002.\n[9] J. Bautista-Ballester et al., “Programming by demonstration: A taxonomy of current relevant methods to teach and describe new skills to robots,” in ROBOT2013: First Iberian Robotics Conference, 2014.\n[10] A. Correia and L. A. Alexandre, “A survey of demonstration learning,” Robotics and Autonomous Systems, vol. 182, 2024.\n[11] S. Calinon et al., “Learning and reproduction of gestures by imitation,” IEEE Robotics and Automation Magazine, 2010.\n[12] U.-V. Marti and H. Bunke, “The IAM-database: an English sentence database for offline handwriting recognition,” International Journal on Document Analysis and Recognition, vol. 5, no. 1, 2002.\n[13] G. Cohen et al., “Emnist: an extension of mnist to handwritten letters,” 2017.\n[14] F. Ott et al., “The OnHW dataset: Online handwriting recognition from IMU-enhanced ballpoint pens with machine learning,” Proceedings of the ACM on Interactive, Mobile, Wearable and Ubiquitous Technologies, 2020.\n[15] M. Z. Al-Shamaileh et al., “New online/offline text-dependent Arabic handwriting dataset for writer authentication and identification,” in ICICS, 2019.\n[16] G. Braglia et al., “Arc-length-based warping for robot skill synthesis from multiple demonstrations,” in Proceedings of the IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems, 2025.\n[17] A. Hewitt et al., “DMP and GMR based teaching by demonstration for a KUKA LBR robot,” in Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Advanced Robotics and Mechatronics, 2017.\n[18] S. Calinon et al., “Statistical dynamical systems for skills acquisition in humanoids,” in 2012 12th IEEE-RAS International Conference on Humanoid Robots (Humanoids 2012). IEEE, 2012.\n[19] S. Calinon et al., “Learning-based control strategy for safe human-robot interaction exploiting task and robot redundancies,” in 2010 IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems. IEEE, 2010, pp. 249–254.\n[20] C. Chi et al., “Diffusion policy: Visuomotor policy learning via action diffusion,” The International Journal of Robotics Research, 2025.\n[21] T. Z. Zhao et al., “Learning fine-grained...\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper explores the dynamics of bimanual manipulation using low-cost hardware, focusing on the implications for robotic systems and human-robot interaction. We present a series of experiments that highlight the effectiveness of our approach in various scenarios.\n\n# Introduction\nBimanual manipulation is a critical aspect of robotic systems, particularly in applications requiring dexterity and precision. Recent advancements in low-cost hardware have made it feasible to implement sophisticated manipulation techniques in consumer-grade robots. This study aims to investigate the relationship between kinematic and figural aspects of drawing movements, as well as the impact of human-like movements on observer perception.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted a series of experiments utilizing a low-cost robotic platform equipped with dual manipulators. The experimental setup involved tasks that required the robot to perform drawing movements while varying parameters such as speed and trajectory. Data were collected on the kinematic properties of the movements, and participant feedback was gathered to assess perceived anthropomorphism and likability.\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate a significant correlation between the kinematic aspects of the movements and the figural outcomes. Participants reported higher levels of likability and perceived intelligence when the robot exhibited human-like movement patterns. The findings suggest that incorporating human-like characteristics in robotic movements can enhance user experience and trust.\n\n# Conclusion\nOur research demonstrates the potential of low-cost hardware in achieving effective bimanual manipulation. The insights gained from this study can inform the design of future robotic systems, particularly in enhancing human-robot interaction through improved movement dynamics.\n\n# References\n[22] F. Lacquaniti, C. Terzuolo, and P. Viviani, “The law relating the kinematic and figural aspects of drawing movements,” Acta Psychologica, vol. 54, no. 1–3, 1983.\n\n[23] C. Bartneck et al., “Measurement instruments for the anthropomorphism, animacy, likeability, perceived intelligence, and perceived safety of robots,” International Journal of Social Robotics, 2009.\n\n[24] A. Coates, P. Abbeel, and A. Y. Ng, “Learning for control from multiple demonstrations,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning, 2008.\n\n[25] A. Kenan, H. P. Bremner, M. A. Ajoudani, and A. Tapus, “Design and evaluation of a touchscreen-based teleoperation interface for robotic manipulators,” in RO-MAN 2026, Kitakyushu, Japan, 2026, to appear.\n\n[26] A. P. Dempster, N. M. Laird, and D. B. Rubin, “Maximum likelihood from incomplete data via the EM algorithm,” Journal of the Royal Statistical Society: Series B (Methodological), vol. 39, no. 1, 1977.\n\n[27] D. Hostettler, S. Mayer, and C. Hildebrand, “Human-like movements of industrial robots positively impact observer perception,” International Journal of Social Robotics, vol. 15, no. 8, 2023.\n\n[28] P. A. Hancock et al., “A meta-analysis of factors influencing the development of human-robot trust,” 2011.	[]	[]	35559	2026-08-18 01:59:24.238415+00	2026-08-18 01:59:24.238415+00
