8	70	llamaparse	# BENDTWIN: ROBUST DENSE-TO-SPARSE PHYSICAL RECONSTRUCTION WITH BENDING-AWARE DIFFERENTIABLE SPRING–MASS MODELS\n\nYixiong Jing∗, Qi Wang∗\nUniversity of Cambridge, Institute of Automation, Chinese Academy of Sciences\nyj401@cam.ac.uk, qi.wang@ia.ac.cn\n\nLin Chen, Junwei Jiang, Guangming Wang†\nNorthwestern Polytechnical University, The Hong Kong Polytechnic University, University of Cambridge\nnpuchenlin@foxmail.com, junwei.jiang97@outlook.com, gw462@cam.ac.uk\n\nHaibing Wu, Olaf Wysocki, Wanli Ma, Brian Sheil\nUniversity of Cambridge\nhw657@cam.ac.uk, okw24@cam.ac.uk, wm369@cam.ac.uk, bbs24@cam.ac.uk\n\narXiv:2608.06164v1 [cs.CV] 6 Aug 2026\nAugust 7, 2026\n\n## ABSTRACT\n\nReconstructing objects with mechanical properties from video observations enables physically consistent dynamic prediction, benefiting robotics planning and interaction. Existing spring–mass based physical driven reconstruction approaches offer efficient and differentiable physical reconstruction, but they typically rely on axial springs alone. Such formulations oversimplify the underlying structural mechanics and can become mechanically under-constrained when the physical graph is coarsened, limiting their ability to preserve stable local deformation. We present BendTwin, a bending-aware differentiable spring–mass framework for video-based reconstruction and future prediction of deformable objects. BendTwin introduces bending stiffness and damping over local surface triplets, penalizing deviations from rest angles and regularizing higher-order deformation. These bending constraints improve mechanical stability while preserving the simplicity of spring–mass system. Experiments show that BendTwin consistently outperforms the axial-only PhysTwin baseline. Ablation studies further demonstrate that the bending constraints maintain system stability across different downsampling ratios and consistently improve upon the original PhysTwin formulation. Overall, BendTwin provides an effective approach for constructing mechanically faithful digital twins from sparse-view RGB-D videos.\n\n**Keywords:** Differentiable Physics · Physics-Informed Reconstruction · Spring–Mass Models\n\n## 1 Introduction\n\nCreating digital twins of real-world objects is essential for predicting how objects move and deform under interaction. Accurate simulation of future dynamic states from a physically consistent digital twin enables a wide range of downstream applications, including robot manipulation planning [1, 2, 3], data generation for policy learning [4, 5, 6], and physically grounded rollouts within vision-based world models [7]. To obtain such digital twins, it is critical to...\n\n\n\n# Sparse-view RGB-D 3D Reconstruction\n\n## Abstract\nIn this paper, we introduce BendTwin, a bending-aware differentiable spring–mass framework designed for the reconstruction of deformable objects from sparse-view RGB-D videos. By augmenting conventional axial spring formulations with explicit bending stiffness and damping defined over local surface triplets, BendTwin enhances mechanical expressiveness, enabling improved reconstruction, re-simulation, and future prediction. Our experiments demonstrate that BendTwin consistently outperforms the axial-only PhysTwin baseline, particularly in scenarios with sparse physical graphs.\n\n## Introduction\nThe ability to infer physically plausible models directly from observations such as RGB-D videos has the potential to reduce manual modeling efforts and bridge the gap between simulated dynamics and real-world behavior. However, achieving precise reconstruction of the physical model of a real object remains a significant challenge. Traditional graphics and engineering pipelines often rely on carefully designed constitutive models and discretizations [8, 9, 10], typically assuming that the geometry and mechanical properties of objects are known a priori or identified through controlled experiments. While these methods can yield accurate results, they are often expensive and difficult to generalize.\n\nRecent advancements in 3D reconstruction [11, 12] and generative models [13, 14] have enabled detailed geometric modeling of real-world objects. When combined with classical continuum mechanics simulators, these advancements can produce visually convincing deformations [15, 16]. However, material properties in prior work are often manually assigned or generatively produced, which may not accurately reflect the true dynamics of the corresponding objects, thereby limiting physical fidelity. Differentiable physics [17, 18, 19] addresses this limitation by allowing for the direct optimization of unknown physical parameters in governing equations through the matching of simulated dynamics to observations.\n\nPhysTwin [20] leverages this paradigm by reconstructing object geometry from sparse-view RGB-D videos using 3D Gaussian Splatting [11] and TRELLIS [13], optimizing a differentiable spring–mass system with learnable mechanical and contact parameters while enforcing visual consistency via Gaussian rendering. However, PhysTwin primarily relies on axial springs and damping. While effective for capturing stretching-dominated motion, axial-only formulations constrain only pairwise distances and do not explicitly regulate local angular deformation. As the physical graph (e.g., the node counts for PhysTwin [20]) becomes sparse with fewer axial connections, the limited mechanical expressiveness of axial-only formulations can leave the system under-constrained, leading to challenges in preserving stable local deformation.\n\nTo address this limitation, we introduce BendTwin, a bending-aware differentiable spring–mass framework illustrated in Fig. 1. BendTwin augments the conventional axial spring formulation with explicit bending stiffness and damping defined over local surface triplets. These bending constraints complement axial length constraints by penalizing deviations from rest angles, enabling the model to better preserve local shape and resist unstable deformation modes. Consequently, BendTwin remains mechanically stable even as the spring–mass graph is coarsened to far fewer nodes and connections, while maintaining the simplicity of explicit spring–mass dynamics.\n\nOur experiments demonstrate consistent improvements over the axial-only PhysTwin baseline in reconstruction, re-simulation, and future prediction. Ablation studies further show that explicit bending improves mechanical fidelity and maintains stable behavior as the physical graph becomes increasingly sparse under different downsampling ratios.\n\n## Contributions\nIn summary, our contributions are:\n- We propose BendTwin, a bending-aware differentiable spring–mass framework that augments axial interactions with explicit bending stiffness and damping over local surface triplets, improving mechanical expressiveness for video-based physical reconstruction, simulation, and prediction.\n- We show that explicit bending constraints stabilize the spring–mass system under different downsampling ratios, allowing BendTwin to retain accuracy with far fewer nodes where axial-only formulations become under-constrained.\n\n## Method\n[Details of the method would be included here, including equations and specific algorithms used in BendTwin.]\n\n## Results\n[Results of the experiments, including quantitative and qualitative comparisons with PhysTwin and other baselines, would be presented here.]\n\n## Conclusion\n[The conclusion would summarize the findings and potential future work related to BendTwin and its applications in 3D reconstruction and simulation.]\n\n\n\n# BendTwin: A Bending-Aware Differentiable Physics Framework\n\n## Abstract\nWe present BendTwin, a bending-aware differentiable physics framework designed to estimate mechanical parameters by matching simulated dynamics to partial 3D observations from videos. By augmenting traditional spring-mass models with explicit bending stiffness and damping, BendTwin enhances the mechanical stability of sparse systems while maintaining the simplicity of explicit differentiable simulation.\n\n## Introduction\nThe reconstruction of physically consistent digital twins from multi-view RGB-D videos poses significant challenges, particularly in accurately estimating mechanical parameters. This paper introduces BendTwin, a framework that addresses these challenges by incorporating bending-aware mechanics into a differentiable physics simulation.\n\n## Method\n\n### 2.1 Problem Formulation\nGiven multi-view RGB-D videos of an object undergoing interaction, our goal is to reconstruct a digital twin by estimating its mechanical parameters using BendTwin. The process begins with sparse-view RGB-D observations, which are discretized into a spring-mass system for inverse mechanical parameter estimation.\n\nWe represent the object as a graph \\( G = \\{V, E, T\\} \\), where \\( V \\) is a set of surface mass nodes connected by axial springs \\( E \\) and bending triplets \\( T \\). Each node \\( i \\in V \\) has a position \\( x^t \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 \\) and a velocity \\( v^t \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 \\) at time \\( t \\). The system state at time \\( t \\) is defined as \\( X_t = \\{x^t, v^t\\}_{i \\in V} \\).\n\nLet \\( X^{obs} \\) denote the partial 3D observation of the object at time \\( t \\) (e.g., a point cloud derived from sparse-view RGB-D frames). Our objective is to estimate the physical parameters of the deformable object, which we collect into:\n\n\\[\n\\alpha = \\{k_{ij}, \\gamma_{ij}\\}_{(i,j) \\in E} \\cup \\{k_{\\theta,(i,j,k)}, c_{b,(i,j,k)}\\}_{(i,j,k) \\in T} \\cup \\alpha_{contact},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( k_{ij} \\) is the axial stiffness and \\( \\gamma_{ij} \\) is the dashpot damping coefficient. The term \\( k_{\\theta,(i,j,k)} \\) penalizes deviations from the rest angle of triplet \\( (i, j, k) \\), and \\( c_{b,(i,j,k)} \\) specifies viscous bending damping proportional to the angular rate \\( \\dot{\\theta}_{(i,j,k)} \\). The parameters \\( \\alpha_{contact} \\) characterize the contact model, including restitution and friction coefficients.\n\nOur objective minimizes the discrepancy between simulated and observed motion:\n\n\\[\n\\min_{\\alpha, G_t} L(\\hat{X}^{obs}) = \\sum_{t=0}^{T} \\left( L_{geo}(\\hat{X}^t, X_t) + L_{trk}(\\hat{X}^t, X_t) \\right) \\quad \\text{s.t.} \\quad X_{t+1} = F_{\\alpha, G_t}(X_t).\n\\]\n\n## Results\nThe BendTwin framework was evaluated on various datasets, demonstrating its effectiveness in reconstructing the dynamics of deformable objects. The integration of bending mechanics significantly improved the accuracy of the simulations compared to traditional spring-mass models.\n\n## Conclusion\nBendTwin provides a robust solution for estimating mechanical parameters in deformable objects using sparse RGB-D observations. By incorporating bending-aware mechanics, it enhances the stability and accuracy of simulations, paving the way for more realistic digital twins in various applications.\n\n## Figure Captions\nFigure 2: BendTwin framework overview. (a) represents the 3D reconstruction from sparse-view RGB-D observations into a point-based surface discretization. (b) indicates that BendTwin extends the spring–mass model with explicit bending triplets in addition to axial springs and (c) optimizes physical parameters using geometry and tracking losses. (d) visualizes the details of the proposed bending mechanism. (e) demonstrates differentiable explicit simulation of the bending-aware spring–mass system.\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\n\n[Abstract content goes here.]\n\n## Introduction\n\n[Introduction content goes here.]\n\n## Method\n\n### 2.2 Bending-Enhanced Spring–Mass Dynamics\n\nNaive spring–mass models (used in PhysTwin [20] and Spring-Gau [21]) rely solely on axial springs to resist stretching and compression. While effective for modeling compliant deformation, axial-only interactions do not explicitly model bending mechanics. Consequently, bending-dominated surface deformations must be approximated indirectly through axial coupling, limiting the mechanical expressiveness of the system and reducing its ability to faithfully reproduce complex deformation behaviors.\n\nTo address these limitations, BendTwin augments the spring–mass model with explicit bending stiffness defined directly over local surface configurations. Specifically, we introduce bending springs over node triplets \\((i, j, k)\\) to penalize angular deviations, allowing bending resistance to be modeled explicitly. These bending constraints complement axial springs and reduce under-constrained local deformation modes when the system contains fewer sampled nodes and connections. Meanwhile, BendTwin preserves the simplicity of spring–mass systems while improving the mechanical stability of sparse discretizations.\n\nWe define the total internal potential energy \\(E_{\\text{int}}(X)\\) as\n\n\\[\nE_{\\text{int}}(X) = \\sum_{(i,j) \\in E} E_{\\text{axial}}(i, j) + \\sum_{(i,j,k) \\in T} E_{\\text{bend}}(i, j, k). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(E_{\\text{axial}}(i, j)\\) denotes the standard axial spring energy, whose induced nodal forces are identical to those used in PhysTwin [20] and therefore omitted for brevity. This paper only introduces the derivation of the additional forces introduced by the proposed bending energy \\(E_{\\text{bend}}(i, j, k)\\), which can be obtained by differentiating \\(E_{\\text{bend}}\\) with respect to node positions:\n\n\\[\nf_{\\text{bend}} = -\\nabla_{x} E_{\\text{bend}}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n### 2.2.1 Bending Stiffness\n\nTo model bending, we introduce bending springs over local triplets \\((i, j, k) \\in T\\), where \\(j\\) is the center node and \\(i, k\\) are its neighbors. The rest angle \\(\\theta^0\\) corresponds to the angle between edges \\((i, j)\\) and \\((k, j)\\) in \\(G_0\\). We define the bending energy as\n\n\\[\nE_{\\text{bend}}(i, j, k) = \\frac{1}{2} k_{\\theta,(i,j,k)} (\\theta - \\theta^0)^2, \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\theta\\) denotes the current angle between the two edges. Differentiating Eq. (5) yields restoring forces that act to reduce the angular deviation:\n\n\\[\nf_{\\text{bend}} = -k_{\\theta}(\\theta - \\theta^0) d(i,j,k), \\quad f_{\\text{bend}} = -k_{\\theta}(\\theta - \\theta^0) d(i,j,k). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(d(i,j,k)\\) and \\(d(i,j,k)\\) are unit-scaled directional vectors determined by the local geometry of the \\((i, j, k)\\). These directions ensure torque-consistent application of force and are derived in the Appendix. By Newton’s third law (force balance within the \\((i, j, k)\\)), the force on \\(j\\) is\n\n\\[\nf_{\\text{bend}} = - (f_{\\text{bend}} + f_{\\text{bend}}) = k_{\\theta}(\\theta - \\theta^0) (d(i,j,k) + d(i,j,k)). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n### 2.2.2 Bending Damping\n\nIn addition to elastic bending stiffness, we introduce bending damping to dissipate energy associated with angular motion to avoid the oscillations of the system caused by the extra bending stiffness. For each bending triplet \\((i, j, k)\\),\n\n\\[\n\\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k) \\text{ measures the relative angular motion of edges } (i, j) \\text{ and } (k, j).\n\\]\n\nWe model bending damping as a viscous torque proportional to this angular rate, with damping coefficient \\(c_{b,(i,j,k)}\\). The resulting nodal damping forces are\n\n\\[\nf_{\\text{rot}}^i = c_b \\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k) d_i, \\quad f_k = c_b \\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k) d_k. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe equivalent damping force introduced on the center node \\(j\\) is\n\n\\[\nf_{\\text{rot}}^j = - (f_{\\text{rot}}^i + f_{\\text{rot}}^k) = c_b \\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k) (d_i + d_k). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe force directions \\(d(i,j,k)\\) and \\(d(i,j,k)\\) are identical to those used for elastic bending forces. The explicit computation of \\(\\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k)\\) from nodal velocities is provided in the Appendix.\n\n## Results\n\n[Results content goes here.]\n\n## Conclusion\n\n[Conclusion content goes here.]\n\n## References\n\n[References content goes here.]\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## 2.2.3 Efficient Triplet Construction for High-Valence Nodes\n\nIf node \\( j \\) has \\( N \\) neighbors, enumerating all neighbor pairs yields \\( O(N^2) \\) triplets, which is expensive and often introduces redundant stiffness in the model. Instead, we construct \\( O(N) \\) triplets by selecting a reference neighbor \\( r(j) \\in N(j) \\) and forming triplets \\( (r(j), j, k) \\) for all \\( k \\in N(j) \\setminus \\{r(j)\\} \\) (or a random subset). This preserves bending expressivity while keeping the model scalable. Given the axial springs, bending triplets, and external interactions, the total force acting on node \\( j \\) is computed as\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{tot}} = \\sum_{i \\in NE(j)} (f^{\\text{axial}} + f^{\\text{dash}}) + \\sum_{(i,j,k) \\in T} (f^{\\text{bend}} + f^{\\text{rot}}) + f^{\\text{ext}}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n## 3 Experiments\n\nIn this section, we evaluate BendTwin to assess the impact of bending stiffness on mechanical fidelity and simulation stability. Specifically, we examine (1) whether bending-aware constraints improve object reconstruction &#x26; re-simulation and future prediction over the axial-only PhysTwin baseline; and (2) whether bending constraints preserve accuracy and stability as the physical system is progressively downsampled.\n\n### 3.1 Experimental Settings\n\n**Dataset.** We use the non-cloth deformable object sequences from the PhysTwin benchmark [20]. The revised evaluation excludes fabric-like sheet sequences because cloth is highly compliant and exhibits little intrinsic resistance to bending or shearing, whereas our bending-aware model assumes objects with sufficient structural support for stable bending constraints. Following the original protocol, each sequence is split into training and testing frames with a 7:3 ratio. The training split is used for the reconstruction &#x26; re-simulation, while the test split evaluates the future prediction.\n\n**Baselines.** The primary comparison is against PhysTwin [20], which adopts an axial spring–mass formulation. This comparison directly evaluates whether incorporating bending constraints improves accuracy and enables the assessment of mechanical stability in sparse physical systems.\n\n**Evaluation metrics.** To evaluate reconstruction and prediction accuracy, we report 3D metrics including Chamfer Distance (CD) and tracking error, and 2D rendering metrics including PSNR, SSIM [22], LPIPS [23], and silhouette IoU computed from 3D Gaussian Splatting renderings.\n\n**Implementation details.** Our implementation is based on PyTorch and Warp for GPU-accelerated spring–mass simulation. All experiments are performed on one NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPU. We follow PhysTwin [20] for 3D reconstruction. Parameters \\( \\alpha \\) are optimized via Adam with \\( lr = 10^{-3} \\) to minimize \\( L_{\\text{geo}} + L_{\\text{trk}} \\) with equal weights. For the active non-cloth evaluation, all simulations use the differentiable explicit Euler rollout \\( F^{\\text{exp}} \\) with sub-step \\( \\Delta t = 5 \\times 10^{-5} \\) s, \\( M_t \\approx 667 \\) sub-steps per frame, and maximum axial stiffness \\( k_{ij} = 10^5 \\). Collision parameters \\( \\alpha_{\\text{contact}} \\) use restitution 0.5/0.7 and friction 0.3.\n\n### 3.2 Results\n\n**Reconstruction &#x26; re-simulation.** We first evaluate reconstruction &#x26; re-simulation on the non-cloth object set using the explicit Euler simulator for all methods. The quantitative results are reported in Table 1. Compared with the axial-only PhysTwin baseline, BendTwin reduces CD from 0.0058 to 0.0047 (e.g., 19.0% improvement), and reduces tracking error from 0.0091 to 0.0078 (e.g., 14.3% improvement). These gains indicate that the added bending stiffness and damping provide useful local shape constraints beyond pairwise axial springs, allowing the physical graph to better preserve object structure during re-simulation.\n\nThe improvement on geometric accuracy is reflected in the rendered observations. BendTwin improves PSNR from 28.429 to 28.605, a gain of 0.18 dB, raises IoU from 79.5 to 80.6, and reduces LPIPS from 0.022 to 0.021. Overall, the results show that explicit bending constraints make the reconstructed dynamics more geometrically accurate while maintaining comparable visual quality for deformable objects.\n\n**Future prediction.** We further evaluate future prediction by optimizing physical parameters on the training portion of each sequence and then simulating forward to unseen frames. As shown in Table 1, BendTwin also improves long-horizon prediction over PhysTwin. CD decreases from 0.0095 to 0.0079 (e.g., 16.8% reduction), and tracking error decreases from 0.0166 to 0.0153 (e.g., 7.8% reduction). These results suggest that bending-aware constraints...\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\n[Abstract content goes here.]\n\n## Introduction\n[Introduction content goes here.]\n\n## Method\n[Method content goes here.]\n\n## Results\n\n### Quantitative Results\nTable 1 presents the quantitative results on Reconstruction &#x26; Re-simulation and Future Prediction. The metrics include Chamfer Distance (CD), Tracking Error (Track), Intersection over Union (IoU), Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR), Structural Similarity Index (SSIM), and Learned Perceptual Image Patch Similarity (LPIPS).\n\n| Model            | CD ↓   | Track ↓ | IoU % ↑ | PSNR ↑  | SSIM ↑  | LPIPS ↓ | CD ↓   | Track ↓ | IoU % ↑ | PSNR ↑  | SSIM ↑  | LPIPS ↓ |\n|------------------|--------|---------|---------|---------|---------|---------|--------|---------|---------|---------|---------|---------|\n| PhysTwin         | 0.0058 | 0.0091  | 79.5    | 28.429  | 0.963   | 0.022   | 0.0095 | 0.0166  | 68.7    | 26.412  | 0.957   | 0.036   |\n| BendTwin (Ours)  | 0.0047 | 0.0078  | 80.6    | 28.605  | 0.963   | 0.021   | 0.0079 | 0.0153  | 71.5    | 26.755  | 0.957   | 0.035   |\n\nThe results indicate that BendTwin outperforms PhysTwin in both reconstruction and future prediction tasks. Specifically, BendTwin improves IoU from 68.7 to 71.5 and PSNR from 26.412 to 26.755, corresponding to gains of 2.8 percentage points and 0.34 dB, respectively. These improvements suggest that explicitly modeling bending stiffness leads to more physically consistent motion propagation over time.\n\n### Qualitative Results\nFigure 3 illustrates the qualitative results of BendTwin and the comparison method on the reconstruction &#x26; re-simulation and future predictions of object deformation. The qualitative results show that our method is closer to the true values than other methods, with red boxes highlighting regions where BendTwin better captures object deformation than PhysTwin.\n\n### Ablation Studies\n#### Downsampled Physical System\nWe evaluate whether providing extra bending constraints maintains stable and accurate dynamics as the physical graph is downsampled. For each sampling ratio \\( r \\), both PhysTwin and BendTwin downsample the full physical graph at the same ratio \\( r \\), ensuring a matched graph. The only difference between the two systems is the presence of the proposed bending springs. A smaller \\( r \\) retains fewer mass points (e.g., progressively compressing the physical system) for testing whether bending constraints preserve stability where axial springs alone become under-constrained.\n\nTable 2 shows that BendTwin consistently outperforms the axial-only PhysTwin across all metrics at both downsampling ratios, and that its advantage grows substantially as the physical graph becomes sparser. At \\( r = 0.1 \\), BendTwin reduces reconstruction CD by 9.6% and tracking error by 10.8%, while improving PSNR by 0.45 dB and IoU by 1.6%.\n\n## Conclusion\n[Conclusion content goes here.]\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\n\n[Abstract content goes here.]\n\n## Introduction\n\n[Introduction content goes here.]\n\n## Method\n\n### Robustness to Physical-Graph Downsampling\n\nTable 2 presents the robustness of both PhysTwin and BendTwin to physical-graph downsampling. Both models retain interior and exterior nodes and are downsampled at the same ratio \\( r \\); the only difference is the proposed bending springs.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{Sampling ratio } r &#x26; \\text{Model} &#x26; \\text{Reconstruction \\&#x26; Re-simulation} &#x26; \\text{Future Prediction} \\\\\n\\hline\n&#x26; &#x26; \\text{CD} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{Track} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{IoU} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{PSNR} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{SSIM} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{LPIPS} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{CD} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{Track} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{IoU} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{PSNR} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{SSIM} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{LPIPS} \\downarrow \\\\\n\\hline\n0.05 &#x26; \\text{PhysTwin} &#x26; 0.0139 &#x26; 0.0175 &#x26; 72.2 &#x26; 26.926 &#x26; 0.958 &#x26; 0.0309 &#x26; 0.0216 &#x26; 0.0241 &#x26; 60.8 &#x26; 25.276 &#x26; 0.953 &#x26; 0.0476 \\\\\n&#x26; \\text{BendTwin (Ours)} &#x26; 0.0102 &#x26; 0.0130 &#x26; 77.2 &#x26; 27.996 &#x26; 0.961 &#x26; 0.0262 &#x26; 0.0176 &#x26; 0.0202 &#x26; 67.8 &#x26; 26.417 &#x26; 0.955 &#x26; 0.0411 \\\\\n\\hline\n0.1 &#x26; \\text{PhysTwin} &#x26; 0.0088 &#x26; 0.0120 &#x26; 78.2 &#x26; 28.105 &#x26; 0.962 &#x26; 0.0245 &#x26; 0.0150 &#x26; 0.0198 &#x26; 66.6 &#x26; 26.116 &#x26; 0.955 &#x26; 0.0409 \\\\\n&#x26; \\text{BendTwin (Ours)} &#x26; 0.0079 &#x26; 0.0107 &#x26; 79.7 &#x26; 28.552 &#x26; 0.963 &#x26; 0.0222 &#x26; 0.0139 &#x26; 0.0181 &#x26; 69.1 &#x26; 26.620 &#x26; 0.957 &#x26; 0.0362 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n### Removal of Interior Nodes\n\nPhysTwin discretizes each volumetric object into exterior surface nodes and interior nodes. Although the interior nodes are unobserved and excluded from evaluation, the axial springs incident to them provide internal mechanical support and help prevent the surface from collapsing inward during interaction. We argue that BendTwin no longer requires this support because its bending term directly penalizes changes in local surface angles, thereby resisting the non-physical surface distortions that the interior axial springs are intended to suppress. A volumetric object may therefore be represented as a surface-only physical system.\n\nWe demonstrate this with an ablation at full sampling density (\\( r = 1.0 \\)), removing all interior nodes from BendTwin and simulating only the surface nodes. We compare this surface-only configuration with the axial-only PhysTwin baseline, which retains its complete interior discretization. All metrics are evaluated on surface points for both methods, ensuring that the evaluation support is identical.\n\nAs reported in Table 3, removing the entire interior discretization does not degrade the surface-level accuracy of BendTwin. For reconstruction and re-simulation, the surface-only BendTwin reduces CD by approximately 6.9% and tracking error by 3.3%, while increasing IoU by 1.1 percentage points. The gains are more pronounced for future prediction, i.e., CD and LPIPS decrease by approximately 5.3% and 6.7%, respectively, while PSNR increases by 0.570 dB and IoU by 3.3 percentage points. Bending constraints are therefore sufficient to replace the mechanical role of interior nodes. A pure surface system reproduces volumetric deformation as faithfully as an axial-only system that supports its interior explicitly, while simulating fewer mass points.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\text{Configuration} &#x26; \\text{Interior nodes} &#x26; \\text{Reconstruction \\&#x26; Re-simulation} &#x26; \\text{Future Prediction} \\\\\n\\hline\n&#x26; &#x26; \\text{CD} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{Track} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{IoU} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{PSNR} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{SSIM} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{LPIPS} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{CD} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{Track} \\downarrow &#x26; \\text{IoU} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{PSNR} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{SSIM} \\uparrow &#x26; \\text{LPIPS} \\downarrow \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{PhysTwin} &#x26; \\text{w/} &#x26; 0.0058 &#x26; 0.0091 &#x26; 79.5 &#x26; 28.429 &#x26; 0.963 &#x26; 0.0225 &#x26; 0.0095 &#x26; 0.0166 &#x26; 68.7 &#x26; 26.412 &#x26; 0.957 &#x26; 0.0357 \\\\\n\\text{BendTwin (Ours)} &#x26; \\text{w/o} &#x26; 0.0054 &#x26; 0.0088 &#x26; 80.6 &#x26; 28.487 &#x26; 0.963 &#x26; 0.0220 &#x26; 0.0090 &#x26; 0.0167 &#x26; 72.0 &#x26; 26.982 &#x26; 0.959 &#x26; 0.0333 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n## Results\n\n[Results content goes here.]\n\n## Related Works\n\n### 3D Reconstruction with Dynamics\n\nDynamic reconstruction goes beyond multi-view geometric consistency and additionally requires temporal coherence modeling to capture long-term changes over time. Classical approaches typically rely on inter-frame correspondence estimation, such as feature-based matching [24, 25] to perform tracking and reconstruction across frames. However, these pipelines often struggle to produce dense reconstructions due to sparse keypoint reliance and tracking drift over long sequences.\n\nRecent advances in neural scene representations, particularly Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) [12], have introduced implicit volumetric representations capable of synthesizing high-fidelity novel views. Extensions to dynamic settings [26, 27] incorporate temporal deformation fields to model non-rigid scene evolution. More recently, 3D Gaussian [content continues here].\n\n[Continue with the rest of the paper.]\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\n\nSplatting has emerged as an explicit and efficient 3D representation enabling real-time, high-quality rendering. Dynamic extensions such as 4D Gaussian representations tightly integrate visual tracking, spatio-temporal representation learning, and differentiable rendering, achieving temporally consistent dynamic scene reconstruction with improved efficiency and fidelity. Despite these advances, current reconstruction methods remain largely geometry- and appearance-driven. The reconstructed objects typically lack integrated physical priors, which limits their ability to predict future states or support physically grounded interaction. Without embedding physics-based constraints or dynamics modeling, reconstructed 4D scenes function primarily as descriptive representations rather than interactive prediction or action simulation.\n\n## 4.2 Physical Simulation and Differentiable Physics\n\nClassical physical simulation in computer graphics relies on established numerical formulations such as Lagrangian mechanics, finite element methods, material point methods (MPM), and position-based dynamics. These approaches have enabled realistic simulation of rigid and deformable objects across a wide range of applications. However, they typically assume that material parameters are manually specified or calibrated offline, limiting their applicability when physical properties are unknown. Differentiable physics aims to bridge this gap by making complex dynamic systems differentiable, allowing unknown physical parameters to be optimized from observations using gradient-based methods. A number of differentiable simulators have been developed for rigid-body dynamics and soft-body dynamics. While these methods enable parameter optimization by fitting simulated dynamics to observations, they do not reconstruct a complete digital twin that simultaneously recovers geometry, material behavior, and appearance from sparse visual inputs.\n\n## 4.3 Physics-Driven 3D Reconstruction\n\nRecent works incorporate continuum mechanics into neural scene representations such as NeRF and 3D Gaussian Splatting to enable dynamic deformation and interaction. However, these approaches typically assume predefined material properties and focus on visually plausible motion rather than recovering true object dynamics. Differentiable physics addresses this limitation by enabling optimization of physical parameters directly from RGB observations, but such methods often operate on implicit geometry and do not explicitly reconstruct physically consistent 3D structure. A more closely related line of work constructs physical digital twins using spring–mass models grounded in reconstructed geometry. Spring-Gaussian couples 3D Gaussian splatting with elastic simulation, and PhysTwin reconstructs deformable objects from sparse-view RGB-D videos and differentiably optimizes a spring–mass system to reproduce observed interactions. While flexible and scalable, these axial-spring formulations lack explicit modeling of more complex mechanical behaviors, such as bending-dominated deformation, which motivates our proposed method.\n\n## 5 Conclusion\n\nWe presented BendTwin, a bending-aware differentiable spring–mass framework for physics-informed reconstruction and future prediction of deformable-object dynamics from sparse-view RGB-D videos. By augmenting axial spring interactions with explicit bending stiffness and damping over local surface triplets, BendTwin penalizes deviations from rest angles and improves mechanical expressiveness while preserving the simplicity and differentiability of spring–mass models. BendTwin demonstrates competitive performance across both dense and sparse spring–mass systems. Overall, BendTwin provides an efficient and mechanically expressive foundation for deformable-object digital twins in robotic manipulation, planning, and physical interaction.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Xuanlin Li, Kyle Hsu, Jiayuan Gu, Oier Mees, Karl Pertsch, Homer Rich Walke, Chuyuan Fu, Ishikaa Lunawat, Isabel Sieh, Sean Kirmani, Sergey Levine, Jiajun Wu, Chelsea Finn, Hao Su, Quan Vuong, and Ted Xiao. Evaluating real-world robot manipulation policies in simulation. In Pulkit Agrawal, Oliver Kroemer, and Wolfram Burgard, editors, Proceedings of The 8th Conference on Robot Learning, volume 270 of Proceedings of Machine Learning Research, pages 3705–3728. PMLR, 06–09 Nov 2025.\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\nIn this paper, we present a novel approach to robot benchmarking that leverages real-to-sim translation techniques. Our method, termed Robotarena, allows for scalable evaluation of robotic policies in simulated environments that closely mimic real-world conditions. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach through a series of experiments that highlight the advantages of using real-world data to inform simulation parameters.\n\n## Introduction\nThe gap between simulation and real-world performance remains a significant challenge in robotics. Traditional methods often rely on static simulation environments that do not account for the variability and unpredictability of real-world scenarios. Recent advancements in real-to-sim translation have opened new avenues for bridging this gap. In this work, we explore the potential of these techniques to create a scalable benchmarking framework for robotic systems.\n\n## Method\nOur approach consists of several key components:\n\n1. **Data Collection**: We gather extensive real-world data from robotic interactions in various environments.\n2. **Simulation Environment**: We develop a simulation framework that incorporates the collected data to create realistic scenarios.\n3. **Policy Evaluation**: We implement a policy evaluation mechanism that assesses the performance of robotic policies in both real and simulated environments.\n\nThe mathematical formulation of our policy evaluation can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Performance} = f(\\text{Real Data}, \\text{Simulated Data}, \\text{Policy})\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( f \\) is a function that quantifies the performance of a given policy based on the comparison of real and simulated outcomes.\n\n## Results\nWe conducted a series of experiments to validate our approach. The results indicate that policies evaluated in our Robotarena framework demonstrate improved performance when deployed in real-world scenarios compared to those evaluated solely in traditional simulation environments.\n\nFigure 1 illustrates the performance metrics of various policies across different environments, highlighting the effectiveness of our real-to-sim translation method.\n\n## Discussion\nThe findings suggest that integrating real-world data into simulation environments can significantly enhance the reliability of robotic policy evaluations. Future work will focus on refining our simulation techniques and exploring additional applications of our framework in diverse robotic tasks.\n\n## Conclusion\nOur research contributes to the ongoing efforts to close the sim-to-real gap in robotics. By leveraging real-to-sim translation, we provide a scalable and effective benchmarking solution that can adapt to various robotic applications.\n\n## References\n1. Yash Jangir et al. (2025). Robotarena: Scalable robot benchmarking via real-to-sim translation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.23571.\n2. Kaifeng Zhang et al. (2025). Real-to-Sim Robot Policy Evaluation with Gaussian Splatting Simulation of Soft-Body Interactions. arXiv e-prints, arXiv:2511.04665.\n3. Yevgen Chebotar et al. (2019). Closing the sim-to-real loop: Adapting simulation randomization with real world experience. In ICRA, pages 8973–8979. IEEE.\n4. Fabio Muratore et al. (2022). Neural posterior domain randomization. In Conference on robot learning, pages 1532–1542. PMLR.\n5. Miguel Angel Zamora Mora et al. (2021). Pods: Policy optimization via differentiable simulation. In Proceedings of the 38th International Conference on Machine Learning, pages 7805–7817. PMLR.\n6. Yanjiang Guo et al. (2025). Ctrl-world: A controllable generative world model for robot manipulation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.10125.\n7. Eftychios Sifakis and Jernej Barbic (2012). Fem simulation of 3d deformable solids: a practitioner’s guide to theory, discretization and model reduction. In ACM SIGGRAPH 2012 courses, pages 1–50.\n8. Matthias Müller et al. (2007). Position based dynamics. Journal of Visual Communication and Image Representation, 18(2):109–118.\n9. Alexey Stomakhin et al. (2013). A material point method for snow simulation. ACM Transactions on Graphics (TOG), 32(4):1–10.\n10. Bernhard Kerbl et al. (2023). 3d gaussian splatting for real-time radiance field rendering. ACM Trans. Graph., 42(4):139–1.\n11. Ben Mildenhall et al. (2021). Nerf: Representing scenes as neural radiance fields for view synthesis. Communications of the ACM, 65(1):99–106.\n12. Jianfeng Xiang et al. (2025). Structured 3d latents for scalable and versatile 3d generation. In CVPR, pages 21469–21480.\n13. Jianfeng Xiang et al. (2025). Native and compact structured latents for 3d generation. Tech report.\n14. Tianyuan Zhang et al. (2024). Physdreamer: Physics-based interaction with 3d objects via video generation. In European Conference on Computer Vision, pages 388–406.\n15. Tianyi Xie et al. (2024). Physgaussian: Physics-integrated 3d gaussians for generative dynamics. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, pages 4389–4398.\n16. Yi-Ling Qiao et al. (2021). Efficient differentiable simulation of articulated bodies. In International Conference on Machine Learning, pages 8661–8671. PMLR.\n17. Yi-Ling Qiao et al. (2020). Scalable differentiable physics for learning and control. arXiv preprint arXiv:2007.02168.\n18. Tao Du et al. (2021). Diffpd: Differentiable projective dynamics. ACM Transactions on Graphics (ToG), 41(2):1–21.\n19. Hanxiao Jiang et al. (2025). Phystwin: Physics-informed reconstruction and simulation of deformable objects from videos. ICCV.\n20. Licheng Zhong et al. (2024). Reconstruction and simulation of elastic objects with spring-mass 3d gaussians. In European Conference on Computer Vision, pages 407–423.\n21. Zhou Wang et al. (2004). Image quality assessment: from error visibility to structural similarity. IEEE Transactions on Image Processing, 13(4):600–612.\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper presents a novel approach to dynamic scene rendering using neural radiance fields. We introduce a method that leverages the capabilities of deep learning to enhance the realism and efficiency of rendering dynamic scenes. Our approach is evaluated against existing methods, demonstrating significant improvements in both visual quality and computational performance.\n\n## Introduction\nDynamic scene rendering has become increasingly important in computer graphics and computer vision. Traditional methods often struggle with the complexities of real-time rendering, particularly in dynamic environments. Recent advancements in neural networks, particularly in the context of neural radiance fields (NeRF), have opened new avenues for improving rendering techniques. This paper aims to explore these advancements and propose a new framework that integrates deep learning with traditional rendering techniques.\n\n## Method\nOur method builds upon the foundation of neural radiance fields, extending their capabilities to handle dynamic scenes. We define the scene representation as a function \\( F: \\mathbb{R}^3 \\times \\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^4 \\), where \\( F \\) outputs color and density values for any given 3D point and viewing direction. The training process involves optimizing the parameters of \\( F \\) using a dataset of images captured from various viewpoints.\n\n### Training Procedure\nThe training procedure consists of the following steps:\n1. **Data Collection**: Capture a series of images from different angles and positions.\n2. **Network Architecture**: Design a neural network that can effectively learn the mapping defined by \\( F \\).\n3. **Loss Function**: Utilize a loss function that minimizes the difference between the rendered images and the ground truth images.\n\n## Results\nWe conducted extensive experiments to evaluate the performance of our proposed method. The results indicate that our approach significantly outperforms traditional methods in terms of both rendering quality and speed. For instance, our method achieved a peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) of 35.2 dB, compared to 30.1 dB for the baseline method.\n\n### Figure 1\n*Figure 1: Comparison of rendering quality between our method and traditional methods. The left image shows the output from the baseline method, while the right image displays the results from our proposed approach.*\n\n## Conclusion\nIn conclusion, our proposed method for dynamic scene rendering using neural radiance fields demonstrates substantial improvements over existing techniques. The integration of deep learning not only enhances the visual quality of rendered scenes but also improves computational efficiency. Future work will focus on further optimizing the network architecture and exploring additional applications of this approach in real-time rendering scenarios.\n\n## References\n1. Richard Zhang, Phillip Isola, Alexei A Efros, Eli Shechtman, and Oliver Wang. The unreasonable effectiveness of deep features as a perceptual metric. In Proceedings of the IEEE conference on computer vision and pattern recognition, pages 586–595, 2018.\n2. David G Lowe. Distinctive image features from scale-invariant keypoints. International journal of computer vision, 60(2):91–110, 2004.\n3. Raul Mur-Artal and Juan D Tardós. Orb-slam2: An open-source slam system for monocular, stereo, and rgb-d cameras. IEEE transactions on robotics, 33(5):1255–1262, 2017.\n4. Albert Pumarola, Enric Corona, Gerard Pons-Moll, and Francesc Moreno-Noguer. D-nerf: Neural radiance fields for dynamic scenes. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF conference on computer vision and pattern recognition, pages 10318–10327, 2021.\n5. Keunhong Park, Utkarsh Sinha, Jonathan T Barron, Sofien Bouaziz, Dan B Goldman, Steven M Seitz, and Ricardo Martin-Brualla. Nerfies: Deformable neural radiance fields. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF international conference on computer vision, pages 5865–5874, 2021.\n6. Guanjun Wu, Taoran Yi, Jiemin Fang, Lingxi Xie, Xiaopeng Zhang, Wei Wei, Wenyu Liu, Qi Tian, and Xinggang Wang. 4d gaussian splatting for real-time dynamic scene rendering. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), pages 20310–20320, June 2024.\n7. Youtian Lin, Zuozhuo Dai, Siyu Zhu, and Yao Yao. Gaussian-flow: 4d reconstruction with dynamic 3d gaussian particle. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, pages 21136–21145, 2024.\n8. Miloš Zefran and Francesco Bullo. Lagrangian dynamics. Robotics and Automation Handbook, pages 5–1, 2005.\n9. Marco Cusumano-Towner, Arjun Singh, Stephen Miller, James F. O’Brien, and Pieter Abbeel. Bringing clothing into desired configurations with limited perception. In 2011 IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Automation, pages 3893–3900, 2011.\n10. Chenfanfu Jiang, Craig Schroeder, Joseph Teran, Alexey Stomakhin, and Andrew Selle. The material point method for simulating continuum materials. In ACM SIGGRAPH 2016 Courses, SIGGRAPH ’16, New York, NY, USA, 2016. Association for Computing Machinery.\n11. Yuanming Hu, Yu Fang, Ziheng Ge, Ziyin Qu, Yixin Zhu, Andre Pradhana, and Chenfanfu Jiang. A moving least squares material point method with displacement discontinuity and two-way rigid body coupling. ACM Transactions on Graphics (TOG), 37(4):1–14, 2018.\n12. Miles Macklin, Matthias Müller, and Nuttapong Chentanez. Xpbd: position-based simulation of compliant constrained dynamics. In Proceedings of the 9th International Conference on Motion in Games, pages 49–54, 2016.\n13. Filipe de A. Belbute-Peres, Kevin A. Smith, Kelsey R. Allen, Joshua B. Tenenbaum, and J. Zico Kolter. End-to-end differentiable physics for learning and control. In Proceedings of the 32nd International Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems, NIPS’18, page 7178–7189, Red Hook, NY, USA, 2018. Curran Associates Inc.\n14. Jonas Degrave, Michiel Hermans, Joni Dambre, et al. A differentiable physics engine for deep learning in robotics. Frontiers in neurorobotics, 13:406386, 2019.\n15. Miles Macklin, Kenny Erleben, Matthias Müller, Nuttapong Chentanez, Stefan Jeschke, and Tae-Yong Kim. Primal/dual descent methods for dynamics. In Computer Graphics Forum, volume 39, pages 89–100. Wiley Online Library, 2020.\n16. Marc Toussaint, Kelsey R. Allen, Kevin A. Smith, and Joshua B. Tenenbaum. Differentiable physics and stable modes for tool-use and manipulation planning - extended abstract. In Proceedings of the Twenty-Eighth International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence, IJCAI-19, pages 6231–6235. International Joint Conferences on Artificial Intelligence Organization, 7 2019.\n17. Moritz Geilinger, David Hahn, Jonas Zehnder, Moritz Bächer, Bernhard Thomaszewski, and Stelian Coros. Add: Analytically differentiable dynamics for multi-body systems with frictional contact. ACM Transactions on Graphics (TOG), 39(6):1–15, 2020.\n18. David Hahn, Pol Banzet, James M Bern, and Stelian Coros. Real2sim: Visco-elastic parameter estimation from dynamic motion. ACM Transactions on Graphics (TOG), 38(6):1–13, 2019.\n19. Yuanming Hu, Jiancheng Liu, Andrew Spielberg, Joshua B Tenenbaum, William T Freeman, Jiajun Wu, Daniela Rus, and Wojciech Matusik. Chainqueen: A real-time differentiable physical simulator for soft robotics. In 2019 International conference on robotics and automation (ICRA), pages 6265–6271. IEEE, 2019.\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper presents a novel approach to modeling and simulating physical systems using neural networks. We introduce a framework that integrates physics-based principles with deep learning techniques to enhance the accuracy and efficiency of simulations. Our method leverages differentiable programming to enable gradient-based optimization, allowing for real-time control and adaptation of physical simulations.\n\n## Introduction\nThe intersection of machine learning and physics has garnered significant attention in recent years, particularly in the context of simulating complex physical phenomena. Traditional simulation methods often struggle with high-dimensional data and intricate interactions, leading to computational inefficiencies. In this work, we propose a new framework that combines the strengths of neural networks with established physics principles to address these challenges.\n\n## Method\nOur approach is built upon a differentiable programming paradigm, which allows us to formulate physical laws as differentiable functions. This enables the use of gradient descent methods for optimization. The core of our framework consists of a neural network that learns to approximate the dynamics of a physical system. We define the loss function as follows:\n\n\\[\nL = \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\left( y_i - f(x_i; \\theta) \\right)^2 + \\lambda R(\\theta)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(y_i\\) are the observed outputs, \\(f(x_i; \\theta)\\) is the neural network prediction, and \\(R(\\theta)\\) is a regularization term.\n\n## Results\nWe evaluated our framework on several benchmark problems, including cloth simulation and rigid body dynamics. The results demonstrate that our method achieves state-of-the-art performance in terms of both accuracy and computational efficiency. For instance, in the cloth simulation task, our approach reduced the simulation time by 50% compared to traditional methods while maintaining high fidelity.\n\n## Discussion\nThe integration of physics-based principles with neural networks opens new avenues for research in simulation and control. Our findings suggest that this hybrid approach can significantly enhance the performance of existing simulation techniques. Future work will focus on expanding the applicability of our framework to more complex systems and exploring its potential in real-time applications.\n\n## Conclusion\nIn conclusion, we have presented a novel framework that combines neural networks with physics-based modeling to improve the simulation of physical systems. Our results indicate that this approach not only enhances accuracy but also significantly reduces computational costs. We believe that this work lays the groundwork for future advancements in the field of physics-informed machine learning.\n\n## References\n[42] Yuanming Hu, Tzu-Mao Li, Luke Anderson, Jonathan Ragan-Kelley, and Frédo Durand. Taichi: a language for high-performance computation on spatially sparse data structures. ACM Transactions on Graphics (TOG), 38(6):201, 2019.\n\n[43] Yi-Ling Qiao, Junbang Liang, Vladlen Koltun, and Ming C. Lin. Scalable differentiable physics for learning and control. In Proceedings of the 37th International Conference on Machine Learning, ICML’20. JMLR.org, 2020.\n\n[44] Junbang Liang, Ming Lin, and Vladlen Koltun. Differentiable cloth simulation for inverse problems. Advances in neural information processing systems, 32, 2019.\n\n[45] Qizhen Ying, Guangming Wang, Yangchen Pan, Victor Adrian Prisacariu, Brian Sheil, and Yixiong Jing. NEXUS: Neural Energy Fields for Physically Consistent Contact-Rich 3D Object Dynamics. arXiv e-prints, page arXiv:2606.15015, June 2026.\n\n[46] Yutao Feng, Yintong Shang, Xuan Li, Tianjia Shao, Chenfanfu Jiang, and Yin Yang. Pie-nerf: Physics-based interactive elastodynamics with nerf. In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, pages 4450–4461, 2024.\n\n[47] Tianyi Xie, Yiwei Zhao, Ying Jiang, and Chenfanfu Jiang. Physanimator: Physics-guided generative cartoon animation. In Proceedings of the Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition Conference, pages 10793–10804, 2025.\n\n[48] Xuan Li, Yi-Ling Qiao, Peter Yichen Chen, Krishna Murthy Jatavallabhula, Ming Lin, Chenfanfu Jiang, and Chuang Gan. PAC-neRF: Physics augmented continuum neural radiance fields for geometry-agnostic system identification. In The Eleventh International Conference on Learning Representations, 2023.\n\n[49] Zhiyuan Gao, Jiageng Mao, Hong-Xing Yu, Haozhe Lou, Emily Yue ting Jia, Jernej Barbic, Jiajun Wu, and Yue Wang. Seeing the wind from a falling leaf. In The Thirty-ninth Annual Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems, 2025.\n\n[50] Yixiong Jing, Xingyuan Chen, Guangming Wang, Olaf Wysocki, Haibing Wu, and Brian Sheil. PhySPRING: Structure-Preserving Reduction of Physics-Informed Twins via GNN. arXiv e-prints, page arXiv:2605.07687, May 2026.\n\n\n\n# A Derivation of Bending Forces and Bending Damping\n\nIn this section, we provide the derivations for the bending restoring forces and the bending damping forces induced by a bending triplet \\((i, j, k)\\). Following the main paper, we adopt the compact notation\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{bend}} = -k_{\\theta}(\\theta - \\theta^0) d(i,j,k)\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{rot}} = -\\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k) \\hat{d}(i,j,k),\n\\]\n\nwhere the explicit expressions of \\(\\hat{d}_i\\) and \\(\\hat{d}_k\\) are derived below.\n\n## A.1 Notation\n\nFor a bending triplet \\((i, j, k)\\) with center node \\(j\\), define\n\n\\[\nu = x_i - x_j, \\quad v = x_k - x_j, \\quad \\ell_u = \\|u\\|, \\quad \\ell_v = \\|v\\|, \\quad \\hat{u} = \\frac{u}{\\ell_u}, \\quad \\hat{v} = \\frac{v}{\\ell_v}\n\\]\n\nThe included angle is computed from\n\n\\[\nc = \\hat{u}^T \\hat{v}, \\quad \\theta = \\arccos(c), \\quad s = \\sqrt{1 - c^2} = \\sin(\\theta).\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(\\theta^0\\) denotes the rest angle in the canonical configuration. The bending energy is given by\n\n\\[\nE_{\\text{bend}}(i, j, k) = \\frac{1}{2} k_{\\theta} (\\theta - \\theta^0)^2,\n\\]\n\nwith force convention \\(f = -\\nabla_x E_{\\text{bend}}\\).\n\n## A.2 Derivation of Bending Forces\n\nDifferentiating Eq. (13) gives\n\n\\[\n\\nabla_x E_{\\text{bend}} = k_{\\theta} (\\theta - \\theta^0) \\nabla_x \\theta = -k_{\\theta} (\\theta - \\theta^0) \\nabla_x c,\n\\]\n\nwhere we used \\(\\nabla_x \\theta = -\\frac{1}{s} \\nabla_x c\\). A standard calculation yields\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial u} = \\frac{1}{\\ell_u} \\hat{v} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial \\hat{u}}{\\partial u}, \\quad \\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial v} = \\frac{1}{\\ell_v} \\hat{u} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial \\hat{v}}{\\partial v},\n\\]\n\nand since \\(u = x_i - x_j\\) and \\(v = x_k - x_j\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial x_i} = \\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial u}, \\quad \\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial x_k} = \\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial v}, \\quad \\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial x_j} = -\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial u} - \\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial v}.\n\\]\n\nCombining the above with \\(f = -\\nabla_x E_{\\text{bend}}\\) yields the nodal forces\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{bend}}_i = k_{\\theta} (\\theta - \\theta^0) \\frac{1}{\\ell_u s} \\hat{v},\n\\]\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{bend}}_k = k_{\\theta} (\\theta - \\theta^0) \\frac{1}{\\ell_v s} \\hat{u},\n\\]\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{bend}}_j = - (f^{\\text{bend}}_i + f^{\\text{bend}}_k).\n\\]\n\nAccordingly, the directional vectors used in the main paper can be identified as\n\n\\[\nd(i,j,k) = \\frac{1}{\\ell_v s} \\hat{v}, \\quad d_i = \\frac{1}{\\ell_u s} \\hat{u}, \\quad d_k = \\frac{1}{\\ell_v s} \\hat{v}.\n\\]\n\n## A.3 Bending Damping\n\nWe model bending damping as a viscous torque proportional to the angular rate:\n\n\\[\n\\tau = -c_b \\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k).\n\\]\n\nTo compute \\(\\dot{\\theta}(i,j,k)\\), define the unit normal and tangential directions\n\n\\[\n\\hat{n} = \\frac{u \\times \\hat{v}}{\\|u \\times \\hat{v}\\|}, \\quad \\hat{t} = \\hat{n} \\times \\hat{u}.\n\\]\n\n\n\n# A PREPRINT - AUGUST 7, 2026\n\n## Abstract\n\n[Abstract content goes here.]\n\n## Introduction\n\n[Introduction content goes here.]\n\n## Method\n\nLet node velocities be \\( v_i, v_j, v_k \\). A consistent first-order approximation is given by\n\n\\[\n\\theta(i,j,k) = \\frac{u \\cdot t^\\top (v_i - v_j) \\cdot t^\\top (v_k - v_j)}{\\ell_u \\cdot \\ell_v} \\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nThe derivation of the equivalent nodal damping forces follows the same steps as Equation 15, therefore taking the same geometric directions as the elastic bending forces:\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{rot}}_i = c_b \\theta(i,j,k) d_i \\tag{24}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{rot}}_k = c_b \\theta(i,j,k) d_k \\tag{25}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nf^{\\text{rot}}_j = - (f^{\\text{rot}}_i + f^{\\text{rot}}_k) \\tag{26}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( d(i,j,k) \\) and \\( d(i,j,k) \\) are given in Eq. (20).\n\n## Results\n\nNumerical considerations indicate that when \\( \\theta \\approx 0 \\) or \\( \\theta \\approx \\pi \\), \\( s = \\sin \\theta \\) becomes small. We clip \\( c \\) to \\([-1 + \\epsilon, 1 - \\epsilon]\\) and use\n\n\\[\ns = \\sqrt{\\max(1 - c^2, \\epsilon)} \\| \\tilde{u} \\times \\tilde{v} \\|\n\\]\n\nIf \\( s \\) is below a threshold, we skip the damping term for that triplet.\n\n## Conclusion\n\n[Conclusion content goes here.]\n\n## References\n\n[References content goes here.]	[]	[]	44774	2026-08-18 02:00:55.932079+00	2026-08-18 02:00:55.932079+00
