10	2	llamaparse	# The Bitter Lesson of Tool Calling\n\nIshan Patel, Sahil Sen, Elias Lumer, and Vamse Kumar Subbiah\nCommercial Technology and Innovation Office\nPricewaterhouseCoopers, U.S.A\n\n## Abstract\n\nTool use transforms LLMs into agents that act beyond their training data, and for code-capable models, programmatic tool calling extends this further by replacing rigid JSON calls with scripts that chain and parallelize naturally. However, a systematic evaluation of tools as code on an established benchmark across current and prior model generations under real-world task conditions has not been conducted. In this work, we empirically compare programmatic tool calling (PTC) to native JSON tool calling across 14 language models on BFCL v4. In the programmatic tool calling paradigm, tools are exposed as typed Python stubs that the model invokes through code, with execution and results handled in a single agent turn. Programmatic tool calling matches or exceeds native JSON tool calling in 11 of 14 models on BFCL v4, with the GPT-5.6 family achieving a 10.6% improvement over the JSON tool calling baseline. Further, it matches or outperforms the baseline in 13 of 14 models under parallel fan-out and holds stable under context rot conditions where the baseline degrades 2.3% on average. Our results demonstrate that programmatic tool calling is a viable and robust alternative to JSON tool calling, with performance tracking model capability across release generations. We run three ablation studies targeting sequential chaining, parallel fan-out, and context rot, the task structures where the limitations of JSON tool calling have been most commonly claimed.\n\n## 1 Introduction\n\nLarge language models (LLMs) now act as tool-calling agents, invoking external services through APIs that require the model to emit a structured JSON object at each function call (Anthropic, 2024; Guo et al., 2026). For models that can already write executable code, this format is a design choice, not a necessity. Prior work has established a theoretical case for replacing JSON tool calls with executable code. CodeAct (Wang et al., 2024) showed that code actions achieve up to 20% higher task success with 30% fewer interaction turns on multi-tool tasks, with gains concentrated in parallel and compositional scenarios. A more recent study found that code-only output restrictions change pass rates by an absolute change of fewer than 3% on coding-agent tasks (Yang et al., 2026). Function calling is a harder test case. It requires precise argument serialization, multi-step chaining, and fan-out across heterogeneous APIs, constraints absent from coding-agent evaluations and exactly where paradigm choice has the largest claimed effect. Whether programmatic tool calling matches JSON tool calling under these conditions, across model families and adversarial context loads, remains untested.\n\nWe find that 11 of 14 models match or exceed baseline accuracy under programmatic tool calling on BFCL v4, with results dividing along model generation lines. All five Anthropic models and the three newest GPT generations match or exceed baseline accuracy, while three older GPT models do not. On context flooding, programmatic tool calling sees an absolute improvement of 5.5% on average, compared to an absolute 32% degradation for the filesystem-discovery comparison approach. Our contributions are:\n\n1. A systematic evaluation of programmatic tool calling against JSON tool calling across multiple model generations.\n2. Empirical evidence demonstrating the advantages of programmatic tool calling in real-world task conditions.\n3. Insights from ablation studies that highlight the strengths and weaknesses of both paradigms.\n\n## 2 Method\n\n[Details of the method would be included here, including experimental setup, data collection, and analysis techniques.]\n\n## 3 Results\n\n[Results would be presented here, including statistical analyses, comparisons, and any relevant figures or tables.]\n\n## 4 Discussion\n\n[Discussion of the implications of the results, limitations of the study, and suggestions for future research would be included here.]\n\n## 5 Conclusion\n\n[Concluding remarks summarizing the findings and their significance would be included here.]\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the efficacy of programmatic tool calling compared to traditional JSON tool calling in large language models (LLMs). We demonstrate that programmatic tool calling not only matches but exceeds the accuracy of JSON tool calling, particularly in complex sequential tasks. Our findings reveal significant advantages in execution efficiency and robustness under adversarial conditions, suggesting a paradigm shift in how LLMs can effectively utilize tools.\n\n# Introduction\nThe advent of large language models (LLMs) has revolutionized the way we approach problem-solving in artificial intelligence. Recent advancements have highlighted the potential of programmatic tool calling as a superior alternative to JSON tool calling. This paper aims to explore the viability of programmatic tool calling, particularly in the context of its accuracy and efficiency in executing tasks across various model families.\n\n# Method\nWe study whether programmatic tool calling can replace JSON tool calling for LLM tool use without sacrificing accuracy. We evaluate two paradigms on BFCL v4: JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling. In each paradigm, the model receives the same task description and must invoke the correct functions with the correct arguments. Only the mechanism for expressing and executing tool calls differs. In the context rot ablation, we additionally include a filesystem-based condition as an internal reference point.\n\n# Results\nOur results indicate that programmatic tool calling maintains 100% enumeration accuracy at N = 100, while JSON tool calling drops tool calls entirely above a model-specific fan-out threshold (N = 70–72 for Claude Sonnet 5). Furthermore, programmatic tool calling shows a significant accuracy advantage on sequential tasks, with an 18.8% absolute gap over JSON tool calling at lengths ≥ 12. Under context flooding, programmatic tool calling remains stable, while JSON tool calling degrades by 2.3% on average.\n\n# Related Work\nTwo lines of work motivate replacing JSON tool calls with executable code. The first is code-action agents. CodeAct (Wang et al., 2024) demonstrated that code actions outperform JSON alternatives on multi-tool tasks, particularly in parallel and compositional scenarios. Recursive Agent Harnesses (Lumer et al., 2026) extend this finding, showing that executing code in a subprocess bypasses the per-turn tool-call cap that constrains native JSON function calling at high fan-out.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe findings of this study suggest that programmatic tool calling offers a robust and efficient alternative to JSON tool calling in LLMs. The advantages observed in accuracy and stability under various conditions indicate that adopting programmatic tool calling could significantly enhance the performance of agentic systems. Future work should focus on further refining these methods and exploring their implications in real-world applications.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper evaluates two paradigms for tool calling in language models: JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling. We analyze their performance based on a benchmark function catalog, focusing on the execution mechanisms and the implications for model accuracy and efficiency. The results indicate that while both paradigms achieve similar accuracy, the programmatic approach offers advantages in terms of flexibility and execution control.\n\n# Introduction\nThe integration of external tools into language models has become increasingly important for enhancing their capabilities. This research investigates two primary paradigms for tool calling: JSON tool calling, which utilizes structured JSON objects, and programmatic tool calling, which involves writing and executing Python scripts. We aim to understand the differences in execution mechanisms and their impact on model performance.\n\n# Method\n## Task Definition\nEach benchmark entry specifies a natural-language user query \\( q \\), a set of available functions \\( F = \\{f_1, \\ldots, f_k\\} \\) each with a typed signature, and a ground-truth set of function calls \\( C^* = \\{(f_i, a_i)\\} \\) where \\( a_i \\) is the argument mapping for call \\( i \\). A model is deemed correct on an entry if its output produces a set of calls that matches \\( C^* \\) under the benchmark’s normalized string comparison, which strips punctuation and lowercases all values before matching. This definition is consistent across all paradigms, with differences arising in prompting and output parsing.\n\n## Paradigms\n### JSON Tool Calling\nIn this paradigm, the model receives a system prompt containing function schemas formatted as JSON tool definitions and an API call that invokes the tool-calling endpoint. The model outputs a structured JSON object at each function call, which is the standard deployment pattern for tool-augmented LLMs.\n\n### Programmatic Tool Calling\nThe model receives a system prompt that embeds the source of a typed Python module whose functions correspond one-to-one with the benchmark’s function schemas. Each stub function captures its arguments and returns them as a structured dict. The model writes a Python script that imports this module and calls the appropriate functions. The agent loop executes the script in a native shell subprocess, capturing stdout, from which the scorer extracts function names and argument values.\n\n# Results\nThe evaluation of both paradigms reveals that they consume the same number of LLM calls per entry, making accuracy comparisons straightforward. For instance, consider a task that asks the model to retrieve the circumference of a circle with radius 7 and the area of a square with side 5. In JSON tool calling, the model emits two sequential JSON tool-call objects. In programmatic tool calling, the model writes a Python script that executes the necessary calculations.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study highlights the strengths and weaknesses of JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling. While both paradigms achieve similar accuracy, the programmatic approach provides greater flexibility and control over execution, which may be beneficial in more complex scenarios. Future work will explore the scalability of these paradigms in larger and more diverse benchmarks.\n\n# Figure\nFigure 1: Overview of the two primary paradigms evaluated. In JSON tool calling, the model emits JSON tool-call objects via the API. In programmatic tool calling, it writes a Python script using typed stubs; the agent loop executes it in a subprocess. A filesystem-discovery condition is included as a secondary reference point.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the performance of different function calling paradigms in programming language models, specifically focusing on JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling. We analyze the accuracy of these paradigms in various task structures, including chaining and parallelism, using a benchmark dataset (BFCL v4) that encompasses a wide range of function call scenarios.\n\n# Introduction\nRecent advancements in programming language models have led to the exploration of various paradigms for function calling. This study aims to evaluate the effectiveness of JSON tool calling versus programmatic tool calling in terms of accuracy and efficiency. We utilize the BFCL v4 benchmark, which includes a diverse set of tasks, to assess the performance of these paradigms under different conditions.\n\n# Method\n## Benchmark and Evaluation\nBFCL v4 (Vaghasiya et al., 2026) provides 309 representative entries drawn from eight task categories: simple_python, multiple, parallel, parallel_multiple, live_simple, live_multiple, live_parallel, and live_parallel_multiple. Entries were sampled proportionally to mirror the category distribution of the full BFCL v4 benchmark, with per-category minimums applied to ensure sufficient entries in smaller categories. The complete entry ID list is released with the evaluation harness.\n\n### Chaining\nSequential multi-hop function calls, where the output of \\( f_1 \\) must be computed and passed as an argument to \\( f_2 \\). In JSON tool calling, this requires two model turns. The model calls \\( f_1 \\), receives the return value, then calls \\( f_2 \\). In programmatic tool calling, both calls appear in a single script. The model computes the intermediate value using parametric knowledge and passes it directly. The chaining ablation contains 52 entries with chain lengths of 2 to 20 calls.\n\n### Parallelism\nIndependent fan-out function calls, where \\( N \\) calls must be issued in a single step. JSON tool calling issues them as parallel tool-call objects. Programmatic tool calling writes an `asyncio.gather` block or a sequential loop. The parallelism ablation uses 32 enumeration-type entries with fan-out counts ranging from 7 to 48, plus targeted probe entries at \\( N \\in \\{60, 70, 72, 75, 100\\} \\) for Claude Sonnet 5 to locate the fan-out threshold at which JSON tool calling begins dropping calls.\n\n## Ablation Design\nThe three ablation studies target task structures where paradigm choice has been claimed to matter. Each ablation subset was constructed by selecting entries from BFCL v4 that represent the target task structure without duplicating main-evaluation entries. The complete entry ID lists are released with the evaluation harness. The chaining subset (n = 52) was optimized to cover the full range of chain lengths (\\( n_{\\text{chain}} = 2–20 \\)), with the distribution weighted toward longer chains where JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling diverge.\n\n### Context Rot\nThe flood condition injects decoy function schemas into the context alongside the entry’s relevant schemas, increasing total context size to 128 schemas. The two conditions are filtered (only the functions the query uses) and flood (128 total schemas, including the relevant functions plus corpus decoys drawn from unrelated domains).\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate that programmatic tool calling outperforms JSON tool calling in terms of accuracy across various task structures. The chaining and parallelism tasks demonstrated significant differences in performance, particularly in scenarios with higher complexity and longer chain lengths. The evaluation metrics reveal that programmatic tool calling maintains a higher accuracy rate, especially in enumeration-type entries.\n\nIn summary, our findings suggest that the choice of function calling paradigm significantly impacts the performance of programming language models, with programmatic tool calling providing a more efficient and accurate approach in complex scenarios. Further research is needed to explore the implications of these findings in real-world applications and to refine the benchmarks used for evaluation.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper evaluates the performance of 14 models from two families over a span of 20 months, focusing on their accuracy in programmatic tool calling and JSON tool calling. The results indicate significant variances in performance across models, with specific attention to the impact of model generation and training data on accuracy.\n\n# Introduction\nRecent advancements in AI models have led to a variety of architectures and training methodologies. This study aims to assess the performance of these models in specific tasks, particularly in the context of tool calling. We analyze the accuracy of different models and identify patterns that may inform future developments in AI.\n\n# Method\nWe evaluated 14 models, as listed in Table 1, which spans releases from November 2024 to July 2026. All models were run at a temperature of 0 to ensure consistency in output. The evaluation focused on two types of tool calling: JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling. The accuracy was measured as the fraction of entries where every required function call was both present and correctly parameterized.\n\n# Results\n## 3.5 Models\nThe models evaluated are summarized in Table 1. All tables report 95% Wilson confidence intervals per row. Due to the small size of ablation entries (n = 31–52), the intervals are wide, and individual model results should be interpreted as directional rather than conclusive. Notably, programmatic tool calling underperformed JSON tool calling by an average of 14.1% in the parallel and parallel_multiple categories, while showing a 10.0% improvement in live_multiple.\n\n## 4 Experiments\nWe conducted experiments on BFCL v4 and three ablation studies (chaining, parallelism, and context rot). All experiments were run at temperature 0. The results indicate that all five Anthropic models (Claude Haiku 4.5 through Sonnet 5) matched or exceeded baseline accuracy under programmatic tool calling, with deltas ranging from 0.0 to 6.5 percentage points.\n\n### 4.1 BFCL v4 Main Evaluation\nTable 2 presents the accuracy for JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling across all 14 models on BFCL v4. The GPT-5.6 family achieved the largest gains in programmatic tool calling, with GPT-5.6-Sol and GPT-5.6-Terra each showing an absolute improvement of 10.6% over their JSON tool calling baseline. In contrast, older models such as GPT-4o, GPT-4.1, and GPT-5.4-mini fell below baseline by 19.7% to 26.9%. The failure mode for these models was consistent, producing code with literal \\n escape sequences in multiline scripts, leading to syntax errors.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe evaluation of the 14 models reveals significant differences in performance, particularly in the context of tool calling. The findings suggest that advancements in model architecture and training data can lead to substantial improvements in accuracy. Future work should focus on addressing the identified failure modes and exploring the implications of these results for the development of more robust AI systems.\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the performance of various models in the context of chaining and parallelism ablation tasks. We analyze the accuracy of different models when utilizing JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling, focusing on enumeration and aggregation accuracy.\n\n# Introduction\nRecent advancements in AI models have led to significant improvements in their ability to perform complex tasks. This study aims to evaluate the performance of several models, specifically in the context of chaining and parallelism tasks, using two different calling methods: JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted experiments with a range of models, measuring their accuracy in chaining and parallelism ablation tasks. The models were evaluated based on their ability to issue calls and produce correct aggregate answers. The sample sizes for the chaining and parallelism tasks were 52 and 32, respectively.\n\n# Results\n## 4.2 Chaining Ablation\nIn the chaining ablation (n = 52 entries, chain lengths 2–20), programmatic tool calling handles sequential multi-hop calls differently from JSON tool calling. In JSON tool calling, the model issues \\( f_1 \\), receives its return value, then issues \\( f_2 \\) in a second inference turn. In programmatic tool calling, the model writes both calls in a single script and computes the intermediate value from parametric knowledge before passing it to \\( f_2 \\).\n\nTable 3 shows per-model results. Claude Sonnet 5 achieves the largest programmatic tool calling gain (80.8% → 96.2%), followed by Claude Opus 4.8 (80.8% → 94.2%), while six models maintain near-parity (within 5% absolute). GPT-4.1 is the sole outlier, with its baseline accuracy of 98.1% collapsing to 40.4% under programmatic tool calling, driven by the \\n encoding failure described above.\n\n## 4.3 Parallelism Ablation\nProgrammatic tool calling matches or exceeds baseline accuracy for 13 of 14 models on the parallelism ablation (n = 32 entries, fan-out counts of 7 to 48). Table 4 separates the results for clarity. Token costs cross over at \\( N \\approx 26 \\). Below this threshold, programmatic tool calling is more expensive due to its fixed system-prompt overhead.\n\n# Conclusion\nThe results indicate that programmatic tool calling generally provides superior performance in both chaining and parallelism tasks compared to JSON tool calling. However, certain models, particularly GPT-4.1, exhibit significant performance degradation due to encoding issues. Future work should focus on addressing these limitations to enhance model reliability in complex task execution.\n\n# Tables\n**Table 3:** Chaining ablation accuracy (%, n = 52, chain lengths 2–20). JSON=JSON tool calling; PTC = programmatic tool calling. ± columns show 95% Wilson CI half-widths. Bold indicates the higher value per row (ties bolded on both).\n\n**Table 4:** Parallelism ablation accuracy (%, n = 32). JSON=JSON tool calling; PTC = programmatic tool calling. Enumeration and overall accuracy are identical for all models here; aggregation accuracy is discussed separately in the text. ± columns show 95% Wilson CI half-widths. Bold indicates the higher value per row (ties bolded on both).\n\n\n\n# Abstract\nThis paper investigates the performance of various language models under different conditions of context load, specifically focusing on programmatic tool calling versus JSON tool calling. We analyze the accuracy of these models in generating code and handling complex tasks, particularly under flooding conditions where the context is overloaded with irrelevant information.\n\n# Introduction\nRecent advancements in language models have led to significant improvements in their ability to generate code and perform complex tasks. However, the impact of context load on their performance remains an area of active research. This study aims to explore how different models handle varying amounts of relevant and irrelevant context, particularly in the context of tool calling.\n\n# Method\nWe conducted a series of experiments using multiple language models, including Anthropic and OpenAI variants. Each model was evaluated under two conditions: filtered (only relevant function schemas) and flood (128 total schemas, including irrelevant corpus decoys). The accuracy of each model was measured in terms of its ability to generate correct code for programmatic tool calling and JSON tool calling.\n\n# Results\nThe results indicate that programmatic tool calling accuracy relative to JSON tool calling tracks model generation rather than model family. All five Anthropic models matched or exceeded baseline performance across all three ablation studies. Notably, the three newest GPT-5.6 variants also performed well. However, models that fell below baseline under programmatic tool calling (GPT-4o, GPT-4.1, and GPT-5.4-mini) exhibited a specific failure in generating Python code, producing incorrect newline sequences that led to syntax errors.\n\n## 5 Analysis\n### 5.1 Model Generation Predicts Programmatic Tool Calling Viability\nThe clearest pattern in the results is that programmatic tool calling accuracy relative to JSON tool calling tracks model generation, not model family. The models that fell below baseline under programmatic tool calling produced Python code with `\\n` character sequences instead of real newlines, causing syntax errors in multiline scripts.\n\n### 5.2 Programmatic Tool Calling Handles Fan-Out Structurally; JSON Tool Calling Does Not\nProgrammatic tool calling achieved near-ceiling accuracy for GPT-5 on the parallelism ablation, improving from 71.9% to 96.9%. In contrast, JSON tool calling accuracy degraded above fan-out counts of 13. This is because JSON tool calling requires the model to emit a parallel tool-call block in a single response, leading to omissions at high fan-out. In contrast, programmatic tool calling allows for loops or sequences of function calls in Python, which imposes no structural limit on count and benefits from the model’s code-generation training.\n\n### 4.4 Context Rot Ablation\nThe context rot ablation compared each paradigm under two context loads: filtered and flood. Table 5 reports accuracy under both conditions. JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling were both stable under flooding, with a mean accuracy change of −2.3% for JSON tool calling and +5.5% for programmatic tool calling. The improvement in programmatic tool calling under flood conditions was driven by models that struggled with the strict type constraints of the filtered condition.\n\nFigure 2 illustrates both trends side by side, highlighting the differences in performance between the two tool calling paradigms under varying context loads.\n\n# Conclusion\nThis study demonstrates that programmatic tool calling is more resilient to context overload compared to JSON tool calling, particularly in the presence of irrelevant information. The findings suggest that model generation capabilities play a crucial role in determining the effectiveness of tool calling strategies, with implications for future research and development in language model applications.\n\n\n\n# Anthropic models: JSON tool calling vs. inline execution\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper investigates the performance differences between JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling (PTC) across various language models, specifically focusing on the accuracy and latency of these methods. We present a systematic evaluation of 14 language models on the BFCL v4 benchmark, revealing that PTC often matches or exceeds the performance of JSON tool calling, particularly in terms of enumeration accuracy and latency on chaining tasks.\n\n## Introduction\nAs language model agents increasingly rely on tools to extend their capabilities beyond training data, the choice of tool interface—structured JSON calls versus programmatic tool calling—has significant implications for performance. This study aims to quantify the differences in accuracy and latency between these two methods across multiple model generations.\n\n## Method\nWe conducted experiments using 14 language models, evaluating their performance on the BFCL v4 benchmark. The models were assessed based on their enumeration accuracy and latency when using JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling. The enumeration accuracy was measured to determine whether the models invoked the tools correctly, while latency was measured in wall-clock time for chaining tasks.\n\n## Results\n### Accuracy\nFigure 2 illustrates the accuracy (%) on the BFCL v4 subset for JSON tool calling and programmatic tool calling (PTC) by model generation. The results indicate that OpenAI models required several generations to close the accuracy gap, while Anthropic models demonstrated consistent PTC parity from the outset.\n\n- **OpenAI Models**: The performance of OpenAI models, such as GPT-4o and GPT-5-nano, shows a gradual improvement in accuracy, with PTC starting 26.9% below JSON tool calling for GPT-4o and crossing the baseline at GPT-5-nano.\n- **Anthropic Models**: In contrast, Anthropic models consistently matched or exceeded JSON tool calling across all five models, with no generation-level gap.\n\n### Latency\nProgrammatic tool calling was found to complete chaining entries in approximately half the wall-clock time of the baseline for 13 of 14 models. The per-entry latency ratios ranged from 0.32 to 0.96 of the baseline. Notably, the GPT-5.6 family achieved a 10.7% improvement over the JSON tool calling baseline.\n\n### Enumeration Accuracy\nThe enumeration vs. aggregation split revealed a programmatic tool calling behavior that diverged from the intended design. Several models produced the correct aggregation answer by leveraging parametric world knowledge rather than executing the enumeration calls. We report enumeration accuracy as the primary metric because it measures whether the model actually invoked the tools, not just whether the final answer was correct.\n\nTo quantify the fan-out count at which this structural advantage emerges for Anthropic models, we probed Claude Sonnet 5 baseline at \\( N \\in \\{60, 70, 72, 75, 100\\} \\). Enumeration accuracy was 100% at \\( N \\leq 70 \\), dropped to 75% at \\( N = 72 \\), and reached 0% at \\( N = 100 \\), indicating that the degradation onset occurs between \\( N = 70 \\) and \\( N = 72 \\). In contrast, programmatic tool calling maintained 100% enumeration accuracy at both \\( N = 72 \\) and \\( N = 100 \\).\n\n## Conclusion\nThe choice of tool interface has practical consequences for chaining, parallelism, and robustness under real-world conditions. Our findings suggest that programmatic tool calling is a viable and reliable alternative to JSON tool calling, with significant improvements in both enumeration accuracy and latency for many models. The remaining gap in performance indicates that further research is needed to explore the systematic advantages of programmatic tool calling across different model generations and real-world task conditions.\n\n\n\n# Limitations\n\nFour limitations bound the claims in this paper. First, BFCL v4 uses echo-return stubs. Each function returns its arguments verbatim rather than executing real API calls. This means we measure argument serialization accuracy, not end-to-end tool-use correctness. Results may not transfer to settings where return values affect downstream calls.\n\nSecond, the ablation entry counts are small (n = 31–52 per condition), so individual model results carry wide confidence intervals and should be read as directional. Only the aggregate cross-model patterns (11 of 14 on BFCL v4, consistent programmatic tool calling improvement under flood) are large enough to interpret reliably.\n\nThird, a recent audit found 20% evaluator-human misalignment in BFCL v4’s LLM-judge evaluation mode (Vaghasiya et al., 2026). Our deterministic scorer avoids this path, but the benchmark’s ground-truth labels may still contain noise that our scorer inherits.\n\nFourth, programmatic tool calling carries a fixed input-token overhead relative to JSON tool calling: the system prompt embeds the full instruction template as prose, whereas JSON tool calling passes function schemas through the API tools parameter, which some providers count separately from conversation input tokens. On the chaining ablation, programmatic tool calling uses 1.5× the input tokens of JSON tool calling. This overhead reverses at high fan-out, as discussed in Section 4.3. Output token counts do not differ across paradigms.\n\n# References\n\nTamer Alkhouli, Katerina Margatina, James Gung, Raphael Shu, Claudia Zaghi, Monica Sunkara, and Yi Zhang. 2025. CONFETTI: Conversational function-calling evaluation through turn-level interactions. In Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics, ACL 2025, pages 7993–8006.\n\nAnthropic. 2024. Building effective agents. https://www.anthropic.com/research/building-effective-agents.\n\nKinjal Basu, Ibrahim Abdelaziz, Subhajit Chaudhury, Soham Dan, Maxwell Crouse, Asim Munawar, Sadhana Kumaravel, Vinod Muthusamy, Pavan Kapanipathi, and Luis A. Lastras. 2024. API-BLEND: A comprehensive corpora for training and benchmarking API LLMs. In Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics, ACL 2024, pages 9510–9529.\n\nZehui Chen, Weihua Du, Wenwei Zhang, Kuikun Liu, Jiangning Liu, Miao Zheng, Jingming Zhuo, Songyang Zhang, Dahua Lin, Kai Chen, and Feng Zhao. 2024. T-Eval: Evaluating the tool utilization capability of large language models step by step. In Proceedings of the 62nd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics, ACL 2024, pages 9510–9529.\n\nCloudflare. 2026. Code mode. https://developers.cloudflare.com/agents/tools/codemode/.\n\nCloudflare Agents documentation. Last updated June 24, 2026.\n\nYaxin Du, Yifan Zhou, Yujie Ge, Jiajun Wang, Xianghe Pang, Shuo Tang, Tuney Zheng, Bryan Dai, Jian Yang, and Siheng Chen. 2026. HyperTool: Beyond step-wise tool calls for tool-augmented agents. arXiv preprint arXiv:2606.13663.\n\nDongxin Guo, Jikun Wu, and Siu Ming Yiu. 2026. The deterministic horizon: When extended reasoning fails and tool delegation becomes necessary. In Proceedings of the 43rd International Conference on Machine Learning, ICML 2026.\n\nShijue Huang, Wanjun Zhong, Jianqiao Lu, Qi Zhu, Jiahui Gao, Weiwen Liu, Yutai Hou, Xingshan Zeng, Yasheng Wang, Lifeng Shang, Xin Jiang, Ruifeng Xu, and Qun Liu. 2024. Planning, creation, usage: Benchmarking LLMs for comprehensive tool utilization in real-world complex scenarios. In Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: ACL 2024, pages 4363–4400.\n\nHugging Face. 2024. smolagents: Building agents with code-first design. https://huggingface.co/blog/smolagents.\n\nSeth Karten, Alex L. Zhang, Kevin Thomas, Sebastian Müller, and Prime Intellect Team. 2026. Prime Agent: A self-improving RLM agent. https://www.primeintellect.ai/blog/prime-agent. Accessed: 2026-08-06.\n\nMinghao Li, Yingxiu Zhao, Bowen Yu, Feifan Song, Hangyu Li, Haiyang Yu, Zhoujun Li, Fei Huang, and Yongbin Li. 2023. API-Bank: A comprehensive benchmark for tool-augmented LLMs. In Proceedings of the 2023 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, EMNLP 2023, pages 3102–3116.\n\nTong Liu, Cheng Qian, Matej Cief, Yuan He, Daniele Dan, Nikolaos Aletras, and Gabriella Kazai. 2026. On effectiveness and efficiency of agentic tool-calling and RL training. In Proceedings of the 43rd International Conference on Machine Learning, ICML 2026.\n\nElias Lumer, Sahil Sen, Kevin Paul, and Vamse Kumar Subbiah. 2026. Recursive agent harnesses. arXiv preprint arXiv:2606.13643.\n\n\n\n# A Per-Category Accuracy on BFCL v4\n\nXuying Ning, Katherine Tieu, Dongqi Fu, Tianxin Wei, Zihao Li, Yuanchen Bei, Jiaru Zou, Mengting Ai, Zhining Liu, Ting-Wei Li, Lingjie Chen, Yanjun Zhao, Ke Yang, Bingxuan Li, Cheng Qian, Gaotang Li, Xiao Lin, Zhichen Zeng, Ruizhong Qiu, and 23 others. 2026. Code as agent harness. arXiv preprint arXiv:2605.18747.\n\n## Abstract\n\nThis paper presents a comprehensive analysis of the per-category accuracy of various models on the BFCL v4 benchmark. We evaluate the performance of 14 models across different categories, providing insights into their strengths and weaknesses.\n\n## Introduction\n\nThe BFCL v4 benchmark serves as a critical tool for assessing the capabilities of coding agents in various contexts. This study aims to quantify the accuracy of these agents across different categories, thereby identifying areas for improvement and further research.\n\n## Method\n\nWe conducted a series of evaluations on 14 models, measuring their performance in different categories. The categories included JSON tool calling, programmatic tool calling, and various live execution scenarios. The accuracy metrics were calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Accuracy} = \\frac{\\text{Number of Correct Predictions}}{\\text{Total Predictions}} \\times 100\n\\]\n\n## Results\n\nThe results of our evaluations are summarized in Table 6, which presents the mean accuracy per BFCL v4 category, averaged across all models. The table includes the following categories:\n\n- **JSON**: 40 samples, accuracy 86.1% ± 10.7\n- **Multiple**: 20 samples, accuracy 95.4% ± 11.1\n- **Parallel**: 40 samples, accuracy 85.5% ± 10.9\n- **Par. Multiple**: 40 samples, accuracy 85.5% ± 10.9\n- **Live Simple**: 25 samples, accuracy 76.6% ± 15.9\n- **Live Multiple**: 96 samples, accuracy 73.1% ± 8.7\n- **Live Parallel**: 24 samples, accuracy 66.1% ± 17.7\n- **Live Par. Mult.**: 24 samples, accuracy 66.1% ± 17.7\n\nOverall, the mean accuracy across all categories was 78.6% ± 4.6, indicating a robust performance by the models evaluated. The gaps observed in the parallel and par. multiple categories were largely driven by the three OpenAI models, which exhibited encoding failures.\n\n## Discussion\n\nThe findings highlight the varying performance of coding agents across different categories. The high accuracy in the JSON and multiple categories suggests that these models are well-equipped to handle structured data and multiple tool calls. However, the lower accuracy in live execution scenarios indicates a need for further refinement in real-time processing capabilities.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThis study provides valuable insights into the performance of coding agents on the BFCL v4 benchmark. The results underscore the importance of category-specific evaluations in understanding the strengths and limitations of these models.\n\n## Appendix B: Programmatic Tool Calling Walkthroughs\n\nThis appendix shows concrete execution traces to illustrate how the programmatic tool calling agent loop operates in each evaluation setting. Each trace is a verbatim excerpt from a recorded trajectory, lightly condensed (absolute workspace paths are shortened to `<workspace>/`).\n\n### B.1 BFCL Single-Call (simple_python)\n\n**Task (simple_python_0)**: “Find the area of a triangle with a base of 10 units and height of 5 units.” The stub module provided to the model contains:\n\n```python\ndef calculate_triangle_area(base: int, height: int, unit: str = None) -> dict:\n"""Calculate the area of a triangle."""\nreturn _rpc.call('calculate_triangle_area', base=base, height=height, unit=unit)\n```\n\nThe agent emits a single tool call:\n\n```python\nexecute_python(code="""\nfrom stubs import calculate_triangle_area\nresult = calculate_triangle_area(base=10, height=5)\nprint(__import__('json').dumps(result))\n""")\n```\n\nThe subprocess prints:\n\n```\n{"calculate_triangle_area": {"base": 10, "height": 5}}\n[exit code 0]\n```\n\nThe stop middleware intercepts the next model call and terminates the loop. The scorer extracts function name and argument mapping from stdout, compares against ground truth, and records a single LLM turn for the entry.</workspace>\n\n\n\n# B.2 Chaining Ablation\n\n**Task (chaining_0):** “Compute the circumference of a circle with radius 7, then use that circumference as the side length of a square to find the square’s perimeter.”\n\nThe two relevant stubs are `geometry_circumference(radius)` and `geometry_square_perimeter(side)`.\n\n**Programmatic tool calling.** The agent writes a single script that computes the intermediate value in Python before passing it to the second stub:\n\n```python\nimport json, math\nfrom stubs import (\ngeometry_circumference,\ngeometry_square_perimeter)\n\nf1 = geometry_circumference(radius=7)\ncirc = 2 * math.pi * 7 # 43.982...\nf2 = geometry_square_perimeter(side=circ)\nprint(json.dumps(f1))\nprint(json.dumps(f2))\n```\n\n**Subprocess output:**\n```json\n{"geometry.circumference": {"radius": 7}}\n{"geometry.square_perimeter": {"side": 43.98}}\n```\n[exit code 0]\n\nBoth calls are resolved in a single subprocess, completing the entry in a single LLM turn.\n\n# B.3 Parallelism Ablation\n\n**Task fanout_population_7_a:** “Of 7 countries — Zorbania, Keltross, Miventa, Drulan, Quelfor, Hartox, and Bivessa — which 3 have the largest population?”\n\nThe available stub is `country_info_population(country: str) -> dict`.\n\n**Programmatic tool calling.** The agent issues one `execute_python` call that fans out all seven lookups concurrently and ranks the results in the same script:\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json\nfrom stubs import country_info_population\n\npop = country_info_population # alias\nasync def main():\ncc = ["Zorbania","Keltross","Miventa",\n"Drulan","Quelfor","Hartox","Bivessa"]\nres = await asyncio.gather(*[\nasyncio.to_thread(pop, country=c)\nfor c in cc\n])\npairs = sorted(\nzip(cc, [r["population"] for r in res]),\nkey=lambda x: x[1], reverse=True)\ntop3 = [{"country": c, "population": p}\nfor c, p in pairs[:3]]\n```\n\nThe prompt specifies that real newlines (not \\n escape sequences) must be used in multiline scripts. The stop middleware intercepts the first model call after the execute tool returns and terminates the agent loop without issuing a second inference.\n\n**Stub module design.** Each stub function is generated from the benchmark’s function schema. It accepts the schema’s typed keyword arguments, immediately returns them as a structured dict, and prints the result to stdout. No external service is contacted. The scorer parses stdout to recover function name and argument mapping, then normalizes and compares against ground truth.	[]	[]	31684	2026-08-18 02:01:27.927209+00	2026-08-18 02:01:27.927209+00
